#:pack{:created-at #inst "2026-07-09T02:37:05.295-00:00",
       :version "2026.07",
       :rules
       [#:rule{:title
               "API surface classes — internal (EDN/Transit), first-party cross-language (JSON/SSE/GraphQL), customer extension (REST/webhooks)",
               :agent-behavior
               "Every API in a miniforge product is one of three classes. The class\ndetermines the wire format, the contract stability expectations, and\nthe authentication / versioning posture. Mixing classes — or evolving\nan internal contract as if it were a public one — creates lock-in\nand customer-visible churn.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: API surface classes — internal (EDN/Transit), first-party cross-language (JSON/SSE/GraphQL), customer extension (REST/webhooks)"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["**/*.clj"
                 "**/*.cljc"
                 "**/*.cljs"
                 "**/*.rs"
                 "**/*.py"
                 "**/*.ts"
                 "**/*.js"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# API surface classes\n\nEvery API in a miniforge product is one of three classes. The class\ndetermines the wire format, the contract stability expectations, and\nthe authentication / versioning posture. Mixing classes — or evolving\nan internal contract as if it were a public one — creates lock-in\nand customer-visible churn.\n\nThe architectural rule: **canonical domain model and external wire\nformats are not the same thing.** EDN is the semantic centre. Adapters\nproject EDN out to JSON, GraphQL, webhooks, exports.\n\n## The three classes\n\n| Class | Audience | Wire | Stability | Authentication |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **1. Internal domain boundary** | same product, same team, same language | EDN, Transit, EQL | refactor freely | trusted-process |\n| **2. First-party cross-language boundary** | own clients in other languages (native apps, Rust services, browser non-Fulcro consumers) | JSON-REST, SSE / WebSocket, possibly GraphQL | versioned, deprecation policy | session / OAuth / API key |\n| **3. Customer extension boundary** | third-party integrators, customer scripts, partner systems | REST/JSON, webhooks, signed callbacks, import / export formats, optional GraphQL | long-lived compatibility | API key / OAuth / signed requests |\n\n## API-001 — Choose the class explicitly\n\nEvery new API surface MUST declare its class in the namespace docstring\n(Clojure) or module-level comment (Rust/Python/etc.) and in the PR\ndescription. Implicit class assignment hides design decisions.\n\n```clojure\n(ns example.fleet-server.recommendations\n  \"API surface class 2 (first-party cross-language).\n   Wire: flat JSON over HTTP. Stable; versioned in the URL path.\n   Consumers: fleet-dashboard, native operator apps, customer SREs.\")\n```\n\n## API-002 — EDN is the canonical internal shape\n\nClass-1 surfaces use EDN (or Transit for over-the-wire CLJ↔CLJ) as\ntheir data shape. Domain events, internal commands and results, rule\nsources, fixtures, tests, and resolver inputs / outputs SHOULD be EDN.\n\nThis earns its keep when:\n\n- Clojure components compose without a JSON boundary in the middle\n- rules / policies compile to EDN data the runtime evaluates directly\n- fixtures and snapshots are diffable and human-readable\n- Fulcro / Pathom can consume the model without translation\n\n## API-003 — JSON for cross-language clients\n\nClass-2 surfaces MUST be JSON-flat, REST-shaped (or SSE / WebSocket for\nstreams). They MUST NOT require knowledge of EDN, EQL, Transit, or\nFulcro to consume.\n\n```\nGOOD:  GET /recommendations → {\"recommendations\": [{\"kind\":\"pause\", ...}]}\nBAD:   GET /recommendations → \"[#:recommendation{:kind :pause ...}]\"\n```\n\nThe internal model may be richer (namespaced keys, EDN sets) but the\nprojection at the class-2 boundary MUST translate to plain JSON\n(stringified keys, arrays for sets, ISO-8601 strings for instants).\n\nGraphQL is allowed at class 2 only when the client genuinely needs\nselective deep graph traversal that REST + a few endpoints can't\nexpress simply. Default to REST + SSE.\n\n## API-004 — Customer surfaces are intentionally narrow\n\nClass-3 surfaces MUST be:\n\n- documented separately from internal surfaces\n- versioned with a long-lived compatibility window (announced\n  deprecation periods, semver-style breaking-change policy)\n- minimal in surface area — every endpoint is a permanent commitment\n- delivered with reference SDKs or example code where possible\n\nWebhooks MUST be signed (HMAC or equivalent). Import / export formats\nMUST be text-based and stable (JSON, CSV, EDN) rather than internal\nbinary formats.\n\nClass-3 endpoints MUST NOT leak class-1 shapes (no Transit-encoded\nEDN, no `:namespaced/keywords` over the wire as keys).\n\n## API-005 — Do not promote without intent\n\nPromoting an internal (class-1) surface to a customer (class-3)\nsurface MUST be an explicit decision in an ADR. It is one-way: once a\ncustomer depends on a shape, you cannot quietly change it.\n\nA common failure mode: a private REST endpoint added \"just for the\ninternal dashboard\" gets discovered by a customer's script, becomes\nload-bearing, and traps the team into supporting an unintended\ncontract. Mitigate by:\n\n- documenting class-1 endpoints as internal in their docstring\n- requiring authenticated access for everything (no anonymous endpoints\n  except explicitly public ones like `/health`)\n- rate-limiting or path-prefixing internal endpoints (e.g. `/_internal/`)\n  so accidental adoption is visible and cheap to break\n\n## API-006 — Boundary patterns to recognise\n\nPatterns that recur across miniforge products. Use these to classify\nsurfaces in your own repo:\n\n- **OSS event envelope on the wire** (`{\"type\":\"event\",\"event_type\":...}`)\n  — class 2. Stable JSON-flat shape, intended for any consumer in any\n  language.\n- **Server REST** (`GET /<resource>`, `POST /<resource>/<id>/<action>`)\n  consumed by both first-party UIs and customer-side scripts — class 2.\n  Flat JSON, versioned, no namespaced keys on the wire.\n- **Fulcro internal Pathom / Transit surface** between a Fulcro UI and\n  its server — class 1. May use EQL, may use Transit, MUST NOT\n  replace the class-2 REST surface that other clients consume.\n- **In-process Clojure dependency** between two products in the same\n  workspace — class 1. Namespaces, protocols, EDN commands. No HTTP.\n- **Webhook / signed callback** delivered to customer-controlled\n  endpoints — class 3. Long-lived shape, signed payload, versioned.\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen designing or reviewing an API:\n1. State the class in the namespace docstring before writing handlers.\n2. If a class-2 endpoint emits a Transit-encoded blob, namespaced\n   keyword key, or EDN set, project it to JSON-flat shape.\n3. If a class-1 surface is being consumed by a class-2 client (e.g.\n   the native app reaches into a Pathom resolver directly), stop and\n   add a class-2 adapter.\n4. New customer-facing endpoints (class 3) require an ADR and a\n   versioning plan — no exceptions.",
               :id :std/api-surface-classes,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "332",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (332): API surface classes — internal (EDN/Transit), first-party cross-language (JSON/SSE/GraphQL), customer extension (REST/webhooks)"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Build / launch / dev tasks belong in bb.edn as Babashka tasks, not in scripts/*.sh — workspace convention",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. **Check** for `bb.edn` at the repo root. If present, the new task goes there.\n2. If **not** present, check whether `bb` is on PATH (`which bb`). If yes, create `bb.edn` and add the task there — do not start with `scripts/*.sh`.\n3. If proposing a `scripts/*.sh`, justify against the \"When `.sh` is still acceptable\" list. If no item applies, the proposal is wrong.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Build / launch / dev tasks belong in bb.edn as Babashka tasks, not in scripts/*.sh — workspace convention"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement},
                :file-globs ["bb.edn" "scripts/**/*.sh"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "<version>1.0.0</version>\n\n# Babashka Tasks Over Shell Scripts\n\n## The rule\n\nBuild, launch, package, lint, test, and dev-loop tasks **SHOULD** be Babashka tasks declared in `bb.edn`, **NOT** shell scripts under `scripts/*.sh`.\n\nThe `bb.edn` in `miniforge` is the canonical reference for the long-form, multi-namespace pattern. The `bb.edn` in `miniforge-control` (added in SA-1 PR #119) is the canonical reference for the self-contained, inline-body pattern.\n\nOperator framing, verbatim:\n\n> we have BB in the repo — why are we adding .sh scripts?\n\n## Why bb over bash\n\n- **Composability.** `:depends [other-task]` is one line. The bash equivalent needs `set -euo pipefail`, careful re-invocation guards, and an ad-hoc convention for what \"already ran\" means.\n- **Cross-platform.** Babashka runs on macOS, Linux, and Windows from a single source. Bash scripts assume a POSIX shell plus GNU coreutils plus macOS quirks (`install_name_tool`, `codesign`, BSD vs GNU `sed`).\n- **Helpers.** `babashka.process/shell` raises on non-zero exit — no `set -euo pipefail` boilerplate. `babashka.fs` covers path manipulation without `realpath` / `readlink -f` portability traps.\n- **Discovery.** `bb tasks` lists every task with its docstring. `ls scripts/` does not.\n- **Convention parity.** Every other Miniforge repo (miniforge, thesium-career sub-projects, and so on) is bb-first. A `scripts/*.sh` for a build/launch task is a one-off that breaks reader expectations.\n\n## When `.sh` is still acceptable\n\n- **Single-line shims** that just invoke a binary with one set of args (e.g. `scripts/codesign-release.sh ./dist/...`) — the bb wrapper would add no value.\n- **POSIX-only contexts** where bb is genuinely unavailable: Alpine container init scripts that run *before* bb is installed, bootstrapping a developer machine that does not yet have bb on PATH.\n- **Git hooks** where bb's startup cost matters and the hook is one shell builtin (`echo`, `test`, `exec`).\n\nEverything else: bb.edn.\n\n## How to apply\n\n- **New repos.** Drop a `bb.edn` at the root from day one. The miniforge `bb.edn` `:init` block (helpers like `run!`, `run-stream!`) is a reasonable starter template.\n- **Existing repos with `scripts/*.sh`.** Introduce `bb.edn` alongside, move tasks one at a time, delete the `.sh` once the bb task is verified equivalent. See [no-dead-code](../foundations/no-dead-code.mdc) — do not leave both versions in the tree.\n- **Naming.** One task per logical action, named after the verb (`build-app`, not `do-build-stuff`). Use `:depends [...]` for ordering.\n- **Long bodies.** When a task body exceeds ~40 lines, factor the logic into a `tasks/` directory namespace and reduce the `bb.edn` task body to a single call into it. The miniforge `tasks/lint.clj`, `tasks/test.clj` files are the pattern.\n\n## Reference exemplars\n\nStable GitHub URLs — these resolve regardless of how this rule file is\nconsumed (in-repo, as `.standards/` submodule, or via the IDE plugin):\n\n| Pattern | Repo | File |\n|---|---|---|\n| Long-form, multi-namespace (tasks/ directory) | miniforge | [bb.edn](https://github.com/miniforge-ai/miniforge/blob/main/bb.edn) + [tasks/](https://github.com/miniforge-ai/miniforge/tree/main/tasks) |\n| Self-contained, inline-body | miniforge-control | [bb.edn](https://github.com/miniforge-ai/miniforge-control/blob/main/bb.edn) (SA-1 PR [#119](https://github.com/miniforge-ai/miniforge-control/pull/119): `build-app`, `package-local-dev-app`, `run-local-apple-app`) |\n\n## EDN-reader gotchas\n\nbb.edn task bodies are read by the EDN reader, not a Clojure compiler. Two common pitfalls:\n\n- **No `#(...)` shorthand.** Use `(fn [x] ...)` instead.\n- **No `#\"...\"` regex literals.** Use `(re-pattern \"...\")` instead.\n- **Do not use `:in :inherit` in subprocess calls** when bb may be invoked through a wrapper that closed stdin (CI runners, `open -W`, GUI launchers). The child process will block waiting on input it will never receive. The default `babashka.process/shell` behavior — no stdin — is correct for build, cargo, and swift commands.\n\n## Forbidden patterns\n\n❌ Adding a new `scripts/*.sh` for a build, launch, package, lint, test, or dev-loop task when `bb` is on the developer's path and the repo (or any Miniforge repo) already has a `bb.edn`.\n\n❌ A `scripts/build.sh` that wraps `cargo build` plus `install_name_tool` plus `codesign` — that is a `(build-app)` bb task, not bash.\n\n❌ Adding bb.edn *and* keeping the equivalent `.sh` \"for now\". Pick one. The bb task wins.\n\nCite this rule (`workflows/bb-over-shell`, dewey 740) in PR review when a `scripts/*.sh` appears in a diff that should have been a bb task.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen asked to add a build/launch/package/dev task:\n\n1. **Check** for `bb.edn` at the repo root. If present, the new task goes there.\n2. If **not** present, check whether `bb` is on PATH (`which bb`). If yes, create `bb.edn` and add the task there — do not start with `scripts/*.sh`.\n3. If proposing a `scripts/*.sh`, justify against the \"When `.sh` is still acceptable\" list. If no item applies, the proposal is wrong.\n4. When refactoring an existing `.sh` into a bb task, delete the `.sh` in the same change once the bb task is verified — see [no-dead-code](../foundations/no-dead-code.mdc).\n\n<example>\nAdding a new `build-app` task in a repo that already has `bb.edn`:\n\n```clojure\n;; bb.edn\n{:tasks\n {build-app\n  {:doc \"Build the release binary and codesign it.\"\n   :task (do (shell \"cargo build --release\")\n             (shell \"install_name_tool\" \"-id\" \"@rpath/libfoo.dylib\" \"target/release/libfoo.dylib\")\n             (shell \"codesign\" \"--sign\" \"-\" \"target/release/libfoo.dylib\"))}}}\n```\n\nInvoke with `bb build-app`. Discoverable via `bb tasks`. Cross-platform-safe (the macOS-only parts are in `shell` invocations that fail loudly on Linux, which is the right behavior).\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\nSame repo, same task, as `scripts/build-app.sh`:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\ncargo build --release\ninstall_name_tool -id @rpath/libfoo.dylib target/release/libfoo.dylib\ncodesign --sign - target/release/libfoo.dylib\n```\n\nWrong: bb is already in the repo, the task is build/launch tier, none of the \"When `.sh` is still acceptable\" exceptions apply, and the script is now invisible to `bb tasks`. Reviewer cites `workflows/bb-over-shell` (dewey 740) and the PR removes it in favor of a bb task.\n</example>",
               :id :std/bb-over-shell,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "740",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (740): Build / launch / dev tasks belong in bb.edn as Babashka tasks, not in scripts/*.sh — workspace convention"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Browser security — server-enforced authz, CSRF, no secrets in browser, blast-radius UI for destructive ops",
               :agent-behavior
               "Four invariants for any browser-facing surface in a miniforge product.\nThese are *blocker* severity — a violation MUST NOT merge.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Browser security — server-enforced authz, CSRF, no secrets in browser, blast-radius UI for destructive ops"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["**/*.html" "**/*.js" "**/*.cljs" "**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Browser security standards\n\nFour invariants for any browser-facing surface in a miniforge product.\nThese are *blocker* severity — a violation MUST NOT merge.\n\n## BSEC-001 — Server enforces authorization\n\nClient-side visibility MUST NOT be treated as authorization. Hiding a\nbutton does NOT prevent the underlying action; the server MUST refuse\nunauthorized requests independent of any UI gating.\n\n```\nBAD:  \"Only show the Delete Repo button to admins.\"\nGOOD: \"Only show the Delete Repo button to admins, AND have the server\n       refuse DELETE /repos/<id> from non-admin sessions.\"\n```\n\nIf the server-side check is missing, the UI gate is decorative and the\nendpoint is exploitable. Reject any PR adding privileged UI without the\nmatching server check.\n\n## BSEC-002 — CSRF protection required\n\nState-changing browser requests MUST include CSRF protection unless the\nauthentication mechanism makes CSRF structurally impossible (e.g.\nAuthorization header with a token NOT auto-attached by the browser).\n\nCookie-based sessions MUST use one of:\n- synchronizer token pattern (CSRF token in form / header, validated\n  server-side)\n- double-submit cookie pattern\n- `SameSite=Strict` or `SameSite=Lax` on the session cookie *plus* a\n  documented threat-model note\n\nGET requests MUST NOT change server state (this is also CSRF-relevant —\nforged GETs from `<img src>` don't carry CSRF tokens).\n\n## BSEC-003 — No secrets in browser code\n\nBrowser-shipped code (JS bundles, HTML, inline scripts, source maps)\nMUST NOT contain:\n\n- API secrets or service tokens\n- private keys (signing, encryption, JWT, etc.)\n- internal credentials of any kind\n- internal hostnames / IPs that aren't intended to be public\n- vendor API keys that aren't designed for browser exposure\n\nBrowser-safe identifiers (publishable Stripe keys, public OAuth client\nIDs, analytics property IDs) are fine but SHOULD be pulled from\nconfiguration, not hard-coded.\n\nIf a secret leaks in browser code, treat as a security incident: rotate\nthe secret first, then fix the code.\n\n## BSEC-004 — Destructive operations need blast-radius UI\n\nHigh-risk actions MUST show the affected resources before execution.\nThe user must see *what* they are about to break, not just *that* they\nare about to break something.\n\n| Action | Blast-radius preview |\n|---|---|\n| delete a repository integration | repos affected, workflows that depend on it |\n| revoke a credential | services that use it, last-used timestamp |\n| cancel running workflows | which runs, which agents, current step |\n| deactivate a policy pack | rules disabled, repos newly out of compliance |\n| change an environment | apps deployed there, current versions |\n\nPair this with JS-007 (typed confirmation for high blast radius) and\nFUL-023 (Fulcro mutations for destructive remote effects require prior\nUI confirmation).\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen writing or reviewing browser code:\n1. For every privileged UI gate, locate the matching server-side check.\n   No match → blocker.\n2. For every state-changing request, verify CSRF posture (header,\n   token, or SameSite documented).\n3. Grep the browser bundle for `key`, `secret`, `token`, `password`,\n   `credential` — investigate every hit.\n4. For every destructive action, confirm the UI shows blast-radius\n   *before* the confirmation prompt, not after.",
               :id :std/browser-security,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "331",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (331): Browser security — server-enforced authz, CSRF, no secrets in browser, blast-radius UI for destructive ops"}
        #:rule{:title "Clojure Polylith + per-file stratified design",
               :remediation
               {:strategy :mechanical,
                :replacement "(get $2 $1 $3)",
                :auto-fixable-default true,
                :exclude-contexts
                [{:path-contains "server/"}
                 {:current-contains
                  [":type" ":priority" ":status" ";; JSON"]}]},
               :agent-behavior
               "- **Exception**: Pure interface files (pass-throughs only) are not bound by the 3-layer maximum\n   - However, if an interface has >5 layers, consider if the component should be split into smaller components",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Clojure Polylith + per-file stratified design"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["components/**/src/**/*.clj"
                 "components/**/src/**/*.cljc"
                 "bases/**/src/**/*.clj"
                 "bases/**/src/**/*.cljc"
                 "projects/**/src/**/*.clj"
                 "projects/**/src/**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Clojure style guidelines\n\n## Polylith architecture (ALWAYS)\n\n- Workspace: `components/`, `bases/`, `projects/`.\n\n- **Cross-component deps** must target `...<component>.interface` (not `.core`).\n- `bases` can depend on component **interfaces**; `projects` can depend on bases and component interfaces.\n- `components` may depend on other components **only via** their `.interface` ns.\n- **Never** depend on `projects` from `bases` or `components`.\n\n## Polylith stability tagging (ALWAYS)\n\n- Create stable tags **only after verification** (E2E tests + dogfooding runs), **not on every merge**.\n- Tag format: `git tag -a stable-YYYYMMDD -m \"Stable: <milestone description>\"`\n- Stable tags tell `poly info` which components have changed since last verified state.\n- Without stable tags, `poly info` shows all components as changed (since initial commit).\n- See **N1-architecture.md § 4.2.2** for complete tagging guidelines and verification checklist.\n\n## Per-file stratified design (ALWAYS)\n\nThe per-file instance of `foundations/stratified-design` (001): inside one\nnamespace, the call graph is a one-way DAG of abstraction. Mark the strata so the\nstructure is legible.\n\n- Structure each ns with **labeled strata** as headings:\n\n  - `;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0`\n  - `;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1`\n  - …\n- **Reverse DAG**: Layer 0 sits at the top of the file (pure/value-level, lowest\n  in the dependency order). A function in **Layer N may call functions in Layer ≤ N**\n  (same or lower-numbered), never a higher-numbered layer. The DAG **branches** —\n  a higher fn composes several lower ones and a lower fn is shared by many above;\n  that is expected, not a violation.\n- **The heading must be true.** If a \"higher\" layer inlines work that belongs\n  below, the label is decorative — extract that work into its own fn at the right\n  layer. A label the code crosses is worse than no label.\n- **Max 3 strata** is a **split heuristic**, not the shape of the idea: a file\n  wanting a fourth band is the signal to split the namespace (see splitting\n  strategy above), never to flatten real strata into three.\n- Reserve the **very bottom** of the file for a `(comment …)` section (REPL testing). No `def/defn/defmacro` after it.\n\n## Component layout conventions\n\n### Required structure\n\n- `components/<name>/src/.../<name>/interface.clj` — **Complete public API surface**\n- `components/<name>/src/.../<name>/core.clj` — Main implementation (≤ 3 layers)\n- `components/<name>/src/.../<name>/spec.clj` — Malli schemas only; no functions except inside `(comment ...)`\n\n### Interface.clj pattern (CRITICAL)\n\n**Layer guidance for interfaces:**\n\n- The 3-layer maximum is **not a hard rule** for pure interface files that only contain pass-through functions\n- However, if an interface has many layers (>5), it may indicate the component is doing too much\n- Consider splitting large components into smaller, focused components to maintain simplicity and clear boundaries\n- Example: An agent component with 10 layers of pass-throughs might be better as separate planner,<br>\nimplementer, and tester components\n\n**Interface requirements:**\n\n- **interface.clj must contain ALL public functions** for the component\n- **interface.clj should contain NO implementations** — only thin pass-throughs\n- Pass-through functions forward to implementation functions in `core.clj` or other namespaces within the component\n- Input validation (specs) should happen in interface before forwarding\n- Example:\n\n  ```clojure\n  ;; CORRECT: interface.clj\n  (defn create-task\n    \"Create a new task (public API).\"\n    [task-data]\n    (s/validate ::task-spec task-data)\n    (core/create-task-impl task-data))\n\n  ;; WRONG: implementation in interface\n  (defn create-task [task-data]\n    (let [id (uuid/v4)\n          task (assoc task-data :id id)]\n      (store/save! task)))\n  ```\n\n### Implementation organization\n\n- **core.clj** — Main implementation logic; organized in ≤ 3 layers\n- **Additional namespaces** — When core.clj exceeds 3 layers or becomes too large:\n  - **Related functions** → subnamespaces (e.g., `views/table.clj`, `views/chart.clj`, `views/panel.clj`)\n  - **Unrelated functions** → separate namespaces (e.g., `executor.clj`, `factory.clj`, `validator.clj`)\n- **Protocol files** — `protocol.clj` for defining protocols/interfaces\n- All implementation namespaces are internal to the component; only `interface.clj` is public\n\n### Namespace splitting strategy\n\nWhen a file exceeds 3 layers:\n\n1. **Identify coherent groups** of functions\n2. **For related functions** (e.g., different views, different formatters):\n   - Create subnamespace: `components/<name>/src/.../<name>/<group>/<subname>.clj`\n   - Example: `reporting/views/table.clj`, `reporting/views/chart.clj`\n3. **For unrelated functions** (e.g., executor + factory + validator):\n   - Create separate namespaces: `components/<name>/src/.../<name>/<concern>.clj`\n   - Example: `agent/executor.clj`, `agent/factory.clj`, `agent/memory.clj`\n4. **Update interface.clj** to import and forward to new namespaces\n5. **Keep each new file** to ≤ 3 layers\n\n## Map access patterns (ALWAYS)\n\n- **Prefer `(get m k default)` over `(or (:k m) default)`**\n\n  ```clojure\n  ;; BAD — silently returns default for any falsy value (false, 0, \"\")\n  (or (:timeout opts) 5000)\n\n  ;; GOOD — explicit default, survives falsy values\n  (get opts :timeout 5000)\n  ```\n\n- The `(or (:k m) fallback)` form is only acceptable when choosing between two\n  **different keys** in the same map (canonical vs. legacy field), never when\n  `fallback` is a computed or literal default:\n\n  ```clojure\n  ;; Acceptable — picking between two key names for the same semantic field\n  (or (get event :workflow/id) (get event :workflow-id))\n\n  ;; BAD — use get with default instead\n  (or (:workflow/id event) \"unknown\")          ; → (get event :workflow/id \"unknown\")\n  (or (:tokens result) total-tokens)           ; → (get result :tokens total-tokens)\n  ```\n\n## Inline expressions: extract anything that grows (ALWAYS)\n\nThe rule that follows applies uniformly to **anonymous functions** (`fn` /\n`#(...)`), **`let` bindings**, and **`cond` / `case` / `condp` arms**. If the\nbody grows beyond a single, intent-bearing line, extract it to a `defn-`\nwith a name that says *what it does* — not how.\n\n### Anonymous functions (`fn` / `#(...)`)\n\nAnonymous functions should be used **only when they enhance the clarity of\nthe surrounding code** — not as a default for every callback.\n\n**Prefer named functions when:**\n\n- The logic has a name worth expressing (extract to `defn-`)\n- The body is more than one non-trivial expression\n- The function is used more than once\n- The parent expression already reads at a high level of abstraction and the\n  anonymous body forces the reader to shift mental levels mid-expression\n\n**Acceptable anonymous function uses:**\n\n```clojure\n;; Short, self-evident predicate inline — intent is clear from context\n(filter :active? users)\n(remove nil? results)\n(keep :name items)\n\n;; Simple structural transform that names itself through context\n(map #(assoc % :processed? true) records)\n\n;; One-off adaptor that has no reusable name\n(sort-by #(get key->cat %) rule-ids)\n```\n\n**Extract to a named function when the body obscures intent:**\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — the anon fn body is complex and the parent reads poorly\n(into {} (map (fn [[k vs]]\n                [k (get (first vs) :rule/category \"0\")])\n              groups))\n\n;; GOOD — named fn expresses the intent; parent reads as a pipeline\n(defn- key->category-entry [[k vs]]\n  [k (get (first vs) :rule/category \"0\")])\n\n(into {} (map key->category-entry groups))\n```\n\n**Never embed anonymous functions in data maps as configuration values.**\nConfiguration maps should reference named functions so the config remains\ndata and the behavior is separately testable:\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — anonymous fn buried in a map; untestable, hard to read\n{:file-pred (fn [path] (str/ends-with? path \".clj\"))}\n\n;; GOOD — named fn at the correct stratum; referenced by the config\n(defn- clojure-file? [path] (str/ends-with? path \".clj\"))\n\n{:file-pred clojure-file?}\n```\n\n### `let` bindings\n\nThe same one-line rule applies to `let` binding right-hand sides. A\nbinding whose value is computed by a multi-form expression obscures the\nshape of the `let` and entangles binding with computation.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — non-trivial computation inline; the let reads as code, not data flow\n(let [config       (get-in ctx [:execution/config])\n      threshold    (or (get-in config [:per-backend backend])\n                       (get config :default)\n                       (* 30 60 1000))\n      enabled?     (and (true? (:agent/watchdog-enabled? config))\n                        (some? threshold)\n                        (not (:agent/dry-run? ctx)))]\n  ...)\n\n;; GOOD — each value is a named helper; the let reads as a pipeline of names\n(defn- resolve-threshold [config backend]\n  (or (get-in config [:per-backend backend])\n      (get config :default)\n      default-gap-threshold-ms))\n\n(defn- watchdog-enabled? [config ctx]\n  (and (true? (:agent/watchdog-enabled? config))\n       (not (:agent/dry-run? ctx))))\n\n(let [config    (get-in ctx [:execution/config])\n      threshold (resolve-threshold config backend)\n      enabled?  (watchdog-enabled? config ctx)]\n  ...)\n```\n\n### `cond` / `case` / `condp` arms\n\nArm bodies in **all three** dispatch forms must fit on one expression.\nA multi-form body hides branching logic inside what reads like a\ndispatch table — the visual shape of the form lies about what it does.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD (cond) — each arm has multi-form work; the dispatch table is gone\n(cond\n  (= phase :verify)\n  (let [budget (get-budget ctx)\n        result (run-tests! ctx :timeout-ms budget)]\n    (record-metrics! ctx result)\n    (build-verify-result result))\n\n  (= phase :review)\n  (let [issues (analyze ctx)\n        decision (decide ctx issues)]\n    (record-fingerprint! ctx issues)\n    (build-review-result decision issues)))\n\n;; GOOD — one named helper per arm; cond is a dispatch table again\n(cond\n  (= phase :verify) (run-and-record-verify ctx)\n  (= phase :review) (run-and-record-review ctx))\n\n;; BAD (case) — multi-form body buried under the dispatch keyword\n(case status\n  :completed (do (mark-done! ctx)\n                 (emit-completion! ctx)\n                 (build-success-result ctx))\n  :failed    (do (record-failure! ctx)\n                 (build-failure-result ctx)))\n\n;; GOOD\n(case status\n  :completed (finalize-success ctx)\n  :failed    (finalize-failure ctx))\n\n;; BAD (condp) — predicate-shaped dispatch with let-laden arms\n(condp re-matches input\n  #\"v1/.*\" (let [parsed (parse-v1 input)]\n             (validate-v1! parsed)\n             (handle-v1 parsed))\n  #\"v2/.*\" (let [parsed (parse-v2 input)]\n             (validate-v2! parsed)\n             (handle-v2 parsed)))\n\n;; GOOD\n(condp re-matches input\n  #\"v1/.*\" (process-v1 input)\n  #\"v2/.*\" (process-v2 input))\n```\n\n### Map literal construction (ALWAYS)\n\nMap literals must read as **key → value, one line per pair**. If a\nvalue requires computation, compute it in a `let` above the map and\nreference the binding by name.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — computation embedded in map values; intent is buried\n{:execution/id        (random-uuid)\n :execution/started?  true\n :execution/budget-ms (* (get config :budget-mins 30) 60 1000)\n :execution/threshold (or (get-in config [:per-backend backend])\n                          (get config :default)\n                          90000)\n :execution/backends  (->> (get config :allowed-backends)\n                           (remove #{:claude-legacy})\n                           sort\n                           vec)\n :execution/mode      (if (:governed? ctx) :governed :local)}\n\n;; GOOD — values pre-computed in let; map construction is pure data\n(let [budget-ms (* (get config :budget-mins 30) 60 1000)\n      threshold (resolve-threshold config backend)\n      backends  (allowed-backends config)\n      mode      (execution-mode ctx)]\n  {:execution/id        (random-uuid)\n   :execution/started?  true\n   :execution/budget-ms budget-ms\n   :execution/threshold threshold\n   :execution/backends  backends\n   :execution/mode      mode})\n```\n\nAcceptable inline values: literals, constant lookups (`:foo`,\n`some-const`), one-arg function calls whose name carries the intent\n(`(random-uuid)`, `(now)`, `(read-config)`). Anything with arithmetic,\nthreading, or branching belongs in the `let` above.\n\n### Test code is not exempt\n\nAll rules in this section apply to `test/` namespaces. Tests are also\ndocumentation — a multi-form `cond` arm or a 4-line `let` binding in\na test obscures the contract the test is meant to pin.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen creating/editing a namespace:\n\n1) Insert/maintain **Layer 0/1/2** headings and keep them **monotonic increasing** (0 → 1 → 2).\n2) Keep **pure helpers** and external calls that are side-effect free in **Layer 0**.\n3) Put orchestration that **only depends on Layer 0** in **Layer 1**, and so on.\n4) If you cross 3 layers, **propose a split** using the namespace splitting strategy above.\n   - **Exception**: Pure interface files (pass-throughs only) are not bound by the 3-layer maximum\n   - However, if an interface has >5 layers, consider if the component should be split into smaller components\n5) Ensure the last top-level form is a single `(comment …)` block with quick REPL samples.\n6) For cross-component calls, target `...<other>.interface` — and surface a fix if `.core` is used.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Good: Thin interface with pass-through\n\n```clojure\n;; components/task/src/ai/miniforge/task/interface.clj\n(ns ai.miniforge.task.interface\n  (:require [ai.miniforge.task.core :as core]\n            [ai.miniforge.task.validator :as validator]))\n\n;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0\n;; Public API - validation + forwarding only\n\n(defn create-task\n  \"Create a new task.\"\n  [task-data]\n  (validator/validate-task-data task-data)\n  (core/create-task-impl task-data))\n\n(defn get-task\n  \"Retrieve task by ID.\"\n  [task-id]\n  (core/get-task-impl task-id))\n```\n\n### Good: Split oversized file by related functions\n\n```clojure\n;; Before: reporting/views.clj with 8 layers\n;; After:\n;; - reporting/views/table.clj (≤3 layers)\n;; - reporting/views/chart.clj (≤3 layers)\n;; - reporting/views/panel.clj (≤3 layers)\n\n;; components/reporting/src/ai/miniforge/reporting/interface.clj\n(ns ai.miniforge.reporting.interface\n  (:require [ai.miniforge.reporting.views.table :as table]\n            [ai.miniforge.reporting.views.chart :as chart]\n            [ai.miniforge.reporting.views.panel :as panel]))\n\n(defn render-table [data] (table/render data))\n(defn render-chart [data] (chart/render data))\n(defn render-panel [data] (panel/render data))\n```\n\n### Bad: Implementation in interface\n\n```clojure\n;; components/task/src/ai/miniforge/task/interface.clj\n(ns ai.miniforge.task.interface\n  (:require [datalevin.core :as d]))\n\n;; WRONG: Implementation details in interface\n(defn create-task [task-data]\n  (let [conn (d/get-conn)\n        id (java.util.UUID/randomUUID)\n        task (assoc task-data :id id :created-at (java.time.Instant/now))]\n    (d/transact! conn [task])\n    task))\n```",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/clojure,
               :detection
               {:type :content-scan,
                :pattern
                "\\(or \\((:[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9?!_*+<>-]*)\\s+([a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*)\\)\\s+((?:nil|true|false|\"[^\"]*\"|-?\\d+(?:\\.\\d+)?|\\[\\]|\\{\\}|:[a-zA-Z][a-zA-Z0-9_-]*))\\)",
                :mode :positive},
               :category "210",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (210): Clojure Polylith + per-file stratified design"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Clojure exception handling — prefer slingshot `try+` / `throw+`; plain `try` only in narrow exemptions (no-slingshot-dep leaf catch, leaf inside `try+`, REPL/`(comment …)`)",
               :agent-behavior
               "- Default new catch sites to `try+`; add the slingshot require if absent.\n- If the throw site is `throw+` / `throw-anomaly!`, the catch site MUST be `try+` — never plain `try` digging into `ex-data` for `:anomaly/category`.\n- Reject `try+` whose every catch is class-only AND the component has no other slingshot use — either commit to `try+` across the component or drop the `+`.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Plain `try`/`throw` where slingshot `try+`/`throw+` is required. Catch by value with a key-value selector and throw structured payloads via throw+/throw-anomaly!; plain try/throw only in the documented narrow exemptions. (Whether to throw at all in normal flow is exceptions-as-data, not this rule.)"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["components/**/src/**/*.clj"
                 "components/**/src/**/*.cljc"
                 "bases/**/src/**/*.clj"
                 "bases/**/src/**/*.cljc"
                 "projects/**/src/**/*.clj"
                 "projects/**/src/**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Clojure exception handling — prefer `try+` / `throw+`\n\nSlingshot's `try+` is a **strict superset** of Clojure's `try`. It catches\nevery Throwable that `try` does (selector form: class name → `(instance?\n<class-name> %)`), and adds three more selector forms — key-value vectors,\npredicates, and `%`-substituting selector forms — that operate on the\nthrown value's shape.\n\nThere is no functional reason to choose `try` over `try+` once slingshot\nis on the component's classpath. New code defaults to `try+`. Legacy `try`\nsites are not a defect on their own and don't need urgent rewrites, but\nshould converge on `try+` opportunistically and during the post-dogfood\naudit (see `project/dogfood-readiness` follow-up notes).\n\nThis rule is the operational complement to:\n\n- `foundations/result-handling` (003) — return anomalies as data; predicates\n  / constructors over hand-built maps.\n- `foundations/exceptions-as-data` (005) — throws are reserved for absolute\n  process boundaries; flows return anomalies.\n\nWhen you DO catch — at one of those boundaries — this rule says *which form*.\n\n## Default: `try+`\n\nFor any component that already imports slingshot (or that catches anything\nbeyond a single trivial Java class), use `try+`:\n\n```clojure\n(:require [slingshot.slingshot :refer [try+ throw+]])\n\n;; Catches everything plain `try` catches (class selector)…\n(try+\n  (.read input)\n  (catch IOException _ nil))\n\n;; …and dispatches on the thrown map's shape (key-value selector)…\n(try+\n  (verify-budget! state)\n  (catch [:anomaly/category :anomalies.budget/exceeded] anomaly\n    (escalate! anomaly)))\n\n;; …and on a predicate, or a `%`-substituting form, or a destructured map.\n(try+\n  (do-thing)\n  (catch map? m (handle-map m))\n  (catch #(= :recoverable (:severity %))\n         {:keys [reason]}\n    (recover! reason)))\n```\n\n`&throw-context` inside any catch clause carries `:object`, `:message`,\n`:cause`, `:stack-trace`, and (for non-Throwable catches) `:wrapper` and\n`:throwable`. That introspection is available even when the only selector\nin the form is a Java class.\n\n## Allowed: plain `try`\n\n`try` is acceptable in three narrow cases:\n\n1. **The component has no slingshot dep and adding one would only be for\n   this catch.** Adding `slingshot/slingshot` is small but real footprint;\n   if the component otherwise has no use for it, plain `try` keeps the\n   dep graph tight.\n\n2. **A leaf `try` inside a `try+` body** that exists only to satisfy a\n   Java try-with-resources / try-finally pattern that's clearer with\n   class-only catches. Slingshot supports nested forms; readability wins.\n\n3. **REPL / scratch / `(comment …)` blocks** where the import overhead\n   isn't worth it.\n\nIn any of these cases, the choice is **deliberate**, named in a code\ncomment if non-obvious, and reviewable.\n\n## When you DO throw\n\nThrowing is reserved for absolute boundaries (per `foundations/exceptions-\nas-data`). When you throw at a boundary:\n\n**Scope (211 vs 005).** This rule governs the *macro choice* —\n`throw+` / `throw-anomaly!` vs plain `throw` — for a throw that is itself\nlegitimate (at an absolute boundary). Whether to throw **at all** in\nnormal control flow belongs to `foundations/exceptions-as-data` (005),\nnot here. A `(throw (ex-info …))` guarded by a status check in ordinary\nflow is a 005 violation (it should return an anomaly as a value); do not\n*also* flag it as a 211 violation. 211 fires on a throw only once the\nthrow is legitimate and the question is which macro carries the payload.\n\n- **Use `throw+` (or `response.anomaly/throw-anomaly!`) for structured\n  payloads.** A map carries the anomaly category, the offending values,\n  any actionable context. `try+` consumers can dispatch on\n  `:anomaly/category` directly — no `ex-data` digging.\n\n```clojure\n(throw-anomaly! :anomalies.llm/rate-limited\n                \"Rate limit hit\"\n                {:anomaly.llm/backend :anthropic :status 429})\n```\n\n- **Use plain `throw` only for genuine Java/JVM exceptions** that you want\n  to propagate as a Throwable (e.g., `IllegalArgumentException` for an\n  exhaustive case match — see `foundations/exceptions-as-data` §\n  programmer-error guards).\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- **`try+` block with one class-only catch when the component has no other\n  slingshot use** — adds a dep without reason. Either drop the `+` or\n  introduce `try+` consistently across the component's catch sites.\n\n- **Plain `try` catching a value thrown by `throw+` / `throw-anomaly!`.**\n  The catch site reaches into `ex-data` to read the anomaly's category,\n  defeating the structured contract. Use `try+` and a key-value selector.\n\n  ```clojure\n  ;; BAD — plain try, ex-data digging, brittle\n  (try\n    (verify-budget! state)\n    (catch Exception e\n      (when (= :anomalies.budget/exceeded\n               (-> e ex-data :anomaly/category))\n        (escalate!))))\n\n  ;; GOOD — try+, key-value selector reads the anomaly directly\n  (try+\n    (verify-budget! state)\n    (catch [:anomaly/category :anomalies.budget/exceeded] _\n      (escalate!)))\n  ```\n\n- **Switching between `try` and `try+` mid-component for stylistic\n  consistency only.** If the component's catch sites all see Java classes\n  today and it has no slingshot dep, leaving them as `try` is fine.\n  Mass-rewriting them just to \"match\" a new `try+` site dilutes diff\n  signal without behavior change.\n\n- **Using `throw` to throw a map (no `ex-info`) and catching with `try`.**\n  This works (the wrapper handling is implicit) but loses every readability\n  benefit of slingshot. Use `throw+` and `try+`.\n\n## Migration posture\n\nThe miniforge codebase as of 2026-05-04 has ~7 `try+` callsites and ~240\nplain `try` callsites. The plain-`try` count is not a bug list — most of\nthose sites catch a single Java class, swallow the failure, and would\ngain nothing from the value-shape selectors. They predate this rule.\n\nWhen picking up legacy code:\n\n1. If you're already editing a callsite for unrelated reasons and the\n   component already imports slingshot, switch the form to `try+` —\n   essentially free.\n2. If the component does not yet import slingshot and the catch is\n   class-only, leave `try` alone unless something else in the same PR\n   pulls slingshot in.\n3. Don't open standalone \"convert try to try+\" PRs while dogfood\n   stabilization is in progress — the migration audit is scheduled for\n   the consistently-passing phase, prepping for the next major release\n   (see `project_try_plus_migration` memory).\n\n## Agent behavior\n\n- Default new catch sites to `try+`; add the slingshot require if absent.\n- If the throw site is `throw+` / `throw-anomaly!`, the catch site MUST be `try+` — never plain `try` digging into `ex-data` for `:anomaly/category`.\n- Reject `try+` whose every catch is class-only AND the component has no other slingshot use — either commit to `try+` across the component or drop the `+`.\n- Plain `try` is allowed only in three narrow cases: (a) component has no slingshot dep and adding one would be solely for this catch; (b) leaf `try` nested inside a `try+` body for try-finally clarity; (c) REPL / `(comment …)` blocks.\n- Don't mass-migrate. Convergence is opportunistic during normal work; full audit deferred to post-dogfood readiness.\n\n## Examples\n\n<example>\nMixed selectors in one form — slingshot's actual strength.\n\n```clojure\n(try+\n  (run-step ctx)\n  (catch [:anomaly/category :anomalies.llm/rate-limited]\n         {:keys [anomaly.llm/backend]}\n    (backoff! backend))\n  (catch IOException _e\n    (response/failure :step/io-failed))\n  (catch map? anomaly\n    (response/failure anomaly)))\n```\n</example>\n\n<example>\nPlain `try` is fine here — leaf component with one class-only catch and\nno other slingshot use.\n\n```clojure\n(ns ai.miniforge.tiny-util.core)\n\n(defn parse-int-safe [s]\n  (try\n    (Long/parseLong s)\n    (catch NumberFormatException _ nil)))\n```\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\nPlain `try` digging into `ex-data` to read an anomaly the producer threw\nvia `throw+`. Replace with `try+` and a key-value selector.\n\n```clojure\n(try\n  (verify-budget! state)\n  (catch Exception e\n    (when (= :anomalies.budget/exceeded\n             (-> e ex-data :anomaly/category))\n      (escalate!))))\n```\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n`try+` with a single class-only catch in a component with no other\nslingshot use. Either drop the `+` or commit to `try+` across the\ncomponent's catch sites.\n\n```clojure\n(ns ai.miniforge.tiny-util.core\n  (:require [slingshot.slingshot :refer [try+]]))\n\n(try+\n  (.read input)\n  (catch IOException _ nil))\n```\n</example>",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/clojure-exception-handling,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "211",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (211): Clojure exception handling — prefer slingshot `try+` / `throw+`; plain `try` only in narrow exemptions (no-slingshot-dep leaf catch, leaf inside `try+`, REPL/`(comment …)`)"}
        #:rule{:title
               "No requiring-resolve — fix the underlying load-order/design, don't lazy-resolve around it",
               :agent-behavior
               "Never introduce `(requiring-resolve ...)`. When you need a function from another namespace, add a direct `(:require [ns :as alias])` to the `ns` form and call `alias/fn`. If a direct require would create a load-order cycle, do NOT reach for `requiring-resolve` — instead fix the underlying design: move the shared interface to a lower stratum, split the over-large namespace, or add an adapter component.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "requiring-resolve is an anti-pattern. Use a direct (:require [ns :as alias]) and call alias/fn. If a direct require would cause a load-order cycle, fix the design (move the interface down a stratum, split the namespace, or introduce an adapter) — do not lazy-resolve around it."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.clj" "**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# No requiring-resolve (BLOCKING)\n\n`requiring-resolve` is an agent-smell. It almost always papers over a load-order\nor design problem that should be fixed at the source, not deferred to runtime.\n\n## The rule\n\nDo not call `requiring-resolve`. Resolve dependencies with a direct\n`(:require [some.ns :as alias])` in the namespace form and call `alias/fn`.\n\nThe only legitimate use is a genuine plugin/extension point where the dependency\nis *optionally* present at the module level (a true late-binding seam, declared\nas such). That is rare; default to a direct require.\n\n## Why it's blocked\n\nA direct require that would cause a cycle is signalling a real design issue:\n\n- an \"upward\" dependency (a lower stratum reaching into a higher one),\n- a namespace that does too much and should be split, or\n- a missing adapter/interface between two components.\n\n`requiring-resolve` hides that signal and lets the cycle persist. Fixing the\ndesign keeps the dependency graph honest and the load order static.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nNever introduce `(requiring-resolve ...)`. When you need a function from another\nnamespace, add a direct `(:require [ns :as alias])` to the `ns` form and call\n`alias/fn`. If a direct require would create a load-order cycle, do NOT reach for\n`requiring-resolve` — instead fix the underlying design: move the shared\ninterface to a lower stratum, split the over-large namespace, or add an adapter\ncomponent. If you believe a case is a genuine optional plugin/extension point,\ncall it out explicitly in the code and the PR rather than using it silently.",
               :id :std/clojure-no-requiring-resolve,
               :detection
               {:type :content-scan,
                :pattern "[(/]requiring-resolve\\b",
                :mode :positive},
               :category "212",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (212): No requiring-resolve — fix the underlying load-order/design, don't lazy-resolve around it"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Code quality — composable pipelines, no nested conditionals, DRY component extraction",
               :agent-behavior
               "When writing or reviewing code:\n1. If a function exceeds one screen, ask: can this be split into named pipeline stages?\n2. If you see nested `if`/`when` blocks, refactor to `cond` or guard clauses.\n3. If you find yourself duplicating a function from another namespace, stop — create or identify the right component and add it to the interface.\n4. Composition is the design. Don't name composed pipelines \"main\" or \"run\" — name them for what they produce.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Code quality — composable pipelines, no nested conditionals, DRY component extraction"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Code Quality (ALWAYS)\n\n## Composable functions — small and pipeline-oriented\n\nEvery function must read as a **pipeline**: data enters, transforms, exits. No hidden branches, no side effects buried in the middle, no implicit state mutations.\n\n**Design rule:** Write the smallest function that does one thing. Compose up.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — one function does discovery, filtering, and formatting\n(defn process-results [items]\n  (let [results []]\n    (doseq [item items]\n      (when (valid? item)\n        (when-let [x (transform item)]\n          (conj results (format x)))))\n    results))\n\n;; GOOD — three pure functions, each one concern\n(defn valid-items   [items]   (filter valid? items))\n(defn transform-all [items]   (keep transform items))\n(defn format-all    [items]   (mapv format items))\n\n;; Composed pipeline — reads as data flow\n(defn process-results [items]\n  (-> items valid-items transform-all format-all))\n```\n\nThe composed form is **the function** — not a \"main\" that calls helpers. There is no incidental complexity.\n\n## No nested conditionals\n\nNested `if`/`cond`/`when` blocks are a signal that control flow and data transformation are entangled. Restructure instead.\n\n**Prefer:**\n- Guard clauses (`when`/`if` at the top, return early via `nil` or exception)\n- `cond->` / `cond->>` for conditional transformation chains\n- Lookup tables / dispatch maps over `cond` branches\n- `some->` / `some->>` for optional-chain patterns\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — nested conditionals\n(defn classify [evt]\n  (if (= :workflow/started (:event/type evt))\n    (if-let [id (:workflow/id evt)]\n      (if (valid-id? id)\n        :valid-start\n        :bad-id)\n      :missing-id)\n    :not-a-start))\n\n;; GOOD — flat cond\n(defn classify [evt]\n  (let [type (:event/type evt)\n        id   (:workflow/id evt)]\n    (cond\n      (not= :workflow/started type) :not-a-start\n      (nil? id)                     :missing-id\n      (not (valid-id? id))          :bad-id\n      :else                         :valid-start)))\n```\n\nA maximum of **one level of conditional nesting** is the goal. Two levels demands a refactor. Three is never acceptable.\n\n## DRY — extract to a component, import the interface\n\nDuplication of logic is a signal that a component boundary is missing.\n\n**Rules:**\n- If two components share more than one helper function, that logic belongs in a third component.\n- Never copy-paste implementations across namespaces. If you find yourself doing so, stop and create a shared component.\n- The shared component exposes an `interface.clj`. Consumers depend on the interface, never on the internal namespace.\n\n```\n;; BAD: same timestamp normalization in events.clj and workflows.clj\n(defn normalize-ts [ts] ...)  ; duplicated!\n\n;; GOOD: shared component\ncomponents/temporal/src/ai/miniforge/temporal/interface.clj\n  (def normalize-ts core/normalize-ts)\n\n;; Consumers:\n(:require [ai.miniforge.temporal.interface :as temporal])\n(temporal/normalize-ts ts)\n```\n\n**What belongs in a component:**\n- Any pure function used in more than one component\n- Any protocol/schema referenced across components\n- Any formatting or parsing utility with multiple callers\n\n**What stays local:**\n- Functions called only within one component's internal namespaces\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. If a function exceeds one screen, ask: can this be split into named pipeline stages?\n2. If you see nested `if`/`when` blocks, refactor to `cond` or guard clauses.\n3. If you find yourself duplicating a function from another namespace, stop — create or identify the right component and add it to the interface.\n4. Composition is the design. Don't name composed pipelines \"main\" or \"run\" — name them for what they produce.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Functions with multiple concerns named after a verb (e.g., `process-and-save`, `load-and-validate`)\n- `let` bindings that grow to 10+ forms — split the function\n- `cond` branches with `let` bodies — extract each branch to a named function\n- Utility functions copied between components instead of extracted\n- \"Helper\" files that grow without bound because no component owns them",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/code-quality,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "002",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (002): Code quality — composable pipelines, no nested conditionals, DRY component extraction"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Adversarial PR-review obligations — happy-path tracing, bootstrap-order verification, compile-test fact-claims, default-value scrutiny, intent over symptoms, refactor-not-exempt",
               :agent-behavior "<version>1.1.0</version>",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Adversarial PR-review obligations — happy-path tracing, bootstrap-order verification, compile-test fact-claims, default-value scrutiny, intent over symptoms, refactor-not-exempt"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Code-Review Rigor (ALWAYS)\n\n<version>1.1.0</version>\n\n## Why this exists\n\n`feedback_standards_review_per_pr` already says: run a standards audit on every PR before push. That memory specifies *which* rule files to scan against. It does not specify *how* the scan must be conducted.\n\nAcross a recent burst of subagent-authored PRs (miniforge-control #126, #127, #128) significant correctness bugs landed in the spot-check-passed diff and were caught only by GitHub Copilot's review. Pattern audit:\n\n- A `repo_root` resolution that fell back to a non-git directory on the **default** code path. Guaranteed failure for the most common operator flow. Not an edge case; the common case.\n- A restoration trigger keyed on `handshake == .ready` when the store's own source line 51 said ready flips *before any payload arrives*. The subagent's report described the timing correctly; the reviewer (me) read the report and did not connect the dots.\n- A subagent claim that a Swift language feature \"doesn't work\" — accepted at face value, the workaround built around it. The claim was wrong. A thirty-second compile-test would have refuted it.\n- A `codesign --remove-signature` that exits non-zero on unsigned files and aborts the pipeline on first run. Default-value path again.\n\nThe common shape across all four: **symptom-grep passed; trace-execution would have caught them.** The reviewer scanned for missing `final`, inline strings, force-unwraps — patterns subagents are already trained to avoid — and skipped the questions that catch real bugs.\n\nThis rule codifies the obligations the reviewer MUST satisfy before declaring a PR review clean.\n\n## Scope\n\nApplies to every PR review pass — self-authored, subagent-delegated, Copilot-suggested, human-collaborator. Fires alongside `feedback_standards_review_per_pr` (which selects *what* to scan); this rule governs *how* to scan.\n\n## The obligations\n\n### 1. Happy-path trace\n\nWalk one realistic operator flow field-by-field through the diff before declaring the spot-check clean.\n\nFor each step:\n\n- State the value each parameter takes when the operator does the simplest thing.\n- For each guard / `if let` / validation: confirm the value passes.\n- For each function boundary: confirm the receiving side accepts the shape the calling side produced.\n\nThe bug-class this catches: **default-value holes**. The operator does what feels natural — leaves an optional field blank, accepts the picker default, types nothing where typing was optional — and the code under review rejects them at a downstream guard.\n\nWorked example (real, from PR #128):\n\n> Operator opens the LaunchAgentSheet, picks createNew worktree mode, types a branch name (the only required field), leaves cwd blank, hits Launch.\n>\n> - `LaunchAgentSheet.launch()` calls `coordinator.createWorktreeAndLaunchAgent(branch: \"x\", base: nil, path: nil, ..., cwd: nil)`.\n> - `createWorktreeAndLaunchAgent` calls `createWorktree(branch: \"x\", base: nil, path: nil)`. FFI returns a worktree id.\n> - Resolved cwd chain: cwd is nil → path is nil → empty string.\n> - `launchAgent(cwd: \"\")` hits the empty-cwd guard → `requestEncodeFailed`. The launch phase never fires.\n\nThat trace takes ninety seconds. The bug was invisible to the standards-pass that ran instead.\n\n### 2. State-machine / bootstrap-order verification\n\nFor any race / state-transition / lifecycle code, READ THE SOURCE ORDER. Do not take a subagent's prose timing claim at face value.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- For `init` / `bootstrap` / `start!` / `attach!` functions: list the steps in execution order. Note what state exists between each step.\n- For `@Observable` / observer-driven view code: identify what counter or flag triggers the observation. Confirm that counter is non-zero (or whatever invariant the consumer assumed) at the moment the consumer fires.\n- For supervisor / replay / handshake paths: ask what the bucket / queue / state looks like at the FIRST observation point. Note that \"marked ready\" is not the same as \"has data.\"\n\nThe bug-class this catches: **observation-before-data**. State flips to a sentinel value before any payload populates the structure the observation depends on. The consumer fires, sees nothing, latches, and never refires.\n\nWorked example (real, from PR #127):\n\n> `SupervisoryStateStore.bootstrap()` line 51, verbatim: `// Mark ready optimistically; the first snapshot delta will populate the buckets.`\n>\n> The view-side restoration triggered on `.onChange(of: store.handshake)`. That observation fires when handshake flips to `.ready` — which the source comment says is BEFORE the first delta lands. The bucket-membership check failed against an empty bucket; the one-shot latch flipped; the snapshot that arrived a moment later was permanently ignored.\n>\n> The line 51 comment was in the subagent's reference frame the whole time. The reviewer read past it. Fixing the bug after Copilot caught it took fifteen minutes; preventing it would have taken thirty seconds of \"wait, ready before any data?\"\n\n### 3. Compile-test fact-claims\n\nWhen a subagent (or memory entry, or past intuition, or operator-recalled lore) claims \"X does not work\" or \"this language has constraint Y,\" verify by compilation BEFORE building the workaround.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- If the claim is \"Swift rejects this pattern\" → write the pattern in the worktree, run `swift build`, read the compiler's actual error.\n- If the claim is \"the FFI rejects this payload shape\" → write the test, run it, read the rejection.\n- If the claim is \"this library does not expose Y\" → grep the library source for `pub fn Y` / `public func Y` / etc.\n\nThe bug-class this catches: **workarounds built on wrong premises**. The wrong premise persists indefinitely because no one tests it; the workaround accumulates context and becomes load-bearing.\n\nWorked example (real, from PR #127):\n\n> Subagent claim: `Swift rejects Self.<key> in a stored-property initializer` (covariant Self). The subagent proposed string-literal duplication as the workaround.\n>\n> Copilot review pointed out `DetailActionBar.swift` uses exactly `@AppStorage(Self.confirmDestructiveKey)`. The narrower truth: `Self.<key>` fails in `final class`, succeeds in `struct`. The reviewer accepted the broader-claim workaround without testing.\n>\n> Compile cost to refute: thirty seconds. Real fix once refuted: a file-scope enum sidesteps both the literal-required constraint AND the covariant-Self constraint.\n\n### 4. First-run / default-value / nil-path scrutiny\n\nTreat default values as the **common** case, not the edge case. For every parameter, ask:\n\n- What value does this hold if the operator does the simplest thing? (Often: nil, empty string, the default in the picker, the env var unset.)\n- What value does this hold on the very first cold run, with no prior state?\n- Does the code accept that value, or reject it via a guard?\n\nDefault values are the path most operators take most often. A code path that fails on the default is broken for the modal user, not for an edge case.\n\nWorked example (real, from PR #126):\n\n> `codesign --remove-signature` exits non-zero when the target is unsigned. The sign-app task ran this as a \"best-effort cleanup\" before re-signing.\n>\n> First-run state: a freshly-built dylib is unsigned. `--remove-signature` fails. `shell!` aborts the task. The very first invocation of sign-app fails.\n>\n> The fix: `:continue true` on the cleanup step. The diagnosis is one sentence; the question that would have surfaced it is \"what happens on the first run?\"\n\n### 5. Intent, not symptoms\n\nStandards-pass that grep for missing `final`, inline strings, force-unwraps, `Any` usage catches style violations subagents are *already trained to avoid*. Style violations are not where bugs hide. Trace intent.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- For every numeric or string literal in the diff: ask \"what does this value mean?\" Not \"is it through a `Tokens.*` / `Strings.*` namespace?\" The namespace catches the obvious 2; the meaning catches the wrong 3.\n- For every error variant: ask \"when does this fire, and what is the operator-visible consequence?\" Not \"is it a typed enum?\" The enum catches the obvious failure; the wrong-arm-for-the-failure catches the bug. (PR #123 conflated request-encode and response-decode errors. Both were typed; the wrong one fired on request-encode.)\n- For every test assertion: ask \"what fails this test that the production code currently does correctly?\" Not \"is it a typed assertion?\" Tests that always pass catch nothing.\n\n### 6. Refactor is not exempt\n\nA \"pure restructuring\" / \"no behaviour change\" / \"just a move\" claim is a **scrutiny suppressant**, not a scrutiny waiver. Moved code is new code at its new site. Apply obligations 1–5 to the moved lines as if you had just written them — *especially* obligation 4 (first-run / default-value / nil-path scrutiny), because latent bugs ride along invisibly: the diff reads as a move, so the eye skips the body.\n\nSpecifically:\n\n- For every block lifted from elsewhere (extracted helper, inlined function, relocated file): re-trace it at the destination. Do not let the provenance (\"this already shipped\") stand in for a review.\n- If the moved code arrived with **no tests**, the move is the moment to add them — the absence at the origin is why the bug survived. `tests-with-code` (716) already says a no-test-diff PR must claim pure restructuring; this obligation closes the loop by making that claim *cost* a re-trace, not buy a pass.\n- A bug that was latent in `main` becomes **yours** the moment your PR ships it into a new file or call site. \"It was already broken\" is not a defence in review.\n\nThe bug-class this catches: **latent bugs laundered through a refactor**. The original site never got obligation-4 scrutiny; the move re-presents the code as already-trusted, so it never gets it at the destination either.\n\nWorked example (real, from PR #132):\n\n> `renderValue` was lifted verbatim from SA-1's `PropertyList` into a new `detailValueString` helper, labelled \"no behaviour change.\" It checked `as? NSNumber` before `as? Bool` — and `JSONSerialization` bridges JSON booleans to `__NSCFBoolean`, which IS an `NSNumber`, so every boolean field rendered as `1`/`0`.\n>\n> The bug had shipped in SA-1 and was untested. The extraction PR re-presented it as a trusted move; obligation 4 (\"what does a boolean value render as?\") was never asked because the helper was tagged moved-not-written. Compile-and-test cost to surface: one assertion decoding `{\"x\": true}`. Caught by Copilot instead.\n\n## How to apply\n\nAfter the implementation is done, before pushing the PR:\n\n1. Re-read the diff with the obligations as a checklist.\n2. For obligation 1: pick one realistic operator flow. State each parameter's value at each step. Confirm each guard.\n3. For obligation 2: identify any race / bootstrap / observation site. Read its source order. List what state exists at each step.\n4. For obligation 3: identify any \"X doesn't work\" claim in subagent reports, memory entries, or your own reasoning. Verify by compiling / grepping / testing.\n5. For obligation 4: for each parameter, write down its first-run / nil / default value. Confirm the code accepts it.\n6. For obligation 5: pick three literals + three error variants + three test assertions from the diff. Apply the intent question. If any fails, fix before push.\n7. For obligation 6: for every block the diff *moves* rather than writes, re-run obligations 1–5 on it at the destination. If it arrived untested, add the test now.\n\nIf any obligation surfaces a bug: fix it in a same-branch commit before push. Do NOT push and rely on Copilot to catch it — Copilot is not your QA pass, and Copilot's catches add review-round-trip latency.\n\n## What this rule does NOT do\n\n- It does not replace the per-language standards rules. `swift.mdc`, `rust.mdc`, `clojure.mdc` still govern *style*. This rule governs *correctness review*.\n- It does not require a written audit document. The obligations are mental and quick; capture them in your reasoning, not as an artifact.\n- It does not apply to trivial conversational turns or single-line typo fixes. It applies when a diff carries meaningful behaviour.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- `feedback_standards_review_per_pr` (operator memory) — the *what* to audit against. This rule is the *how*.\n- `pre-commit-discipline` (dewey 715) — what blocks the commit. This rule fires before the commit succeeds.\n- `tests-with-code` (dewey 716) — a no-test-diff PR must claim pure restructuring. Obligation 6 makes that claim cost a re-trace instead of buying a pass.\n- `pr-layering` (dewey 722) — what the PR contains. This rule reviews that content.\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen a subagent reports \"standards-pass clean on first read\" or \"no fix commits needed\", treat that as *zero* signal toward correctness. Run the obligations yourself. If you cannot run them — the diff is too large to trace in a session, or you lack context for the realistic operator flow — say so and ask the operator to pause before merge rather than rubber-stamping the subagent's confidence. Treat \"it's just a refactor\" with the same suspicion (obligation 6) — that phrase has shipped a latent bug into a new file at least once (PR #132).",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/code-review-rigor,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "720",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (720): Adversarial PR-review obligations — happy-path tracing, bootstrap-order verification, compile-test fact-claims, default-value scrutiny, intent over symptoms, refactor-not-exempt"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Configuration is data — operational values live in .edn files, not as embedded literals in code, and never as runtime constructions of the configuration shape",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Every operational literal in code (timeouts, retry counts, thresholds, URLs, paths) — ask: would an operator want to change this without a code release? If yes → move to EDN.\n2. Reject PRs that add a feature flag, threshold, or backend list as a hard-coded `def`.\n3. When extracting to EDN, also add (or extend) the config's Malli schema.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Operator-tunable value hardcoded in code. Timeouts, retries, sizes, endpoints and similar operational values belong in an .edn config with a Malli schema; keep a named def only as the silent-config fallback, resolved through a config lookup."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Configuration is Data (ALWAYS)\n\n**Operational configuration lives in `.edn` files, not in code.** If a value can be reasonably overridden at install time, run time, or per-environment — by an operator who is not editing source — it is configuration, and it goes in EDN.\n\n## The rule\n\nThree signals that a value is configuration:\n\n1. **Different deployments want different values.** Local dev wants short timeouts; production wants long ones. A test fixture wants a stub URL; production wants a real one.\n2. **An operator should be able to change it without a code release.** Adjusting a retry policy, swapping a backend image digest, toggling a feature flag.\n3. **The value composes naturally with other related values.** A `:self-healing` block holds threshold + cooldown + backend allow-list, all tunable together.\n\nIf a value matches any of (1), (2), or (3) — it is configuration. It lives in an `.edn` resource (or a layered profile of them), not in a `def` and not in a hand-built map in `(defn create-foo ...)`.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — operational policy embedded in code; the per-backend table\n;; is the policy the operator wants to tune, not the function the\n;; agent calls.\n(defn watchdog [backend]\n  (let [overrides {:claude 120000}\n        threshold (get overrides backend 90000)]\n    (create-watchdog {:threshold-ms       threshold\n                      :check-interval-ms  5000\n                      :allowed-backends   [:claude :codex]})))\n\n;; GOOD — configuration is data, code is parametric\n;; resources/config/agent-stream-watchdog.edn\n{:agent/stream-gap-threshold-ms       90000\n :agent/check-interval-ms             5000\n :agent/allowed-failover-backends     [:claude :codex]\n :agent/per-backend-gap-thresholds    {:claude 120000}}\n\n;; src/agent/stream_watchdog.clj\n(defn- resolve-threshold [config backend]\n  (or (get-in config [:agent/per-backend-gap-thresholds backend])\n      (:agent/stream-gap-threshold-ms config)))\n\n(defn watchdog [config backend]\n  (create-watchdog (assoc config\n                          :resolved-threshold-ms\n                          (resolve-threshold config backend))))\n```\n\n## EDN structure conventions\n\n- **Namespaced keys** (`:agent/stream-gap-threshold-ms`, not `:threshold-ms`) so configs can be merged across components without collision.\n- **Units in the key name** (`-ms`, `-bytes`, `-percent`) — values shouldn't have to be guessed.\n- **One file per component or per domain** under `resources/config/`. A `default-user-config.edn` ships embedded defaults; per-deployment overrides layer on top via Aero or equivalent.\n- **No code execution at load time** — EDN reader literals only where the schema explicitly allows them. Reading config must not be Turing-complete.\n\n## Where named constants live (code) vs config values (EDN)\n\n| Belongs in code (`def`) | Belongs in EDN config |\n|---|---|\n| Algorithmic constants (golden ratio, max-redirects guard) | Anything an operator might tune |\n| Protocol-level values (event-type keywords, schema field names) | Per-environment thresholds, timeouts, retries |\n| Test-only values used in fixtures | Production defaults the team agrees on |\n| The **fallback default** when config is absent | The **active value** the system uses |\n\nPattern: code has a `default-X` named constant for \"what to do when config is silent\"; the EDN config carries the actual operational value:\n\n```clojure\n(def default-gap-threshold-ms\n  \"Hard-coded fallback when config is absent. The active value comes\n   from :agent/stream-gap-threshold-ms in resources/config/agent.edn.\"\n  90000)\n\n(defn resolve-threshold [config]\n  (get config :agent/stream-gap-threshold-ms default-gap-threshold-ms))\n```\n\n## Schema and validation\n\nEvery EDN config file has a Malli (or equivalent) schema. Validation runs at config load, NOT at every read. A bad config fails fast at startup with a clear error pointing at the offending key — not as a `NullPointerException` three layers deep at runtime.\n\n```clojure\n(def ConfigSchema\n  [:map\n   [:agent/stream-gap-threshold-ms      {:default 90000} pos-int?]\n   [:agent/check-interval-ms            {:default 5000}  pos-int?]\n   [:agent/per-backend-gap-thresholds   {:default {}}    [:map-of keyword? pos-int?]]\n   [:agent/allowed-failover-backends    {:default []}    [:vector keyword?]]])\n\n(defn load-config [path]\n  (-> (slurp path) edn/read-string (validate ConfigSchema)))\n```\n\nSee [validation-boundaries](./validation-boundaries.mdc) for the broader principle: validate at the boundary, trust inside.\n\n## Provider credentials and model routing\n\nLLM provider credentials and model/provider routing are operational\nconfiguration. Product code should not grow direct provider-key wrappers\nor one-off API calls for agent backends when a configurable CLI provider\nwrapper already owns that concern.\n\nDefault rule for agent/LLM backends:\n\n- Use OpenCode (the open-source CLI tool that centralizes LLM provider\n  auth, model routing, and agent configuration) as the common\n  provider/auth wrapper when adding a new agent backend path. OpenCode\n  owns provider credentials, model aliases, provider discovery, project\n  config, `.env` loading, MCP configuration, and per-agent\n  tool/permission profiles.\n- Product code may pass provider/model/profile selections as data\n  (`:backend`, `:model`, `:agent`, config path, attach URL), but should\n  not read provider-specific secrets such as `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or\n  `OPENAI_API_KEY` on the OpenCode path.\n- Direct provider SDK/HTTP integration is not acceptable for ordinary\n  miniforge agent runs. If a product needs a provider-specific adapter\n  for a non-agent surface, the adapter must be explicitly scoped as\n  non-agent infrastructure, the config schema must own every operational\n  value, and the PR must explain why OpenCode is not the right boundary.\n- Do not duplicate provider credential discovery across components.\n  There should be one place to ask for LLM-provider credentials for\n  ordinary agent runs; downstream callers select capabilities, not\n  secret-loading strategies.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. Every operational literal in code (timeouts, retry counts, thresholds, URLs, paths) — ask: would an operator want to change this without a code release? If yes → move to EDN.\n2. Reject PRs that add a feature flag, threshold, or backend list as a hard-coded `def`.\n3. When extracting to EDN, also add (or extend) the config's Malli schema.\n4. The code's `default-X` constant stays — it is the fallback if config is absent — but the operational value lives in EDN.\n5. Never use `eval`, `read-string` without binding `*read-eval* false`, or any other Turing-complete config-reading path.\n6. Reject new direct LLM provider-key wrappers for agent backends. The\n   supported provider API-key path for miniforge agent runs is OpenCode.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Hard-coded URLs / paths / backend image digests in `defn` bodies\n- Per-deployment values gated by `if` on environment name (`(if (= env :prod) 90000 5000)`) — that's config disguised as code\n- Hand-built option maps inside `(defn create-X ...)` for what should be a passed-in config\n- EDN files that contain Clojure forms (`#=(...)`) instead of pure data\n- Config files with no schema — every config file MUST have one\n- \"Magic numbers extracted to a `def`\" when the value should have been EDN\n- Configuration spread across multiple `def`s instead of a single namespaced EDN map",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/config-as-data,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "007",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (007): Configuration is data — operational values live in .edn files, not as embedded literals in code, and never as runtime constructions of the configuration shape"}
        #:rule{:title
               "CSS — design tokens, semantic class names, shallow selectors, accessible focus",
               :agent-behavior
               "CSS in any miniforge web surface SHOULD lean on design tokens,\ncomponent-purpose class names, and shallow selectors. No hard-coded\nproduct colours; no anti-accessible focus suppression.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: CSS — design tokens, semantic class names, shallow selectors, accessible focus"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.css" "**/*.scss"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# CSS standards\n\nCSS in any miniforge web surface SHOULD lean on design tokens,\ncomponent-purpose class names, and shallow selectors. No hard-coded\nproduct colours; no anti-accessible focus suppression.\n\n## CSS-001 — Use design tokens\n\nCSS MUST use shared custom properties for product colours, spacing,\ntypography, borders, shadows, and radii. Tokens live at `:root` (or a\nshared `tokens.css`) and are referenced via `var(--token-name)`.\n\n```css\n:root {\n  --color-bg: #0f172a;\n  --color-surface: #1e293b;\n  --color-accent: #14b8a6;\n  --color-accent-soft: #a7f3d0;\n  --space-1: 0.25rem;\n  --space-2: 0.5rem;\n  --space-4: 1rem;\n  --radius-md: 0.5rem;\n}\n```\n\n## CSS-002 — No hardcoded product colours outside tokens\n\n```css\n/* BAD */\n.button { background: #14b8a6; }\n\n/* GOOD */\n.button { background: var(--color-accent); }\n```\n\nThe only acceptable hex/RGB literals in CSS are inside the token\ndefinitions themselves. Anywhere else is a violation.\n\n## CSS-003 — Name classes by component purpose\n\n| Acceptable | Not acceptable |\n|---|---|\n| `.policy-card` | `.big-blue-box` |\n| `.workflow-run-list` | `.left-thing` |\n| `.evidence-panel` | `.new-style` |\n| `.repo-status-badge` | `.thing-2` |\n\nVisual-only names (`.red`, `.large`, `.bold`) are forbidden. They tie\nmarkup to current visual style and break the moment the design changes.\n\n## CSS-004 — Avoid deep selector chains\n\nSelectors SHOULD NOT exceed three levels.\n\n```css\n/* BAD */\n.main .content .panel .body .row button span {}\n\n/* GOOD */\n.policy-card__action {}\n```\n\nDeep chains are a smell that the markup needs better named hooks (see\nCSS-003) or that the rule is over-coupled to DOM structure.\n\n## CSS-005 — Use reusable layout utilities\n\nCommon layout patterns SHOULD be expressed as named utilities, not\nre-coded per component.\n\nRecommended utilities to factor out:\n\n- `.stack` — vertical rhythm with consistent gap\n- `.cluster` — horizontal flex with wrap and gap\n- `.grid` — CSS grid with sensible defaults\n- `.sidebar-layout` — main/aside split that collapses responsively\n- `.page-shell` — top-level page chrome\n\n## CSS-006 — Responsive layout required\n\nNew pages MUST be usable at:\n\n- 390px width (mobile) unless explicitly desktop-only\n- 1280px (laptop)\n- large desktop monitor widths (1920px+)\n\nDocument any \"desktop-only\" exception in the page's PR description.\n\n## CSS-007 — Visible focus states required\n\nInteractive elements MUST define a visible `:focus-visible` style.\n\n```css\n.button:focus-visible {\n  outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}\n```\n\n## CSS-008 — Do not hide focus without replacement\n\n```css\n/* BAD — hides focus globally with no replacement */\n*:focus { outline: none; }\n```\n\nFocus suppression is allowed only when paired with an equivalent visible\nfocus indicator on the same selector. Reject any rule that removes\nfocus styling without compensation.\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen writing or reviewing CSS:\n1. Grep for hex/RGB/HSL literals outside the tokens file — each is a\n   CSS-002 violation.\n2. Count selector depth on any new rule; >3 levels gets refactored.\n3. Verify every interactive element class has a `:focus-visible` partner.\n4. Check for `outline: 0`, `outline: none`, `:focus { outline:` —\n   reject unless replaced.",
               :id :std/css,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "260",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (260): CSS — design tokens, semantic class names, shallow selectors, accessible focus"}
        #:rule{:title "DateVer versioning convention",
               :remediation
               {:strategy :mechanical,
                :replacement "$1.$2.$3.0",
                :auto-fixable-default false},
               :agent-behavior
               "- When generating versions, use `(version)` from build.clj\n- Never use SemVer (X.Y.Z) format\n- In changelogs, use DateVer headings: `## 2026.01.18.1`",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message "Standard: DateVer versioning convention"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["build.clj"
                 "**/version.edn"
                 ".github/workflows/*.yml"
                 ".github/workflows/*.yaml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# DateVer Versioning (ALWAYS)\n\nUse **DateVer** instead of SemVer for all version numbers.\n\n## Format\n\n```zsh\nYYYY.MM.DD.N\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- `YYYY` — four-digit year\n- `MM` — two-digit month (zero-padded)\n- `DD` — two-digit day (zero-padded)\n- `N` — build number for the day (1-indexed)\n\n## Examples\n\n| Version | Meaning |\n|---------|---------|\n| `2026.01.18.1` | First build on January 18, 2026 |\n| `2026.01.18.5` | Fifth build on January 18, 2026 |\n| `2026.03.01.1` | First build on March 1, 2026 |\n\n## Rationale\n\n- **Chronologically sortable**: Versions naturally sort by time\n- **No arbitrary major/minor**: Avoids bikeshedding about \"breaking changes\"\n- **Self-documenting**: Version tells you when it was built\n- **Simple**: One rule—today's date plus build count\n\n## Implementation\n\n```clojure\n;; In build.clj\n(defn version []\n  (let [now   (java.time.LocalDate/now)\n        year  (.getYear now)\n        month (.getMonthValue now)\n        day   (.getDayOfMonth now)\n        n     (today-commit-count)]\n    (format \"%d.%02d.%02d.%d\" year month day n)))\n```\n\n## Git Tags\n\nTag releases as `v{version}`:\n\n```bash\ngit tag v2026.01.18.1\ngit push origin v2026.01.18.1\n```\n\n## Comparison to SemVer\n\n| SemVer | DateVer | Notes |\n|--------|---------|-------|\n| 1.0.0 | 2026.01.15.1 | Initial release |\n| 1.0.1 | 2026.01.15.2 | Bug fix same day |\n| 1.1.0 | 2026.01.20.1 | Feature add |\n| 2.0.0 | 2026.03.01.1 | \"Breaking change\" (no special handling) |\n\nBreaking changes are communicated via changelog and documentation, not version numbers.\n\n## Agent Behavior\n\n- When generating versions, use `(version)` from build.clj\n- Never use SemVer (X.Y.Z) format\n- In changelogs, use DateVer headings: `## 2026.01.18.1`",
               :id :std/datever,
               :detection
               {:type :content-scan,
                :pattern "\\b(\\d+)\\.(\\d+)\\.(\\d+)\\b(?!\\.\\d)",
                :mode :positive},
               :category "730",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (730): DateVer versioning convention"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Keep source documentation to the current contract when writing or reviewing any file, so it stays scarce and high-fidelity — per-surface length limits, schemas/fields document contracts not history, narrative → external docs, examples → tests",
               :agent-behavior
               "**Source files describe the current contract, not the story of how it came to exist.** Documentation earns its place only where it states a durable contract — a boundary, invariant, or data-model semantic — that naming, decomposition, a schema, or a test cannot carry. Default to less. When you reach for prose, first try to make the code carry it.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Keep source documentation to the current contract when writing or reviewing any file, so it stays scarce and high-fidelity — per-surface length limits, schemas/fields document contracts not history, narrative → external docs, examples → tests"},
               :applies-to {:phases #{:review :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Documentation Discipline (ALWAYS)\n\n**Source files describe the current contract, not the story of how it came to exist.** Documentation earns its place only where it states a durable contract — a boundary, invariant, or data-model semantic — that naming, decomposition, a schema, or a test cannot carry. Default to less. When you reach for prose, first try to make the code carry it.\n\nThis is the **how-much-and-where** complement to `foundations/self-documenting-code` (009), which governs comment *quality* (WHY, never WHAT). 009 decides whether a comment earns its place; this rule decides how long documentation may be and where each kind belongs.\n\n## Length limits\n\nExceed these only for a stated boundary-contract reason. Longer material belongs outside the source file (see the placement table).\n\n| Surface | Limit |\n|---|---|\n| Namespace docstring | 1–5 lines |\n| Schema docstring | 1–3 sentences |\n| Field description | one sentence |\n| Ordinary `defn` docstring | **omitted by default** |\n| Inline comment | one line |\n\nMost `defn` forms carry no docstring: the name, args, schema, and tests say it. A docstring is for a real boundary — a public/interface fn, a protocol or lifecycle entry point, or a fn whose nil/empty/ordering/failure/determinism behavior is load-bearing (the `009` boundary-vs-impl rule). Even then it states the obligation, never the implementation.\n\n## Schemas are contracts — document them as such\n\nA schema docstring states what the concept is and what non-obvious fields, optional/absent values, and invariants mean — what producers and consumers may rely on. It does not carry history, prior designs, future plans, or downstream pipeline behavior.\n\n```clojure\n;; GOOD — current contract, one line\n(def SkillTag\n  \"Canonical :tech/* technology keyword. Weighted evidence, not a hard gate.\"\n  [:and keyword?\n   [:fn {:error/message \":requirement/skills entries must be :tech/* keywords\"}\n    (fn [k] (= \"tech\" (namespace k)))]])\n\n;; BAD — mixes contract with rationale and downstream architecture\n(def SkillTag\n  \"A skill from the NER vocabulary. Exists because the original character\n   taxonomy did not model skills, and the assembler later compiles this into\n   weighted clauses for the skills dimension...\"\n  ...)\n```\n\nDocument a **field** only when the type does not carry the meaning — and put it in the field's `:description`, not a comment above it. Obvious fields get nothing.\n\n```clojure\n;; GOOD — semantics the type can't show, on the field\n[:jd/tenure-gates\n {:optional true :description \"Absent means no hard tenure floor, not unknown.\"}\n [:vector TenureGate]]\n\n;; BAD — a comment restating the type\n;; The title of the job.\n[:jd/title :string]\n```\n\n## Where each kind of documentation lives\n\nThe source file holds the current contract. Everything else has a home outside it.\n\n| Content | Lives in |\n|---|---|\n| Design rationale, \"why this way\" | an ADR (`docs/adr/`) |\n| Architecture / pipeline narrative | architecture docs (`docs/`) |\n| Change / migration history | the PR doc (`workflows/pr-documentation`, 721) or changelog |\n| Examples | tests, fixtures, generated docs |\n| How you arrived at the code | the PR description, never the source |\n\nIf a comment would start with *previously*, *originally*, *for now*, or *later phase*, it belongs in one of those, not the code.\n\n## Prefer executable examples\n\nWhen an example would clarify a schema or data model, put it in a test or fixture, where it validates against the schema and cannot rot. Reserve inline examples for a namespace that is itself a small public API reference.\n\n## Reject in review\n\n- A docstring or comment that restates the name, type, or body.\n- A `defn` docstring on ordinary internal code (no boundary contract).\n- History, rationale, or roadmap in a schema or namespace docstring (`previously`, `Wave 6 adds`, `TODO later`).\n- An architecture essay in a namespace docstring; a field comment above an obvious field.\n- Documentation that must change whenever the adjacent line changes.\n\n## When touching over-documented code\n\nLeave it cleaner than found — shorten schema docs to the current contract, drop docstrings that only narrate, move still-useful rationale to an ADR or the PR doc. Preserve license/copyright headers (`project/header-copyright` (810)) and `foundations/named-constants` (006) docstrings verbatim. Do not churn unrelated files solely to trim prose.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/documentation-discipline,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "600",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (600): Keep source documentation to the current contract when writing or reviewing any file, so it stays scarce and high-fidelity — per-surface length limits, schemas/fields document contracts not history, narrative → external docs, examples → tests"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Exceptions are data — return anomalies in normal flows; throw only at absolute boundaries",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. **Default to anomaly returns.** If a function in a non-boundary namespace can fail, return an anomaly map.\n2. **Reach for `(anomaly/anomaly type message data)`**, not `(throw (ex-info ...))`.\n3. **At boundaries, use `boundary/execute-with-exception-handling`** (or the local equivalent) to wrap calls into libraries that throw. Convert their exceptions into anomaly steps.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Exception thrown in normal control flow. Return the anomaly as a value and let callers branch on success/failure; throw only at an absolute boundary."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Exceptions Are Data, Not Control Flow (ALWAYS)\n\nOperations that can fail return an **anomaly** as data, not a thrown exception. Exceptions are caught only at absolute process boundaries (HTTP/CLI/MCP/message-consumer entry points) and *converted* into anomaly-shaped data that participates in the composable flow.\n\nCompanion rule to result-handling (003) and validation-boundaries (004). Result-handling defines the result-map shape and predicates. Validation-boundaries defines *where* validation occurs. This rule defines *how errors propagate* — as data, not stack-unwinding.\n\n## The rule\n\nIn a non-boundary namespace, `throw` is a code smell. Two narrow exemptions:\n\n1. **Absolute process boundaries** — see \"Boundary namespace patterns\" below.\n2. **Programmer-error guards** — `IllegalArgumentException` for an exhaustive case match, assertions on impossible-by-construction conditions inside pure helpers.\n\nEverywhere else: return an anomaly.\n\n## Why\n\nComposable AI/agent pipelines need to carry error context alongside successful output. Three problems with exceptions as the default failure path:\n\n- **They lose data.** The stack unwinds past the structured information you wanted to return. Whatever the failing function knew about the failure is gone unless it stuffs it into `ex-info` data, and even then it travels via a side channel rather than the normal return path.\n- **They break parallel composition.** A throw in one branch of a parallel fan-out poisons the whole computation. Anomalies returned as data let the orchestrator inspect each result.\n- **They don't carry provenance.** A response-chain accumulates per-operation anomalies in order, with each step's `:operation` keyword. A thrown exception gives you a stack trace and nothing about what came before it.\n\nData-first errors fold into the same response-chain / evidence-bundle machinery that carries success. That is the difference between \"a RAG toy\" and \"a consumer product that can answer 'why?'.\"\n\n## Anomaly shape\n\nThe canonical anomaly map. The shared implementation is the `ai.miniforge.anomaly` component in miniforge OSS (Apache 2); when miniforge is unavailable, repos may use a local helper that produces the same shape.\n\n```clojure\n{:anomaly/type    :keyword     ;; one of the standard types below\n :anomaly/message \"string\"     ;; human-readable\n :anomaly/data    {...}        ;; structured context\n :anomaly/at      #inst \"...\"} ;; instant of construction\n```\n\nStandard type vocabulary (mirrors cognitect anomalies):\n\n```clojure\n#{:not-found\n  :invalid-input\n  :unauthorized\n  :fault\n  :unavailable\n  :conflict\n  :timeout\n  :unsupported\n  :fatal}\n```\n\nConstruct via `(anomaly/anomaly type message data)`. Test via `(anomaly/anomaly? x)`. Validate via the exported `Anomaly` malli schema.\n\n## Boundary namespace patterns\n\n`throw` and `ex-info` are appropriate in these namespace shapes:\n\n| Pattern | Examples | Why |\n|---------|----------|-----|\n| `*.cli.*` / `*-main` | `bases/cli/...`, `*-main` namespaces | Top-level CLI entry; throw produces a clean exit status |\n| `*.boundary.*` | `ai.miniforge.boundary.*` | Designated exception → anomaly converter component |\n| `*.http.*` / `*.web.*` | HTTP request handlers | The HTTP machinery expects exceptions to render error responses |\n| `*.mcp.*` | MCP tool entry | MCP protocol surfaces errors as throws |\n| `*.consumer.*` / `*.listener.*` | Message-consumer entry points | The consumer framework converts throws to retries / DLQ |\n| Fns named `execute-with-exception-handling` (or similar) | Anywhere | Designated catch-and-convert helpers |\n\nAnything *outside* those patterns should return anomalies.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Violation — throwing inside non-boundary code\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — components/widget/src/ai/miniforge/widget/core.clj\n(defn fetch-widget\n  [store id]\n  (or (get-widget store id)\n      (throw (ex-info \"Widget not found\" {:id id}))))\n```\n\nThree problems: caller has to wrap every call in try/catch; the anomaly's `:type` and `:data` are buried in `ex-data`; parallel `(map fetch-widget …)` fan-outs break on the first miss.\n\n### Correction — return an anomaly\n\n```clojure\n;; GOOD — components/widget/src/ai/miniforge/widget/core.clj\n(ns ai.miniforge.widget.core\n  (:require [ai.miniforge.anomaly.interface :as anomaly]))\n\n(defn fetch-widget\n  [store id]\n  (if-let [w (get-widget store id)]\n    w\n    (anomaly/anomaly :not-found\n                     \"Widget not found\"\n                     {:id id})))\n```\n\nCaller inspects with `anomaly/anomaly?`; parallel calls collect both successes and failures cleanly.\n\n### Acceptable — boundary catches and converts\n\n```clojure\n;; GOOD — bases/cli/src/ai/miniforge/cli/main.clj\n(ns ai.miniforge.cli.main\n  (:require [ai.miniforge.boundary.interface :as boundary]\n            [ai.miniforge.widget.interface :as widget]))\n\n(defn -main\n  [& args]\n  (let [chain (boundary/execute-with-exception-handling\n                :widget-fetch\n                (response-chain/create-chain :widget-cli)\n                :widget/fetch\n                widget/fetch-widget store id)]\n    (if (response-chain/succeeded? chain)\n      (System/exit 0)\n      (do (print-anomaly (response-chain/last-anomaly chain))\n          (System/exit 1)))))\n```\n\nBoundary helper catches throws (from underlying libraries that *do* throw), converts to anomaly steps in the chain, returns the chain as data.\n\n### Acceptable — programmer-error guard\n\n```clojure\n;; GOOD — exhaustive case match on an enum-like value\n(case kind\n  :collection ...\n  :conversation ...\n  :event ...\n  ;; default: unknown kind is a programmer error, not a runtime error\n  (throw (IllegalArgumentException.\n          (str \"Unknown kind: \" kind))))\n```\n\nThis isn't a runtime failure to be recovered from — it's \"I added a new variant and forgot to handle it here.\" Throw is appropriate.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing component code:\n\n1. **Default to anomaly returns.** If a function in a non-boundary namespace can fail, return an anomaly map.\n2. **Reach for `(anomaly/anomaly type message data)`**, not `(throw (ex-info ...))`.\n3. **At boundaries, use `boundary/execute-with-exception-handling`** (or the local equivalent) to wrap calls into libraries that throw. Convert their exceptions into anomaly steps.\n4. **When reviewing PRs**, flag any `throw` or `ex-info` in non-boundary code paths. Suggest the anomaly-return rewrite. Programmer-error guards (exhaustive case matches, impossible-by-construction assertions) get a pass.\n5. **Don't rewrite library code that throws.** That's outside our control. Catch at the boundary.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- `(throw (ex-info \"...\" {...}))` inside `core.clj` of a Polylith component\n- `try/catch` blocks inside non-boundary code paths used to convert thrown exceptions into return values — instead, fix the called function to return an anomaly directly\n- Mixed signaling: a function that *sometimes* returns `nil`, *sometimes* returns an anomaly, *sometimes* throws — pick one (anomaly)\n- \"Throwing for control flow\" — using `throw`/`try` to exit a deep loop or short-circuit a computation. Use proper return paths.\n- Catching `Throwable` deep inside business logic — only boundary helpers should catch, and they catch deliberately\n\n## Linter design\n\nThis rule prescribes the linter shape; the implementation will land in miniforge OSS `bb review` once the canonical anomaly component is in place. The design:\n\nScan `components/*/src/**/*.clj` and `bases/*/src/**/*.clj`. For each `(throw ...)`, `(ex-info ...)`, `IllegalArgumentException.`, `RuntimeException.`, `(throw+ ...)` (slingshot):\n\n1. Extract the namespace of the file containing the form.\n2. If the namespace matches one of the **boundary patterns** above → skip.\n3. If the form is inside a `case`/`condp` default branch and the message contains \"unknown\" or \"unsupported\" → flag as `:fatal-only` (warning-only, can be dismissed).\n4. Otherwise → emit a `:warning` with file:line:col, the offending form, and the suggested anomaly-return rewrite (link to the example in this rule).\n\nOutput format follows the existing `bb review` convention. Severity defaults to `:warning`, not `:error` — there are legitimate exceptions; we want to surface, not hard-block. Promotion to `:error` happens per-repo after that repo's cleanup completes.\n\n## Related rules\n\n- `foundations/result-handling.mdc` (003) — the success/failure result-map shape and predicates\n- `foundations/validation-boundaries.mdc` (004) — *where* validation occurs (interface boundaries + external entry)\n- `foundations/stratified-design.mdc` (001) — layer discipline; this rule's \"boundary\" concept depends on layer hygiene\n\n## Reference component\n\nThe canonical implementation of the anomaly shape and constructor lives in miniforge OSS as the `ai.miniforge.anomaly` Polylith component (Apache 2). It exposes `anomaly`, `anomaly?`, the `Anomaly` malli schema, and the `anomaly-types` set. miniforge-family Clojure repos consume it via the existing vendoring relationship with miniforge.\n\nRepos that do not vendor miniforge may implement the same shape locally; the rule applies regardless of where the constructor lives, because the rule is about the discipline (return data, don't throw) rather than the specific namespace.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/exceptions-as-data,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "005",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (005): Exceptions are data — return anomalies in normal flows; throw only at absolute boundaries"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Fulcro standards — queries, idents, normalisation, mutations, loads, domain naming",
               :agent-behavior
               "Fulcro is the default for stateful product UI in any miniforge web\nsurface that opts into Fulcro per `frameworks/web-architecture-mode.mdc`\nWEB-002. This rule extracts the load-bearing patterns from the\n*Fulcro Developer's Guide* (https://book.fulcrologic.com/) into\nenforceable repository rules. Every rule here traces to the Book;\ncite the section in PR review when the rule is non-obvious.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Fulcro standards — queries, idents, normalisation, mutations, loads, domain naming"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.cljs" "**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Fulcro standards\n\nFulcro is the default for stateful product UI in any miniforge web\nsurface that opts into Fulcro per `frameworks/web-architecture-mode.mdc`\nWEB-002. This rule extracts the load-bearing patterns from the\n*Fulcro Developer's Guide* (https://book.fulcrologic.com/) into\nenforceable repository rules. Every rule here traces to the Book;\ncite the section in PR review when the rule is non-obvious.\n\n## When Fulcro applies\n\nFulcro is for product UI involving durable entities, graph\nrelationships, workflow state, or policy / evidence interactions\n(per WEB-002).\n\n### FUL-001 — Use Fulcro for entity/workflow product UI\n\nIf a page renders durable entities (repositories, workflow runs, policy\npacks, evidence bundles, agents, approvals, etc.) or transitions\nworkflow state, it SHOULD be a Fulcro component tree. Static\ninformational pages stay server-rendered.\n\n## Component basics — queries, idents, initial state\n\nThe four-tuple `query` + `ident` + `initial-state` + `render` is the\nload-bearing contract. Get this right and the rest of the framework\nbehaves; get it wrong and you fight normalisation forever.\n\n### FUL-002 — Components must declare queries\n\nComponents that render application data MUST declare an explicit\n`:query`. Do not pass large opaque maps through component trees when\nthe component can declare its own data requirements.\n\n### FUL-003 — Use `comp/get-query` for joins\n\nParent component queries MUST use `comp/get-query` when joining to\nchild components.\n\n```clojure\n(defsc WorkflowList [_ {:list/keys [runs]}]\n  {:query [{:list/runs (comp/get-query WorkflowRun)}]\n   :ident :list/id\n   :initial-state {:list/id :workflow-list :list/runs []}}\n  ...)\n```\n\n`get-query` carries the metadata Fulcro needs for normalisation;\nhand-writing a child query inline breaks it.\n\n### FUL-004 — Persistent entities must have idents\n\nComponents representing persistent entities MUST define a stable\n`:ident`. Idents are the address into the normalised client DB.\n\nTypical persistent entity examples in a miniforge product UI:\n\n- repository / project / workspace\n- workflow run / step\n- policy pack / rule\n- evidence bundle / item\n- agent / job / task\n- approval / review\n- user / principal\n\nUI-only \"presence\" components (modals, panels, banners) do not need\nidents.\n\n### FUL-005 — Initial state must match UI shape\n\nInitial state SHOULD match the component tree shape and normalise\ncleanly. Root initial state SHOULD compose child initial state via\n`comp/get-initial-state`, not duplicate it manually.\n\n## Naming and key conventions\n\n### FUL-014 — Prefer domain-namespaced keywords\n\nApplication keys MUST be namespaced by domain.\n\n```clojure\n;; GOOD\n:repo/id :repo/name\n:workflow.run/id :workflow.run/status\n:policy.pack/id :evidence.bundle/id\n\n;; BAD — generic, ambiguous, collision-prone\n:id :name :status\n```\n\n### FUL-015 — UI-only state uses `:ui/*`\n\nUI-only state MUST use the `:ui/` namespace (or an equivalent explicit\nUI namespace).\n\n```clojure\n:ui/expanded?     :ui/selected-tab\n:ui/filter-text   :ui/modal-open?\n```\n\n### FUL-016 — Do not persist UI state accidentally\n\nServer writes MUST NOT persist `:ui/*` state unless explicitly\nintended. Strip `:ui/*` keys from mutation payloads before remote\nreturn.\n\n### FUL-029 — Do not leak database schema directly into UI\n\nFulcro entity keys SHOULD represent product / domain concepts, not raw\nstorage implementation details. `:repo/integration-status` is good;\n`:db/repos.integration_status_enum_value` is not.\n\n### FUL-035 — Keep naming consistent across client and server\n\nEntity names, ids, resolver names, and mutation names SHOULD use a\nshared domain vocabulary across CLJS, CLJC, and CLJ.\n\nEach consuming repo SHOULD declare its canonical domain nouns once (in\na shared `domain.cljc` or equivalent) and reference them across CLJS,\nCLJC, and CLJ namespaces. Common miniforge domain shapes include\nrepository, workflow run / step, policy pack / check, evidence bundle,\napproval, agent, integration.\n\n## Normalisation discipline\n\n### FUL-006 — Normalize server data\n\nServer responses intended for Fulcro app state SHOULD be normalisable\nentity graphs (i.e. each entity carries its ident-key, references are\nident-shaped). Avoid returning UI-specific denormalised blobs for\ndurable product state.\n\n### FUL-019 — Treat the client DB as a normalised database\n\nAfter normalisation, do not reason about state as a nested\ncomponent-owned object tree. Entity updates target normalised table\nentries by ident.\n\n### FUL-020 — Do not duplicate entity facts across tables\n\nA durable fact about an entity SHOULD have one canonical location. If a\nvalue appears in multiple UI contexts, render it from the same\nnormalised entity — do not copy.\n\n### FUL-018 — Use unions for polymorphic graph edges\n\nWhen a graph edge can point to different entity types, use Fulcro\nunion queries.\n\nCommon candidates: evidence-item variants, policy-result variants,\nworkflow-step variants, activity-feed events.\n\n## Mutations\n\n### FUL-007 — Mutations are the only state-transition boundary\n\nApplication state changes MUST flow through Fulcro mutations (or\napproved Fulcro mechanisms — load merges, refresh, deferred routing).\nDo NOT mutate app state ad hoc from rendering code, callbacks, or\nforeign JS wrappers.\n\n### FUL-008 — Mutations must be intent-named\n\nMutation names SHOULD describe domain intent, not implementation\ndetail.\n\n| Good | Bad |\n|---|---|\n| `workflow/start!` | `set-flag!` |\n| `policy-pack/activate!` | `update-map!` |\n| `evidence/mark-reviewed!` | `do-click!` |\n| `repo.integration/remove!` | `handle-button!` |\n\n### FUL-009 — Separate local and remote mutation effects\n\nMutations SHOULD clearly separate local optimistic effects from remote\neffects. A mutation with remote effects MUST define its expected\nfailure behaviour (rollback, error message, retry policy).\n\n### FUL-010 — Use transactions for user-driven operations\n\nUser-driven operations SHOULD be modelled as Fulcro transactions\n(`comp/transact!`). The UI submits intent; the mutation decides local\nand remote effects.\n\n### FUL-021 — Use targeted refresh\n\nMutations SHOULD refresh only the affected UI / data where practical.\nAvoid full app refreshes for changes scoped to a small entity set.\n\n### FUL-023 — Destructive mutations require domain-level confirmation\n\nA Fulcro mutation that performs a destructive remote effect MUST\nrequire prior UI confirmation appropriate to blast radius (per\n`languages/javascript.mdc` JS-007 and `frameworks/browser-security.mdc`\nBSEC-004). The confirmation belongs in the UI flow that *triggers* the\nmutation, not inside the mutation body.\n\n## Loads and remote interaction\n\n### FUL-012 — Use loads for remote reads\n\nRemote data reads SHOULD use Fulcro load mechanisms (`df/load!` or\nequivalent) rather than arbitrary `fetch` calls embedded in components.\n\n### FUL-013 — Loading and error state must be modelled\n\nRemote interactions MUST expose loading and error state in the UI\n(typically via load markers or per-entity status fields). Hidden\nspinners and silent failures are violations.\n\nError state SHOULD include enough diagnostic information to support\nevidence / debug workflows (request id, timestamp, error class,\nretryability hint).\n\n### FUL-028 — Server API should match graph needs\n\nThe server data API SHOULD support Fulcro graph reads and mutations\ncleanly. Pathom SHOULD be considered for graph-oriented server\nreads / resolvers. The internal Pathom / EQL surface is API class 1\n(per `frameworks/api-surface-classes.mdc`); the existing flat JSON\nendpoints stay alive as class-2 surfaces alongside it.\n\n## Render discipline\n\n### FUL-011 — No side effects in render\n\nComponent render bodies MUST NOT perform side effects.\n\nForbidden in render:\n- network calls\n- app state mutation\n- timers\n- subscriptions\n- DOM mutation outside React / Fulcro integration points\n\nMove side effects to mutation `:action`s, lifecycle hooks, or load\ncalls triggered by route / mutation events.\n\n### FUL-017 — Component queries are minimal and local\n\nEach component's query SHOULD request only the data it renders or\npasses to declared children. Avoid broad root queries that bypass\ncomponent composition.\n\n### FUL-024 — Components small but not anaemic\n\nA component SHOULD own:\n\n- its query\n- its ident if entity-backed\n- its initial state if applicable\n- its rendering\n- local UI event submission\n\nSplitting components so aggressively that query ownership becomes\nunclear is its own anti-pattern.\n\n### FUL-025 — Business rules out of CSS / DOM state\n\nFulcro logic MUST NOT infer domain truth from CSS classes, DOM\nattributes, or visual state. The data model is the source of truth;\nthe DOM reflects the data model.\n\n### FUL-033 — Avoid huge root components\n\nRoot components SHOULD compose major screen areas and routing, not\ncontain all product rendering logic.\n\n### FUL-034 — Prefer declarative screen composition\n\nScreens SHOULD be composed from queried components rather than\nmanually extracting arbitrary nested data in root-level rendering.\n\n## Workflow / domain modelling\n\n### FUL-022 — Model workflow state explicitly\n\nWorkflow state in the application MUST be explicit in the data model.\nTypical shape:\n\n- `:workflow.run/status`\n- `:workflow.run/current-step`\n- `:workflow.step/status`\n- `:workflow.step/error`\n- `:workflow.step/retryable?`\n\nOperational truth must NOT be derived only from incidental UI flags.\n\n### FUL-027 — Prefer CLJC for shared domain logic\n\nValidation, normalisation helpers, domain predicates, and\npolicy-adjacent logic SHOULD live in CLJC when shared by client and\nserver. Keeps the domain vocabulary single-sourced (FUL-035).\n\n### FUL-030 — Route state is product state when it controls workflow\n\nRoute parameters that control workflow / evidence / policy context\nSHOULD be treated as part of application state, normalised, and\ntested.\n\n## Interop and tooling\n\n### FUL-026 — JS / React interop is isolated\n\nJavaScript / React interop MUST be wrapped in small boundary\ncomponents. Interop components SHOULD expose Fulcro-friendly props\nand events; the rest of the system MUST NOT see the foreign API.\n\n### FUL-031 — Use Fulcro Inspect in development\n\nMiniforge Fulcro applications SHOULD support Fulcro Inspect during\ndevelopment. The build should not strip the integration in dev mode.\n\n### FUL-032 — Tests cover mutations and graph updates\n\nTests SHOULD verify:\n\n- mutation state transitions\n- normalised DB updates\n- remote mutation behaviour\n- load result normalisation\n- critical route transitions\n- failure states\n\nComponent render tests are useful but secondary; the data-model layer\nis where regressions hurt most.\n\n## Severity guidance\n\n| Severity | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| **blocker** | side effects in render (FUL-011); state changes outside mutations (FUL-007); destructive mutation without confirmation (FUL-023); domain truth inferred from DOM (FUL-025) |\n| **major** | persistent entity without ident (FUL-004); duplicated entity facts across tables (FUL-020); generic-keyed durable state (FUL-014); inline `fetch` instead of load (FUL-012) |\n| **minor** | non-intent-named mutation (FUL-008); broad root query (FUL-017); huge root component (FUL-033) |\n| **advisory** | could use a union (FUL-018); could move helper to CLJC (FUL-027) |\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen writing or reviewing Fulcro code:\n1. Open the four-tuple check on every `defsc` rendering data — `:query`,\n   `:ident` (if entity), `:initial-state` (if joined from root),\n   render. Missing pieces are the most common agent failure mode.\n2. For every mutation, confirm: intent-named (FUL-008), local vs\n   remote effects separated (FUL-009), destructive ones gated\n   (FUL-023).\n3. Grep for `fetch(`, `.then(`, `XMLHttpRequest` — each is a likely\n   FUL-012 violation.\n4. Grep for `:id`, `:name`, `:status` as bare keys in app-state\n   contexts — likely FUL-014 violations.\n5. When in doubt about a Fulcro idiom, cite the *Fulcro Developer's\n   Guide* section by number in the PR comment, not framework folklore.",
               :id :std/fulcro,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "340",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (340): Fulcro standards — queries, idents, normalisation, mutations, loads, domain naming"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Fulcro RAD — optional, for conventional CRUD/forms/reports; never forced onto bespoke workflow UI",
               :agent-behavior
               "Fulcro RAD (Rapid Application Development; see\nhttps://book.fulcrologic.com/RAD.html) is an *attribute-driven* layer\non top of Fulcro for CRUD-shaped surfaces. RAD is **optional** —\nuseful for conventional admin forms and reports, harmful when forced\nonto bespoke workflow surfaces.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Fulcro RAD — optional, for conventional CRUD/forms/reports; never forced onto bespoke workflow UI"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.cljs" "**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Fulcro RAD standards\n\nFulcro RAD (Rapid Application Development; see\nhttps://book.fulcrologic.com/RAD.html) is an *attribute-driven* layer\non top of Fulcro for CRUD-shaped surfaces. RAD is **optional** —\nuseful for conventional admin forms and reports, harmful when forced\nonto bespoke workflow surfaces.\n\nThis rule sits alongside `frameworks/fulcro.mdc` (which always\napplies to CLJS / CLJC). RAD adds rules; it does not replace any\nFulcro rule.\n\n## RAD-001 — RAD is optional\n\nFulcro RAD MAY be used where it reduces boilerplate without hiding\nimportant product behaviour. RAD is never required; a hand-written\nFulcro screen is always a valid choice.\n\n## RAD-002 — Use RAD for conventional forms and reports\n\nRAD SHOULD be considered for:\n\n- CRUD-like admin forms\n- tabular reports\n- attribute-driven forms\n- conventional entity management surfaces (list / detail / edit)\n\nThese shapes have well-defined RAD generators that pay back the\nlearning cost.\n\n## RAD-003 — Do not force RAD onto bespoke workflow UIs\n\nRAD SHOULD NOT be forced onto highly custom workflow-control surfaces\nwhere explicit Fulcro components are clearer.\n\nExamples that belong in bespoke Fulcro, not RAD:\n\n- live workflow execution graph\n- PR train visualisation\n- evidence bundle diff / review\n- policy failure triage\n- agent orchestration console\n- recommendation + control panel\n\nWhen the surface is more bespoke than CRUD, the RAD ergonomics fight\nthe design rather than helping.\n\n## RAD-004 — Attribute definitions are domain contracts\n\nRAD attributes, when used, SHOULD be treated as domain model contracts\nand reviewed as carefully as a database schema migration. Renaming or\nre-typing an attribute is a breaking change.\n\nAttribute names follow the same domain-vocabulary rule as\n`frameworks/fulcro.mdc` FUL-035. RAD attribute namespaces match the\nFulcro entity namespaces (`:repo/...`, `:workflow.run/...`, etc.).\n\n## RAD-005 — Escape hatch required\n\nAny RAD usage MUST preserve the ability to drop to custom Fulcro UI\nwhere product behaviour requires it. A surface that is \"100% RAD with\nno escape\" is a code smell — when the requirements move beyond RAD's\ngenerators, you cannot incrementally take over rendering or mutation.\n\nConcretely: each RAD-generated screen SHOULD be wrapped or composed\nsuch that a hand-written Fulcro replacement can be slotted in without\nrewriting the surrounding routing and state.\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen asked to add a CRUD-shaped surface:\n1. Classify the surface against RAD-002 / RAD-003. If bespoke, use\n   plain Fulcro per `frameworks/fulcro.mdc`.\n2. If RAD is the right fit, declare the attribute set in a\n   shared CLJC namespace (per FUL-027) so the contract is single-sourced.\n3. Confirm the escape hatch (RAD-005) before merging — name the\n   replacement strategy in the PR description if the surface looks\n   likely to outgrow RAD.",
               :id :std/fulcro-rad,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "341",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (341): Fulcro RAD — optional, for conventional CRUD/forms/reports; never forced onto bespoke workflow UI"}
        #:rule{:title "Git branch management",
               :agent-behavior
               "**CRITICAL**: **ALWAYS create a new session unique git worktree for your session.\n**CRITICAL**: **ALWAYS create new branches from `main` or `master` (the primary branch) unless explicitly requested otherwise.**",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message "Standard: Git branch management"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Git Branch & PR Management (ALWAYS)\n\n## Branch Creation Rules\n\n**CRITICAL**: **ALWAYS create a new session unique git worktree for your session.\n**CRITICAL**: **ALWAYS create new branches from `main` or `master` (the primary branch) unless explicitly requested otherwise.**\n\n### Pre-Branch Checklist\n\nBefore running `git checkout -b <new-branch>`:\n\n1. **ALWAYS** checkout the primary branch first: `git checkout main` (or `master`)\n2. **ALWAYS** pull latest changes: `git pull origin main` (or `master`)\n3. **VERIFY** you're on the primary branch: `git branch --show-current`\n4. **VERIFY** branch is clean: `git status` (should show \"nothing to commit\")\n5. **ONLY THEN** create the new branch: `git checkout -b <new-branch>`\n\n**Never assume you're on main - always explicitly checkout main first.**\n\n### Never Branch from Feature Branches\n\n❌ **BAD**: Creating a feature branch from another feature branch\n\n```bash\n# On migrate/feature-a\ngit checkout -b migrate/feature-b  # ❌ Wrong - carries feature-a changes\n```\n\n✅ **GOOD**: Always branch from main\n\n```bash\ngit checkout main           # ALWAYS do this first\ngit pull origin main        # ALWAYS pull latest\ngit checkout -b migrate/feature-b  # ✅ Correct - clean start\n```\n\n### Exception: Dependent PRs\n\n**Only branch from another feature branch when:**\n\n1. User **explicitly** requests: \"create this branch from <branch-name>\"\n2. User **explicitly** confirms: \"yes, branch from that branch\"\n3. There's a documented dependency chain that requires it\n4. The dependency is clearly stated in the PR description\n\n**Default behavior**: Always branch from main unless user explicitly says otherwise.\n\n## PR Scope Rules\n\n### Single Purpose PRs\n\nEach PR should have **one focused purpose**:\n\n- ✅ Migrate security groups batch 1\n- ✅ Migrate IAM policies for ElasticSearch\n- ❌ Migrate security groups + DynamoDB + IAM roles (mixed concerns)\n\n### Verify PR Contents Before Pushing\n\nBefore pushing a branch, run:\n\n```bash\ngit log origin/main..HEAD --oneline\ngit diff origin/main..HEAD --stat\n```\n\nThis shows what will be in the PR to catch unintended changes.\n\n## Agent Behavior (Cursor)\n\n### Before Creating Any Branch\n\n1. **ALWAYS** checkout main first: `git checkout main` (or `master`)\n2. **ALWAYS** pull latest: `git pull origin main` (or `master`)\n3. **VERIFY** you're on main: `git branch --show-current`\n4. **VERIFY** branch is clean: `git status`\n5. **ONLY THEN** create new branch: `git checkout -b <new-feature-branch>`\n\n**Never create a branch without first explicitly checking out main.**\n\n### Before Pushing a Branch\n\n1. Run `git log origin/main..HEAD --oneline` \n2. Verify commits match the intended PR scope\n3. If unintended changes detected:\n   - Alert the user\n   - Offer to recreate the branch cleanly from main\n   - Do NOT push the contaminated branch\n\n### PR Documentation\n\nIn PR docs, include:\n\n- **Base Branch**: State whether based on `main` or another feature branch\n- **Depends On**: List any prerequisite PRs that must merge first\n\n## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n1. **Branching from wrong branch** - Always verify with `git branch` first\n2. **Stale main** - Pull latest main before branching\n3. **Mixed PR scope** - If unrelated changes appear, recreate branch from main\n4. **Dependent branches without documentation** - Make dependencies explicit\n\n## When in Doubt\n\n- Check git state first\n- Create a unique git worktree for your work\n- Branch from main\n- Keep PRs focused and single-purpose",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/git-branch-management,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "710",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (710): Git branch management"}
        #:rule{:title "Git Worktrees",
               :agent-behavior
               "Git worktrees enable multiple branches to be checked out simultaneously in separate directories from a single repository.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit, :message "Standard: Git Worktrees"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Git Worktrees for Parallel Workflows\n\n## Context\n\nGit worktrees enable multiple branches to be checked out simultaneously in separate directories from a single repository.\n\n**Core Principle: AI agents always work in their own dedicated worktree.** This ensures isolation, prevents conflicts, and enables concurrent operations.\n\nThe following types of worktrees support different use cases:\n\n1. **AI session isolation**: Each AI agent session operates in its own worktree to prevent conflicts\n2. **Development workflow**: Working on multiple features without branch switching overhead\n3. **miniforge architecture**: The Operator agent managing multiple concurrent workflows with isolated contexts\n4. **IDE integration**: Dedicated worktrees for different editors (e.g., Cursor on `main` for stable reference)\n\n## Development Usage\n\n### Standard Worktree Layout\n\n```\nminiforge.ai/\n├── miniforge/                  # Main worktree (for active feature work)\n├── miniforge-cursor/           # Permanent: Cursor IDE on main branch\n├── miniforge-claude-*/         # AI Session: Claude Code session worktrees (clean up before new session)\n├── miniforge-cursor-ai-*/      # AI Session: Cursor AI session worktrees (clean up before new session)\n├── miniforge-{feature}/        # Temporary: One per active feature PR\n└── miniforge-{context}/        # Optional: For operator spawned workflows\n```\n\n### Worktree Lifecycle\n\n**1. Create** (when starting feature):\n```bash\ngit worktree add -b feat/component-name /path/to/miniforge-component origin/main\n```\n\n**2. Work** (develop in isolation):\n```bash\ncd /path/to/miniforge-component\n# All git operations work normally\ngit add, commit, push, etc.\n```\n\n**3. Cleanup** (after PR merge):\n```bash\ngit worktree remove /path/to/miniforge-component\ngit push origin --delete feat/component-name\ngit worktree prune\ngit fetch --prune\n```\n\n### Cleanup Checklist\n\n**After every PR merge**, clean up:\n\n- [ ] `git worktree remove /path/to/feature-worktree`\n- [ ] `git push origin --delete feat/branch-name` (if needed)\n- [ ] `git fetch --prune` (update local refs)\n- [ ] Verify: `git worktree list` shows only active worktrees\n\n**Before starting each new AI session**, clean up:\n\n- [ ] Remove old AI session worktrees (see \"AI Session Lifecycle Management\" below)\n- [ ] `git worktree prune` - Clean up stale references\n- [ ] Verify: No stale `miniforge-claude-*` or `miniforge-cursor-ai-*` worktrees remain\n\n### Worktree Types and Lifecycle\n\n| Type | Examples | Lifecycle |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Permanent** | `miniforge-cursor`, `miniforge` | Keep indefinitely |\n| **Temporary** | `miniforge-{feature}`, `miniforge-{fix}` | Delete after merge |\n| **AI Session** | `miniforge-claude-*`, `miniforge-cursor-ai-*` | Delete when starting new session |\n\n### AI Session Lifecycle Management\n\n**CRITICAL: Always clean up old AI session worktrees before starting a new session.**\n\n1. **Check for existing AI session worktrees:** `git worktree list | grep -E 'miniforge-(claude|cursor-ai)'`\n2. **Remove old session worktrees:** `git worktree remove /path/to/old-session` — then `git worktree prune` if directory was manually deleted\n3. **Clean up associated branches:** `git branch -D branch-name` and `git push origin --delete branch-name` for any pushed session branches\n4. **Create new session worktree:** `git worktree add -b feat/my-feature /path/to/miniforge-claude-$(date +%Y%m%d) origin/main`\n\n**Session cleanup checklist** (run before each new session):\n\n- [ ] `git worktree list` — identify old AI session worktrees\n- [ ] `git worktree remove /path/to/old-session` — remove each\n- [ ] `git worktree prune` — clean stale references\n- [ ] `git branch -D` — delete local session branches if unneeded\n- [ ] `git push origin --delete` — delete remote session branches if pushed\n- [ ] Verify: only current/active worktrees remain\n\n## miniforge Operator Usage\n\n### Architectural Significance\n\nGit worktrees are a **native feature** for miniforge's multi-loop architecture:\n\n```\nOperator Agent\n    ├── Main Workflow     → miniforge/           (feat/user-feature)\n    ├── Fix Workflow      → miniforge-fix-123/   (fix/workflow-error)\n    ├── Test Workflow     → miniforge-test-456/  (test/new-strategy)\n    └── Review Context    → miniforge-cursor/    (main, read-only)\n```\n\n**Benefits for Operator:**\n\n- **Isolation:** no branch-switching conflicts\n- **Concurrency:** parallel workflows share no state\n- **Provenance:** worktree→branch→workflow mapping for debugging\n- **Recovery:** disk-persisted worktree survives crash\n\nSee [templates/worktree-operator-pattern.clj](../templates/worktree-operator-pattern.clj) for the Clojure operator pattern.\n\n## Anti-Patterns\n\n| Anti-pattern | Rule |\n|---|---|\n| Check out `main` in multiple worktrees | Each worktree needs a unique branch |\n| Leave worktrees after merge | Remove immediately after merge |\n| Long-lived branch worktrees (staging, production) | Worktrees are for short-lived feature branches |\n| Reuse old AI session worktrees | Fresh worktree per session |\n| Start new session without cleanup | Remove old AI session worktrees first (`git worktree remove`), then prune stale refs (`git worktree prune`), before creating a new session worktree. |\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Worktree already exists\n```bash\n# Error: 'feat/branch' is already checked out at '/path/to/worktree'\n# Fix: Remove old worktree first\ngit worktree remove /path/to/worktree\n# Or: Prune if directory was manually deleted\ngit worktree prune\n```\n\n### Can't delete worktree\n```bash\n# Error: working tree already exists\n# Fix: Make sure no processes are using the directory\nlsof +D /path/to/worktree  # Check open files\ngit worktree remove --force /path/to/worktree  # Force removal\n```\n\n### Branch deleted but worktree remains\n```bash\ngit worktree prune\ngit fetch --prune\n```\n\n## References\n\n- Git worktree docs: `git help worktree`\n- See also: [git-branch-management.mdc](./git-branch-management.mdc)\n- See also: [pr-documentation.mdc](./pr-documentation.mdc)\n- Architecture: `specs/normative/N5-delta-supervisory-control-plane.md` (Operator agent / supervisory control plane)\n\n---\n\n**Rule**: AI agents always work in their own dedicated worktree. Use git worktrees for AI session isolation, parallel development, and operator multi-loop execution. Always clean up old AI session worktrees before starting a new session. Create worktrees per feature, cleanup after merge. Leverage for miniforge's concurrent workflow architecture.",
               :id :std/git-worktrees,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "725",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (725): Git Worktrees"}
