                :message
                "Standard: Enforce Miniforge.ai header and copyright on published content — OSS repositories only"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["docs/*.md"
                 "docs/**/*.md"
                 "specs/*.md"
                 "specs/**/*.md"
                 "*.md"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Miniforge.ai Header & Copyright\n\n<version>1.1.0</version>\n\n## Scope — OSS repositories only\n\n**This rule applies exclusively to Apache-2.0 licensed (open source) repositories.**\n\nDetection: the repository root must contain a `LICENSE` file whose content includes the string `\"Apache\"`. If no such file exists, or the file contains `\"Proprietary\"` / `\"All Rights Reserved\"`, **do not apply this rule**.\n\nProprietary Thesium products use `.thesium-standards/project/header-proprietary` instead. Never apply an Apache 2.0 copyright header to a proprietary file.\n\n## Context\n- Use when authoring or editing site, docs, blog, or README content in an Apache-licensed repo.\n\n## Requirements\n- Include this header block verbatim at the top of content.\n- Use copyright owner: Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai).\n- Do not attribute copyright to Miniforge.ai as an entity. ✅\n\n## Examples\n<example>\n  Title: Miniforge.ai\n  Subtitle: An agentic SDLC / fleet-control platform\n  Author: Christopher Lester\n  Line: Founder, Miniforge.ai (project)\n  Copyright 2025-2026 Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai)\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n  Copyright 2025 miniforge.ai\n</example>",
               :id :std/header-copyright,
               :detection
               {:type :content-scan,
                :pattern "Christopher Lester",
                :mode :negative,
                :email-pattern "christopher@miniforge\\.ai"},
               :category "810",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (810): Enforce Miniforge.ai header and copyright on published content — OSS repositories only"}
        #:rule{:title
               "HTML — semantic elements, buttons-vs-links, labelled forms, tables for tabular data",
               :agent-behavior
               "HTML in any miniforge web surface MUST favour semantic elements,\nlabelled controls, and meaningful structure. Generic `<div>` is the\nfallback, not the default.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: HTML — semantic elements, buttons-vs-links, labelled forms, tables for tabular data"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.html" "**/*.htm"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# HTML standards\n\nHTML in any miniforge web surface MUST favour semantic elements,\nlabelled controls, and meaningful structure. Generic `<div>` is the\nfallback, not the default.\n\n## HTML-001 — Semantic HTML first\n\nUse semantic elements before generic `<div>`.\n\n| Use | For |\n|---|---|\n| `<main>` | the primary page content |\n| `<section>` | thematically grouped content with a heading |\n| `<nav>` | navigation block |\n| `<article>` | self-contained item (audit entry, log line, etc.) |\n| `<button>` | actions |\n| `<form>` | form submission |\n| `<label>` | input labels |\n| `<table>` | tabular data (see HTML-004) |\n\n`<div>` is the fallback when none of the above expresses intent.\n\n## HTML-002 — Buttons are actions, links are navigation\n\n| Element | Purpose | Triggers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `<button>` | perform an action | mutations, dialogs, toggles |\n| `<a href=\"…\">` | navigate | route changes, external URLs |\n\n```html\n<!-- BAD — anchor used for an action -->\n<a href=\"#\" onclick=\"cancelRun()\">Cancel</a>\n\n<!-- GOOD -->\n<button data-action=\"cancel-run\">Cancel</button>\n```\n\nA \"button styled as a link\" or \"link styled as a button\" stays the\ncorrect element for its semantic purpose; styling does not change the\nchoice.\n\n## HTML-003 — Forms must be labelled\n\nEvery input MUST have either an explicit `<label for=\"…\">` association\nor an `aria-label` / `aria-labelledby` accessible name.\n\n```html\n<!-- GOOD — explicit label -->\n<label for=\"repo-name\">Repository name</label>\n<input id=\"repo-name\" name=\"repo-name\" />\n\n<!-- ALSO GOOD — accessible name on icon button -->\n<button aria-label=\"Close dialog\" data-action=\"close-dialog\">×</button>\n```\n\nPlaceholder text is NOT a label. Reject any input where the only naming\nmechanism is `placeholder=`.\n\n## HTML-004 — Tables are valid for tabular operational data\n\nTables SHOULD be used for naturally tabular data. Operator surfaces in\nminiforge products have a lot of this:\n\n- workflow runs\n- policy checks\n- audit events\n- evidence rows\n- repository lists\n\nUse `<table>` + `<thead>`/`<tbody>` + `<th scope=\"col\">` for these.\nResist the urge to render tabular data as nested flexboxes — it loses\nkeyboard navigation, screen-reader semantics, and column alignment.\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen writing or reviewing HTML:\n1. Grep for `<div onclick=`, `<a href=\"#\"`, `<a href=\"javascript:`,\n   anchor-as-button patterns — each is an HTML-002 violation (and\n   typically a JS-004 violation).\n2. Every `<input>` / `<select>` / `<textarea>` needs a labelled name.\n3. Tabular data rendered as nested flex containers gets refactored to\n   `<table>`.\n4. `<div>` count per page is a soft signal — high counts often hide\n   missing semantics.",
               :id :std/html,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "270",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (270): HTML — semantic elements, buttons-vs-links, labelled forms, tables for tabular data"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Master index of all rules — consult when searching for applicable rules",
               :agent-behavior
               "Uses Dewey Decimal principles: hierarchical, numeric, extensible.\nDewey codes live in frontmatter (`dewey: \"NNN\"`), not filenames or paths.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Master index of all rules — consult when searching for applicable rules"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Rules Catalog (Dewey-style)\n\n## Classification System\n\nUses Dewey Decimal principles: hierarchical, numeric, extensible.\nDewey codes live in frontmatter (`dewey: \"NNN\"`), not filenames or paths.\n\n| Range | Category | Description |\n|-------|----------|-------------|\n| 000-099 | Foundations | Architecture, design philosophy, core principles |\n| 100-199 | Tools | Linters, formatters, build tools |\n| 200-299 | Languages | Language-specific conventions |\n| 300-399 | Frameworks | Platform & framework rules |\n| 400-499 | Testing | Testing standards & quality |\n| 500-599 | Operations | CI/CD, monitoring, security |\n| 600-699 | Documentation | Docs standards |\n| 700-799 | Workflows | Git, PR, release processes |\n| 800-899 | Project | Reserved for project-specific |\n| 900-999 | Meta | Templates, indexes, deprecated |\n\n## Directory Structure\n\n```\n<repo-root>/                             # consumed as .standards/ submodule\n├── index.mdc                            # This catalog (dewey: \"000\")\n├── foundations/\n│   ├── stratified-design.mdc                 # dewey: \"001\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── code-quality.mdc                      # dewey: \"002\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── result-handling.mdc                   # dewey: \"003\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── validation-boundaries.mdc             # dewey: \"004\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── exceptions-as-data.mdc                # dewey: \"005\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── named-constants.mdc                   # dewey: \"006\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── config-as-data.mdc                    # dewey: \"007\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── no-dead-code.mdc                      # dewey: \"008\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── self-documenting-code.mdc            # dewey: \"009\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── simple-made-easy.mdc                  # dewey: \"010\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── layered-architecture.mdc              # dewey: \"011\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── specification-standards.mdc           # dewey: \"020\"\n│   ├── work-spec-authoring.mdc               # dewey: \"021\"\n│   ├── runtime-no-host-docker-socket.mdc     # dewey: \"030\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── runtime-require-rootless.mdc          # dewey: \"031\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── runtime-restrict-host-mounts.mdc      # dewey: \"032\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── runtime-require-image-digest-pin.mdc  # dewey: \"033\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   └── localization.mdc                      # dewey: \"050\"  alwaysApply: true\n├── languages/\n│   ├── clojure.mdc                      # dewey: \"210\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── clojure-exception-handling.mdc   # dewey: \"211\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── clojure-no-requiring-resolve.mdc # dewey: \"212\"  globs: clj/cljc\n│   ├── python.mdc                       # dewey: \"220\"\n│   ├── rust.mdc                         # dewey: \"230\"\n│   ├── rust-async.mdc                   # dewey: \"231\"\n│   ├── rust-unsafe.mdc                  # dewey: \"232\"\n│   ├── rust-observability.mdc           # dewey: \"233\"\n│   ├── rust-wire-protocols.mdc          # dewey: \"234\"\n│   ├── swift.mdc                        # dewey: \"240\"\n│   ├── javascript.mdc                   # dewey: \"250\"\n│   ├── css.mdc                          # dewey: \"260\"\n│   └── html.mdc                         # dewey: \"270\"\n├── frameworks/\n│   ├── polylith.mdc                     # dewey: \"310\"\n│   ├── polylith-composition.mdc         # dewey: \"311\"\n│   ├── polylith-tool.mdc                # dewey: \"312\"\n│   ├── kubernetes.mdc                   # dewey: \"320\"\n│   ├── web-architecture-mode.mdc        # dewey: \"330\"\n│   ├── browser-security.mdc             # dewey: \"331\"\n│   ├── api-surface-classes.mdc          # dewey: \"332\"\n│   ├── fulcro.mdc                       # dewey: \"340\"\n│   └── fulcro-rad.mdc                   # dewey: \"341\"\n├── testing/\n│   └── standards.mdc                    # dewey: \"400\"  alwaysApply: true\n├── documentation/\n│   └── documentation-discipline.mdc     # dewey: \"600\"  alwaysApply: true\n├── workflows/\n│   ├── git-branch-management.mdc        # dewey: \"710\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── pre-commit-discipline.mdc        # dewey: \"715\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── tests-with-code.mdc              # dewey: \"716\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── code-review-rigor.mdc            # dewey: \"720\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── pr-documentation.mdc             # dewey: \"721\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── pr-layering.mdc                  # dewey: \"722\"  alwaysApply: true\n│   ├── git-worktrees.mdc                # dewey: \"725\"\n│   ├── datever.mdc                      # dewey: \"730\"\n│   └── bb-over-shell.mdc                # dewey: \"740\"\n├── project/\n│   ├── header-copyright.mdc             # dewey: \"810\"\n│   └── rust-miniforge-shape.mdc         # dewey: \"835\"\n└── meta/\n    └── rule-format.mdc                  # dewey: \"900\"\n```\n\n## Current Rules\n\n### foundations/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 001 | `stratified-design` | ✓ | Each layer a small language built on the one below; a one-way **DAG of abstraction** (SICP §2.2.4) — discovered per problem, not a fixed layer stack |\n| 002 | `code-quality` | ✓ | Composable pipelines, no nested conditionals, DRY |\n| 003 | `result-handling` | ✓ | success?/failed? predicates; schema constructors; factory functions for any repeated map shape (event envelopes, task records, etc.) |\n| 004 | `validation-boundaries` | ✓ | Schemas at interface/external boundaries only |\n| 005 | `exceptions-as-data` | ✓ | Anomalies as data in flows; throw only at absolute boundaries |\n| 006 | `named-constants` | ✓ | No magic numbers / magic strings; every meaningful literal extracted to a `def` with an intent-bearing docstring |\n| 007 | `config-as-data` | ✓ | Operational config lives in `.edn` files with a Malli schema; code holds only fallback defaults |\n| 008 | `no-dead-code` | ✓ | Delete unused code in the change that obsoletes it; no commented-out blocks, no `_legacy` shims without an explicit removal plan |\n| 009 | `self-documenting-code` | ✓ | Code is the artifact; comments explain WHY when non-obvious, never restate WHAT; self-document first; document the public boundary (interface), keep impl light (headers + docstrings exempt) |\n| 010 | `simple-made-easy` | ✓ | Simplicity over familiarity, uncomplected artifacts |\n| 011 | `layered-architecture` | ✓ | The Dependency Rule: module deps point inward only (Adapters→Application→Domain→Foundations); pure core; ports & adapters — one coarse module-level instance of 001 |\n| 020 | `specification-standards` |  | Normative specs (N1-N6), spec governance, conformance |\n| 021 | `work-spec-authoring` |  | `work/*.spec.edn` format: priority, theme, normative-refs, testable acceptance criteria |\n| 030 | `runtime-no-host-docker-socket` | ✓ | Reject bind mounts of `/var/run/docker.sock` and equivalents (capsule isolation) |\n| 031 | `runtime-require-rootless` | ✓ | Warn / hard-stop based on the runtime's `:rootless` capability |\n| 032 | `runtime-restrict-host-mounts` | ✓ | Review host bind mounts outside an allowlist |\n| 033 | `runtime-require-image-digest-pin` | ✓ | Reject execution plans whose `:image-digest` is not `sha256:<64-hex>` |\n| 050 | `localization` | ✓ | No raw strings in any emitted output; user-facing → en-US.edn, system-facing → system.edn; msg/t lookup always |\n\n### languages/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 210 | `clojure` | ✓ | Polylith + per-file stratified design, map access patterns |\n| 211 | `clojure-exception-handling` | ✓ | Prefer slingshot `try+` / `throw+` (strict superset of `try`); plain `try` allowed in three narrow cases (no-slingshot-dep leaf catch, leaf inside `try+`, REPL/`(comment …)`) |\n| 212 | `clojure-no-requiring-resolve` | clj/cljc | BLOCKING (`enforcement.action: hard-halt`): no `requiring-resolve` — use a direct `(:require …)`, fix load-order/design instead of lazy-resolving |\n| 220 | `python` |  | Poetry, functional Python |\n| 230 | `rust` |  | Workspaces, typed boundary commands, pipeline selection, data-driven content, toolchain (Rust 2024 + workspace lints), domain newtypes, dependency policy |\n| 231 | `rust-async` |  | Don't block the runtime, no locks across `.await`, cancellation-aware `select!`, bounded channels, task lifecycle ownership, `Arc<Mutex<T>>` not as default architecture |\n| 232 | `rust-unsafe` |  | `unsafe_code = \"deny\"` workspace-wide; isolated behind safe APIs; `// SAFETY:` comment on every site; performance alone insufficient |\n| 233 | `rust-observability` |  | `tracing` not `println!`, correlation IDs via `#[instrument]`, error source-chain preservation, log strings localized to system locale |\n| 234 | `rust-wire-protocols` |  | Wire structs separate from domain, versioned envelopes, validate-on-deserialize, append-only ratchet for audit-trail payloads |\n| 240 | `swift` |  | Swift conventions |\n| 250 | `javascript` |  | Browser JS — sparse use, no globals, ES modules, no inline handlers, safe DOM |\n| 260 | `css` |  | Design tokens, semantic class names, shallow selectors, accessible focus |\n| 270 | `html` |  | Semantic elements, buttons-vs-links, labelled forms |\n\n### frameworks/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 310 | `polylith` |  | Polylith workspace conventions |\n| 311 | `polylith-composition` |  | Brick-oriented production code, interface contracts, validation gates, change-derived CI scope |\n| 312 | `polylith-tool` |  | Polylith as an architectural control tool — preflight reasoning, canonical workflow, agent-role operational requirements |\n| 320 | `kubernetes` |  | K8s/Kustomize/ArgoCD conventions |\n| 330 | `web-architecture-mode` |  | Fulcro for stateful UI, server HTML for simple pages, no new browser fw without ADR |\n| 331 | `browser-security` |  | Server-enforced authz, CSRF, no secrets in browser, blast-radius UI |\n| 332 | `api-surface-classes` |  | Internal (EDN/Transit) vs first-party cross-language (JSON/SSE) vs customer extension (REST/webhooks) |\n| 340 | `fulcro` |  | Queries, idents, normalisation, mutations, loads, domain naming |\n| 341 | `fulcro-rad` |  | RAD optional for CRUD/forms; never forced onto bespoke workflow UI |\n\n### testing/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 400 | `standards` | ✓ | Factory functions, same standards as production code |\n\n### documentation/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 600 | `documentation-discipline` | ✓ | Documentation states the current contract only; per-surface length limits; schemas/fields document contracts not history; narrative → external docs; examples → tests |\n\n### workflows/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 710 | `git-branch-management` | ✓ | Branch from main, single-purpose PRs |\n| 715 | `pre-commit-discipline` | ✓ | Never bypass hooks without investigation; fix root causes |\n| 716 | `tests-with-code` | ✓ | Code changes ship with test changes; \"no test diff\" requires an explicit pure-restructuring claim |\n| 720 | `code-review-rigor` | ✓ | Adversarial PR-review obligations — happy-path trace, bootstrap order, default-value scrutiny, compile-test fact-claims, intent over symptoms, refactor-not-exempt |\n| 721 | `pr-documentation` | ✓ | Living PR docs in `docs/pull-requests/` |\n| 722 | `pr-layering` | ✓ | Stratified PR DAG; <400 lines; independently mergeable |\n| 725 | `git-worktrees` |  | Parallel workflows with worktrees, operator multi-loop |\n| 730 | `datever` |  | Date-based versioning strategy |\n| 740 | `bb-over-shell` |  | Build / launch / dev tasks live in bb.edn, not scripts/*.sh |\n\n### project/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 810 | `header-copyright` |  | Miniforge.ai header + copyright ownership |\n| 835 | `rust-miniforge-shape` |  | Miniforge architectural Rust constraints: typed workflow state, `PolicyDecision` as value, structured `Finding`, adapter traits vs concrete domain, append-only evidence, UI never imported by core |\n\n### meta/\n\n| Dewey | File | Always | Description |\n|-------|------|--------|-------------|\n| 900 | `rule-format` |  | Rule creation template & Dewey classification |\n\n## Adding Rules\n\n1. Find the appropriate Dewey category above\n2. Create `<category>/<slug>.mdc` with `dewey: \"NNN\"` in frontmatter\n3. Set `alwaysApply: true` if the rule should be pre-injected into every agent prompt\n4. Update this index and `CLAUDE.md`",
               :id :std/index,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "000",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (000): Master index of all rules — consult when searching for applicable rules"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Browser JavaScript — sparse use, no global state, ES modules, no inline handlers, safe DOM",
               :agent-behavior
               "JavaScript in any miniforge web surface is a *progressive enhancement*\ntool, not an application-architecture substrate. Stateful product UI\nbelongs in Fulcro (`frameworks/fulcro.mdc`), not in JS modules.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Browser JavaScript — sparse use, no global state, ES modules, no inline handlers, safe DOM"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.js" "**/*.mjs"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# JavaScript (browser) standards\n\nJavaScript in any miniforge web surface is a *progressive enhancement*\ntool, not an application-architecture substrate. Stateful product UI\nbelongs in Fulcro (`frameworks/fulcro.mdc`), not in JS modules.\n\n## JS-001 — Use JavaScript sparingly\n\nPlain JavaScript is for progressive enhancement or isolated widgets only.\n\n| Acceptable | Not acceptable |\n|---|---|\n| menu toggles | application state machines |\n| copy-to-clipboard | multi-entity editing flows |\n| small form enhancement | client routing |\n| simple websocket event display | normalised data caches |\n| local filtering / sorting | optimistic UI for remote ops |\n\nAnything in the not-acceptable column belongs in Fulcro.\n\n## JS-002 — No global application state\n\nApplication state MUST NOT live on `window` or unstructured globals.\n\n```js\n// BAD\nwindow.currentWorkflow = workflow;\nwindow.userState = state;\n\n// GOOD — module-local, scoped, explicit\nconst moduleState = { selected: null };\nexport function setSelected(x) { moduleState.selected = x; }\n```\n\nAcceptable:\n- module-local state inside an ES module\n- isolated per-widget state captured in a closure or class instance\n- explicit named state objects scoped to one module\n\n## JS-003 — Use ES modules\n\nNew JavaScript MUST be authored as ES modules and loaded with\n`<script type=\"module\">`.\n\n```html\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/assets/app.js\"></script>\n```\n\nNo `var`, no IIFE wrappers, no UMD bundles for new code.\n\n## JS-004 — No inline event handlers\n\nHTML MUST NOT use `onclick=`, `onchange=`, etc. Bind in JS using\n`addEventListener` against a `data-action` selector.\n\n```html\n<!-- BAD -->\n<button onclick=\"deleteRepo()\">Delete</button>\n\n<!-- GOOD -->\n<button data-action=\"delete-repo\">Delete</button>\n```\n\n```js\ndocument\n  .querySelector('[data-action=\"delete-repo\"]')\n  .addEventListener('click', onDeleteRepo);\n```\n\n## JS-005 — Bind behaviour with `data-*`, not visual classes\n\nBehavioural selectors MUST use semantic `data-*` attributes. JS MUST NOT\nselect on visual class names — those are CSS's contract, not JS's.\n\n```js\n// BAD\ndocument.querySelector('.blue-button');\n\n// GOOD\ndocument.querySelector('[data-action=\"copy-token\"]');\n```\n\n## JS-006 — Async operations need visible states\n\nAny browser-triggered network operation MUST surface four states to the\nuser: **idle, pending, success, failure**.\n\nFailures MUST be visible (not silently swallowed, not console-only). The\nvisual treatment for each state SHOULD be consistent across the surface\n(e.g. a `data-state` attribute the CSS keys off of).\n\n## JS-007 — Guard destructive actions\n\nDestructive actions MUST require confirmation, undo, or typed\nconfirmation, scaled to blast radius:\n\n| Blast radius | Guard |\n|---|---|\n| local UI dismiss | none |\n| revoke a session | confirm dialog |\n| cancel a workflow run | confirm dialog |\n| delete a policy pack | typed-name confirmation |\n| revoke a credential | typed-name confirmation + audit notice |\n| approve a destructive infra change | typed confirmation + blast-radius preview |\n\nSee `frameworks/browser-security.mdc` BSEC-004 for the\nblast-radius-preview requirement.\n\n## JS-008 — Do not inject untrusted HTML\n\nJS MUST NOT assign server-sourced or user-sourced content to the\n`innerHTML`, `outerHTML`, or `insertAdjacentHTML` sinks. These are\nunconditional XSS surfaces.\n\nFor text content, use the `textContent` property:\n\n```js\n// GOOD\nel.textContent = response.message;\n```\n\nFor structured markup, build the tree explicitly with\n`document.createElement` + `appendChild`, or use a `<template>` element\nwith text-only insertion points.\n\n### Sanitiser escape hatch\n\nIf a sanitizer is genuinely required (e.g. rendering trusted Markdown\nto HTML), the use MUST satisfy ALL of:\n\n1. Sanitization goes through a single vetted library (DOMPurify or\n   equivalent) with a default-deny configuration.\n2. The sanitizer call lives in ONE boundary module per repo, named\n   `safe-html` or similar; no other module touches `innerHTML`.\n3. The boundary module exports only `setSanitisedHTML(el, source)` —\n   no raw `innerHTML` setter is exposed.\n4. The boundary module's allow-list (tags, attributes, URL schemes) is\n   reviewed in the PR that introduces it and in any PR that widens it.\n\nReject any PR that introduces a write to `innerHTML` *outside* the\nboundary module, or that widens the boundary module's allow-list\nwithout explicit reviewer sign-off.\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen writing or reviewing browser JS:\n1. If the change introduces durable client state, multi-entity editing,\n   or workflow state → escalate to Fulcro per `frameworks/web-architecture-mode.mdc`\n   WEB-002.\n2. Grep for `window.<name> =` and `onclick=` / `on*=` in HTML and JS;\n   each is a violation of JS-002 / JS-004.\n3. Grep for the `innerHTML` sink — every hit needs justification or\n   rewrite to `textContent` per JS-008.\n4. Reject any new module that reaches into another module's state via\n   globals.",
               :id :std/javascript,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "250",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (250): Browser JavaScript — sparse use, no global state, ES modules, no inline handlers, safe DOM"}
        #:rule{:title "K8s/Kustomize/ArgoCD conventions",
               :agent-behavior
               "- **Always switch to the appropriate Kubernetes cluster context before running kubectl commands**\n- Use `kubectl config use-context <context-name>` to switch environments\n- Verify current context with `kubectl config current-context` before proceeding",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message "Standard: K8s/Kustomize/ArgoCD conventions"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["k8s/**/*.yaml" "**/overlays/**/**/*.yaml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Kubernetes, Kustomize, ArgoCD conventions\n\n## Environment Context (ALWAYS)\n\n- **Always switch to the appropriate Kubernetes cluster context before running kubectl commands**\n- Use `kubectl config use-context <context-name>` to switch environments\n- Verify current context with `kubectl config current-context` before proceeding\n\n## General guidelines\n\n### ArgoCD\n\n- App-of-Apps per env; image tags tracked in overlay patches.\n- Avoid “going backwards”: pin to immutable tag in manifests, change via PR.\n\n### Kustomize\n\n- Kustomize layout: /k8s/base and /k8s/overlays/{local,nonprod,prod}\n\n#### Kustomize Patching\n\n- **Patch only differences**: Only patch fields that actually differ between environments\n- **Strategic merge over JSON patches**: Use strategic merge for multiple resources of same type\n- **Minimal patch scope**: Don't patch metadata labels unless used for selection/monitoring\n- **Base + patches pattern**: Move common structure to base, patch only environment-specific values\n- **Avoid duplication**: If 90%+ of a resource is identical across environments, use base + patches\n\n#### Patch Design Checklist\n\n- [ ] Does this patch only change what actually differs between environments?\n- [ ] Are we patching metadata that serves no functional purpose?\n- [ ] Could this be simplified by moving more to the base?\n- [ ] Does the patch clearly show the intent (what changes per environment)?\n\n### K8s Networking\n\n- For Services: internal traffic uses ClusterIP; public via ALB ingress; no NodePort.\n- Ingress (AWS ALB): set `alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internal|internet-facing` appropriately; HTTPS only; redirect 80→443.\n\n### Health Checks\n\n- Health:\n  - readiness: dependency-aware endpoint (auth optional)\n  - liveness: cheap, local check; avoid remote deps\n\n### Resource constraints\n\n- Resources: set realistic `requests/limits`; don’t copy placeholders.\n- Secrets:\n  - Prefer Secrets Store CSI for runtime; SealedSecrets for K8s-native secrets.",
               :id :std/kubernetes,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "320",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (320): K8s/Kustomize/ArgoCD conventions"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Layered Architecture (the Dependency Rule) — module dependencies point inward only; pure core; ports & adapters",
               :agent-behavior
               "One common, coarse-grained **instance** of stratified design\n(`foundations/stratified-design`, 001), applied at the module / namespace level:\norganize a system into a small fixed stack and let source dependencies point\n**one way only**. This is Clean Architecture / Hexagonal / Ports-and-Adapters —\n*an* instance of one-way dependencies, not the whole of stratified design.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Layered Architecture (the Dependency Rule) — module dependencies point inward only; pure core; ports & adapters"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Layered Architecture — the Dependency Rule (ALWAYS)\n\nOne common, coarse-grained **instance** of stratified design\n(`foundations/stratified-design`, 001), applied at the module / namespace level:\norganize a system into a small fixed stack and let source dependencies point\n**one way only**. This is Clean Architecture / Hexagonal / Ports-and-Adapters —\n*an* instance of one-way dependencies, not the whole of stratified design.\n\n## Canonical strata\n\nInnermost (most stable) to outermost (most volatile). A module may depend only on\nstrata at its level or below:\n\n0. **Foundations** — pure utilities, types, data schemas, constants (no I/O)\n1. **Domain** — business rules, pure functions, policies, invariants (no I/O)\n2. **Application / Use-Cases** — orchestration of domain operations; defines ports\n   (no infra details)\n3. **Adapters** — HTTP controllers, CLIs, schedulers, event handlers, API/DTO\n   mapping\n4. **Infrastructure** — DB/ORM, queues, caches, cloud SDKs, filesystems, external\n   services\n\n> **Allowed dependencies:** `Adapters → Application → Domain → Foundations`, and\n> `Infrastructure → (Application | Domain | Foundations)` **through\n> ports/interfaces only**. No upward dependencies.\n\nThis is a chain by construction — a deliberate coarsening. The finer,\nproblem-specific, branching strata live *within* these bands and are governed by\nstratified design (001).\n\n## Principles\n\n- **No cycles** — reject any PR that introduces an import / namespace cycle.\n- **Pure core** — Domain has no I/O, frameworks, or runtime state. Inject effects\n  at the edges.\n- **Ports & adapters** — define *ports* (interfaces) in Application; implement\n  *adapters* in Adapters / Infrastructure. Domain never names a concrete adapter.\n- **Boundary DTOs** — translate at the edge; never leak transport / DB types into\n  Domain.\n- **Data flow** — inbound adapter → Application (use-case) → Domain → (ports) →\n  outbound adapter. Never reverse it.\n- **Policy location** — Domain for timeless rules; Application for workflow;\n  Adapters for translation; Infrastructure for mechanics.\n\n## When designing / implementing\n\n1. **Sketch the dependency graph** — modules by stratum, arrows down only.\n2. **Name your ports** — `UserRepo`, `PaymentGateway`; Domain depends on the\n   port, not the concrete.\n3. **Decouple by data** — pass plain data across boundaries; map near the edges.\n4. **Confine effects** — thin effectful shells at the outermost layer; inner\n   layers referentially transparent.\n5. **Prove it** — include a tiny import graph in the PR showing no upward edges\n   and no cycles.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Domain importing framework / ORM / cloud SDKs.\n- Adapters calling Infrastructure directly, bypassing Application ports.\n- God modules mixing controller logic, business rules, and DB calls.\n- Cross-strata singletons / service locators.\n- Treating this five-name stack as the *definition* of stratified design — it is\n  one instance (see 001).\n\n## Enforcement hooks\n\n- Clojure: clj-kondo + namespace-graph check failing on cycles / upward deps.\n- TS/JS: dependency-cruiser contracts. Python: import-linter. Rust: crate-graph /\n  workspace layering checks.\n\n## Related\n\n- `foundations/stratified-design` (001) — the general discipline this\n  instantiates.\n- `frameworks/polylith` (310) / `frameworks/polylith-composition` (311) — brick /\n  interface boundaries that enforce this in Clojure.\n- `languages/clojure` (210) — the per-file `Layer N` instance.\n- `foundations/validation-boundaries` (004) — validate at the inbound edge, trust\n  the core.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/layered-architecture,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "011",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (011): Layered Architecture (the Dependency Rule) — module dependencies point inward only; pure core; ports & adapters"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Localization — every emitted string flows through a catalog; user-facing strings into a user locale (en-US, etc.), developer-facing strings into the system locale",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Every **prose** string literal in code that gets emitted is a violation —\n2. When adding a new emitted **prose** string, decide locale by audience: any user (operator dashboard included) will see it → user locale; logs, metric descriptions, observability-bound anomaly text, and internal tags → system locale.\n3. Add the key to the matching catalog file FIRST, then reference it.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Raw string literal in emitted output. Route every user- or developer-facing string through a localization catalog via (msg/t :key); add the key to the matching catalog file first."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Localization (ALWAYS)\n\n**No raw PROSE string literals in emitted output.** Every authored,\nhuman-readable string a process emits — to a UI, an HTTP response, a log\nline, a metric description, an exception payload — flows through a\nlocalization catalog. Machine dispatch tags, programmer-error/invariant\nassertion messages, and dynamically-rendered values are NOT prose and\nare exempt (see \"Not localizable\" below).\n\n## The rule\n\nEvery emitted string lives in a catalog and is looked up by key. Code\nnever carries the literal text. There are **two locales**, picked by\nwho will see the string:\n\n| Locale | Catalog file | For strings that |\n|---|---|---|\n| **User locale** | `messages/en-US.edn` (plus future `fr-FR.edn`, `ja-JP.edn`, …) | A user will see in a UI, dashboard, formatted error response, or any other product surface |\n| **System locale** | `messages/system.edn` | A user will never see — logs, metric / span descriptions, internal error tags, debug prose, telemetry attributes, exception data shipped to observability pipelines |\n\nBoth catalogs live under `resources/config/<component>/messages/`.\nBoth are looked up via `(msg/t :key)`. The split is about **destination**,\nnot about whether to localize.\n\n## Why two locales (and not one + a \"no\" exemption)\n\nThe previous version exempted developer-facing strings. Wrong for three reasons:\n\n1. **Centralization.** One place to audit, grep, and edit developer-facing prose.\n2. **Sanitization gate.** The catalog is the chokepoint for PII / secrets scanning; raw literals scatter that surface.\n3. **Reclassification without code change.** If a \"developer-facing\" string later lands on a customer console, move its key from `system.edn` to `en-US.edn` without touching emit sites.\n\n## Examples\n\nUser-facing — UI labels, displayed errors, body copy:\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — raw string in view\n[:h3 \"No Workflows Yet\"]\n[:button \"Pause\"]\n\n;; GOOD — user-locale lookup\n[:h3 (msg/t :workflow/none-heading)]\n[:button (msg/t :cp/btn-pause)]\n```\n\nDeveloper-facing — metric descriptions, observability-bound text (same pattern applies to `log/` calls):\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — raw string in metric definition\n(otel/counter \"fleet.events.share\"\n              \"Events appended to the durable event log.\"\n              \"1\")\n\n;; GOOD — system-locale lookup\n(otel/counter \"fleet.events.share\"\n              (msg/t :metric-desc/fleet-events-share)\n              \"1\")\n```\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — raw string in anomaly message\n(anomaly/anomaly :conflict\n                 \"share-event!: event-id reuse with conflicting payload-digest\"\n                 {:event/id id})\n\n;; GOOD — system-locale lookup (anomaly text is observability-bound)\n(anomaly/anomaly :conflict\n                 (msg/t :anomaly/event-conflicting-digest)\n                 {:event/id id})\n```\n\n## Anomaly → user-locale mapping\n\n`:anomaly/type` is a coarse category vocabulary (`:not-found`, `:conflict`, `:invalid-input`, …)\n(see `foundations/exceptions-as-data` for the full vocabulary).\nIt can't uniquely identify a specific user-facing message; mapping requires a finer key.\n\n**Producer responsibility.** When an anomaly might surface in a UI, attach `:user/error-key`:\n\n```clojure\n(anomaly/anomaly :conflict\n                 (msg/t :anomaly/event-conflicting-digest)   ;; system text\n                 {:user/error-key :error/event-conflicting-digest\n                  :event/id id})\n```\n\n**Renderer responsibility.** Read `:user/error-key` and look it up:\n\n```clojure\n(if-let [k (get-in anomaly [:anomaly/data :user/error-key])]\n  (msg/t k)\n  ;; No key → anomaly wasn't meant for a UI, or producer has a gap.\n  ;; Render a generic fallback by :anomaly/type, never the raw system text.\n  (msg/t (default-error-key (:anomaly/type anomaly))))\n```\n\nThe `<feature>-<condition>` naming convention makes the pairing mechanical:\n`:anomaly/event-conflicting-digest` (system) ↔ `:error/event-conflicting-digest` (user).\n\nWhen an anomaly is purely operational (telemetry-only), omit `:user/error-key`.\nCallers that render to a UI fall back to the type-level message.\n\n## Catalog files are the canonical source\n\n```edn\n;; resources/config/<component>/messages/en-US.edn\n{:knowledge-shared/messages\n {:workflow/none-heading \"No Workflows Yet\"\n  :workflow/none-body    \"Workflows will appear here when you run a workflow\"\n  :duration/ms-suffix    \"ms\"}}\n```\n\n```edn\n;; resources/config/<component>/messages/system.edn\n{:knowledge-shared/system\n {:log/share-event-conflict         \"share-event! conflict on event-id\"\n  :anomaly/event-conflicting-digest \"share-event!: event-id reuse with conflicting payload-digest\"\n  :metric-desc/fleet-events-share   \"Events appended to the durable event log.\"}}\n```\n\nNew strings go into the appropriate catalog FIRST, then are referenced by key.\n\n## What goes where\n\nNamespace prefixes: user locale uses `:workflow/`, `:cp/`, `:error/`; system locale uses `:log/`, `:anomaly/`, `:metric-desc/`, `:debug/`.\nUse domain semantics, not UI position: `:workflow/none-heading` not `:sidebar/empty-title`.\nCommon suffixes: `-heading`, `-body`, `-hint`, `-label`, `-prefix`, `-suffix`, `-placeholder`.\n\n| Category | Example | Catalog |\n|----------|---------|---------|\n| UI labels | \"Pause\", \"Submit\", \"Cancel\" | User locale |\n| Headings and body copy | \"No agents registered\" | User locale |\n| Error messages displayed to user | \"Agent not found\" | User locale |\n| Units and suffixes shown in UI | \"ms\", \"s\", \" tokens\" | User locale |\n| Placeholder / hint text | \"Type your response...\" | User locale |\n| Log messages (developer-facing) | \"Error reading file\" | System locale |\n| Metric / span descriptions (OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, etc.) | \"Events appended to the log.\" | System locale |\n| Anomaly `:anomaly/message` text | \"share-event!: id reuse with conflicting digest\" | System locale |\n| Internal error tags shipped in `ex-data` / log fields | \"eql-processing-failed\" | System locale |\n| Code comments | | Not emitted — raw |\n| Test assertions | | Not emitted — raw |\n| EDN / config keys | `:auto-apply/tick-ms` | Not emitted — raw |\n| Source-code identifiers | function / variable names | Not emitted — raw |\n| Machine dispatch tags (code branches / tests match on the value) | `:error {:type \"not_found\"}` matched by callers | Not prose — see below |\n| Programmer-error / invariant messages | `(assert p \"msg\")`, `IllegalArgumentException` guard text | Not prose — see below |\n| Dynamic value rendered as its own text | `(name status-keyword)`, `(str some-id)` | Not prose — see below |\n\n## Not localizable — machine and developer surface (do NOT flag)\n\nLocalization governs **authored prose a human reads**. Three categories look\nlike emitted strings but are not prose, and forcing them through a catalog is\nwrong — exempt them:\n\n1. **Machine dispatch tags.** A string (or, preferably, keyword) VALUE that code\n   branches on or that tests assert on — e.g. an error result's\n   `:type \"not_found\"` that a caller matches with `(= \"not_found\" …)`. It is a\n   machine identifier, not display text. The right fix is to make it a\n   **keyword**, not to localize it. (A dispatch tag is not \"a short user\n   message\" — the test is whether code/tests key off the *value*.)\n2. **Programmer-error and invariant messages.** The text of an `(assert …)`,\n   or of the `IllegalArgumentException` / `throw+` a function raises to say *the\n   call site is wrong* (a programmer-error guard per\n   `foundations/exceptions-as-data`). These fire on bugs during development, not\n   on runtime data a user or operator sees. Leave them raw.\n3. **Dynamic values rendered as their own text.** `(name some-keyword)`,\n   `(str some-id)`, `(str n)` — there is no string *literal* here; the emitted\n   text is runtime data (an enum keyword, an id, a number). You localize the\n   surrounding label and any absent-value fallback, not the value itself.\n   Enumerating every possible domain value into a catalog is not the goal.\n\nThe dividing line: is there an **authored prose string literal** aimed at a\nhuman? If yes, localize it. If the emitted text is a dispatch key, a\ndeveloper-only assertion, or a rendered runtime value, it is out of scope.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. Every **prose** string literal in code that gets emitted is a violation —\n   replace with `(msg/t :key)`. (Machine dispatch tags, programmer-error /\n   invariant assertion messages, and dynamically-rendered values are NOT prose;\n   see \"Not localizable\" above.)\n2. When adding a new emitted **prose** string, decide locale by audience: any user (operator dashboard included) will see it → user locale; logs, metric descriptions, observability-bound anomaly text, and internal tags → system locale.\n3. Add the key to the matching catalog file FIRST, then reference it.\n4. When reviewing a PR, grep emit sites for raw prose string literals.\n   Each one is a missing catalog key. Patterns to scan (skip matches that\n   are dispatch tags, assertion messages, or dynamically-rendered values\n   per \"Not localizable\"):\n   - `(log/(info|warn|error|debug|trace) \"` — log calls\n   - `(otel/counter \"` / `(otel/histogram \"` / `(otel/gauge \"` —\n     metric-instrument descriptions (third arg)\n   - `(anomaly/anomaly :[^ ]+ \"` — anomaly messages\n   - `(throw (ex-info \"` — surviving ex-info text in boundary\n     namespaces (per `foundations/exceptions-as-data`)\n   - `[:p \"` / `[:h1 \"` / `[:button \"` — view namespaces\n5. Format strings that combine a label + value use separate keys:\n   `(str (msg/t :label) value)` not `(str \"Label: \" value)`.\n6. When in doubt about audience, default to **system locale** —\n   relocating to user locale later is a one-line catalog move; the\n   reverse direction usually means a string a user already saw is\n   getting rephrased in a way that reads as a regression.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Raw PROSE string literals in any emit site — Hiccup views, `log/` calls, metric descriptions, anomaly messages — are violations; replace with `(msg/t :key)`. (Dispatch tags, assertion messages, and dynamically-rendered values are exempt — see \"Not localizable\".)\n- \"Developer-facing strings don't need l10n\" — that exemption is removed; they go to the system locale.\n- Format strings with embedded units: `(format \"%.1fs\" n)` — extract the `s` suffix to a key.\n- Duplicate strings across files — one key, referenced in multiple places.\n- Catalogs referencing each other (EDN aliasing, runtime key chain). Catalogs are flat; each\n  is canonical for its locale. When the same condition needs both a system-locale log line AND\n  a user-locale toast, define a key in EACH catalog. The `<feature>-<condition>` naming\n  convention (see \"Anomaly → user-locale mapping\") makes the pairing mechanical without\n  creating a cross-catalog dependency.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/localization,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "050",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (050): Localization — every emitted string flows through a catalog; user-facing strings into a user locale (en-US, etc.), developer-facing strings into the system locale"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Named constants — no magic numbers or magic strings in code, always extract to a well-named def with a docstring that explains intent",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Every numeric literal larger than 2 or smaller than -1 → check if it deserves a name. 2. Every string literal with structure (path fragment, format placeholder, enum value) → check if it deserves a name *or* belongs in i18n. 3. If the same magic literal appears across files — or many times within one file — it MUST be extracted to a shared constant. This covers every \"Magic\" category above: numbers, opaque strings, and structured strings (file paths, format placeholders, status codes).",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Magic number or string in code. Prefer extracting it to a well-named def with a docstring explaining intent; if it’s truly local, bind it to a clearly-named local — no unexplained literals in logic."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Named Constants (ALWAYS)\n\n**No magic numbers, no magic strings in code.** Every numeric or string literal whose value carries meaning beyond \"this is the only valid value for this position\" must be a named `def` with a docstring that explains *what the value means* and *why this value*.\n\n## The rule\n\nIf a reader has to ask **\"why this number?\"** or **\"what does this string mean?\"**, the literal is magic and must be extracted.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — magic 90000 and magic 0.90 buried in code\n(defn watchdog [config backend]\n  (let [threshold (get config :gap-threshold-ms 90000)]\n    ...))\n\n(select-best-backend current-backend 0.90 1800000 nil)\n\n;; GOOD — named constants with intent in the docstring\n(def default-gap-threshold-ms\n  \"Default wall-clock gap between agent emissions before the watchdog\n   fires (90s). Tuned against Claude Code + Codex streaming cadence —\n   normal turns emit at least one event per minute; 90s allows two\n   missed beats before declaring the stream stalled.\"\n  90000)\n\n(def default-backend-health-threshold\n  \"Backend success-rate floor for selection. Below this, the backend\n   is considered degraded and skipped in favor of a healthier one.\"\n  0.90)\n\n(def default-backend-switch-cooldown-ms\n  \"Minimum interval between automatic backend switches (30 min). Stops\n   thrashing when two backends are oscillating between healthy and\n   degraded.\"\n  (* 30 60 1000))\n```\n\nComposed math (`(* 30 60 1000)`) is preferred over a single opaque integer when the unit composition is part of the intent.\n\n## What counts as a \"magic\" literal\n\nMagic:\n- Threshold / timeout / budget values (any time-or-money number)\n- Retry counts, batch sizes, page sizes\n- Confidence cutoffs, success-rate floors, similarity ratios\n- File paths embedded in code (use config or resource lookup)\n- Status codes other than 0 / 1 (exit 124 = `unix-timeout-exit-code`, exit 130 = `unix-sigint-exit-code`)\n- Domain enumerations spelled inline (`:blocking`, `:warning` repeated across files → `actionable-severities`)\n\nNot magic — fine to inline:\n- `0`, `1`, `-1` as loop / index sentinels (their meaning is structural)\n- `true` / `false`\n- Empty literals: `[]`, `{}`, `#{}`, `\"\"`\n- Math identities: `(inc n)`, `(dec n)`, `(* x 2)` where the `2` is \"doubled\" by intent\n- A self-documenting domain keyword used in a few related spots within one file (e.g. a `try+` catch selector `[:anomaly/category :transient]` and its matching `(failure :transient)` tag) — the keyword name *is* the meaning. Extract only when the same enum is repeated across files (the `actionable-severities` case above)\n\n## Test code is not exempt\n\nTests using bare `750`, `5000`, `7200` for \"short enough to be fast\" / \"long enough to time out\" should still name the intent:\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD\n(verify/run-tests! \"/tmp\" :test-cmd \"sleep 7200\" :timeout-ms 750)\n\n;; GOOD\n(def ^:private absurdly-long-cmd\n  \"Sleep command guaranteed to outlive the test deadline — used to\n   force the timeout path. If the test runs without killing it, CI\n   would see a 2-hour orphan, so the assertion below also checks the\n   process tree is reaped.\"\n  \"sleep 7200\")\n\n(def ^:private aggressive-timeout-ms\n  \"Short timeout to make the test fast — the production default is 30min,\n   but the contract under test is `aborts within deadline`, not `tolerates\n   30min wait`.\"\n  750)\n```\n\nThe named-constant rule is stricter than other style rules in tests because test code is also documentation: a reader auditing the test for correctness has to recover the intent from the names.\n\n## Configuration values\n\nValues that the user can override at runtime live in `.edn` config files (see [config-as-data](./config-as-data.mdc)), *not* as `def`s in code. The `def` in code is the **default** if config is silent. Pattern:\n\n```clojure\n(def default-stream-gap-threshold-ms 90000)\n\n(defn resolve-gap-threshold [config backend]\n  (or (get-in config [:per-backend backend])\n      (get config :default)\n      default-stream-gap-threshold-ms))\n```\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. Every numeric literal larger than 2 or smaller than -1 → check if it deserves a name.\n2. Every string literal with structure (path fragment, format placeholder, enum value) → check if it deserves a name *or* belongs in i18n.\n3. If the same magic literal appears across files — or many times within one file — it MUST be extracted to a shared constant. This covers every \"Magic\" category above: numbers, opaque strings, and structured strings (file paths, format placeholders, status codes). The only exception: a self-documenting domain keyword used in a few related spots (e.g. a catch selector and its matching failure tag) is not a magic literal.\n4. The `def`'s docstring must explain the *intent* (what does this value mean?) and the *rationale* (why this value?). \"Default timeout\" is not a docstring; \"Default timeout — 90s allows two missed Claude-Code event heartbeats before declaring the stream stalled\" is.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Inline numeric literals passed to multi-arg functions where the position carries meaning (`(select-best-backend backend 0.90 1800000 nil)` — every positional arg should be a named constant or a runtime value)\n- `(get config :key 90000)` patterns where the default is magic — extract the default\n- Same numeric literal repeated in multiple files\n- `def` named `default-value` / `magic-x` / `the-number` (the name must explain the intent, not flag the pattern)\n- Pure numeric `def`s without docstrings — the name is half the story, the docstring is the other half",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/named-constants,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "006",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (006): Named constants — no magic numbers or magic strings in code, always extract to a well-named def with a docstring that explains intent"}
        #:rule{:title
               "No dead code, no legacy shims, no commented-out blocks — deleting unused code is part of the change, not a follow-up",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Every PR that adds new functionality — check for the old functionality the new replaces. Did you delete it?\n2. Every PR that adds a function — grep for callers. If there are none, why is it being added?\n3. Every PR that renames a function — make sure no `def old-name new-name` shim is left behind unless the migration plan is documented.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Dead, unreachable, or commented-out code left in place. Delete it in the change that obsoletes it — git history is the archive; removal is part of the work, not a follow-up."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# No Dead Code (ALWAYS)\n\n**Code that is no longer reachable, used, or correct must be deleted in the change that obsoletes it — not left as a \"for now\" comment, a `_unused` rename, or a \"legacy\" shim.** Git history is the archive; commented-out code is not.\n\n## The rule\n\nIf a refactor or feature change makes a function, namespace, helper, or branch unused:\n\n- **Delete it in the same PR.** Not in a follow-up. Not \"after we're sure.\" Now.\n- The change is incomplete until the unused code is removed.\n- Rely on git history — `git log -S <symbol>` recovers anything that was once there.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — keeping the old impl \"just in case\"\n(defn calc-budget-v2 [ctx] ...)   ; the new way\n;; (defn calc-budget-v1 [ctx] ...) ; commented out — REMOVE this entirely\n\n;; BAD — renamed instead of deleted\n(defn ^:deprecated calc-budget-legacy [ctx] (calc-budget-v2 ctx))\n;; A deprecation marker WITHOUT a removal plan is just dead code with paperwork.\n\n;; GOOD — delete it, commit the deletion, trust git\n(defn calc-budget [ctx] ...)   ; one impl, current, used\n```\n\n## What counts as dead code\n\nReject in review:\n\n- **Commented-out function bodies, defns, or imports.** \"Might need this later\" is what `git log` is for.\n- **Unused helper fns** — no callers anywhere in the workspace. Use `(clj-kondo --lint)` or grep before assuming a fn is referenced.\n- **Imports / requires that aren't used.** Lint should already catch these; agents must respect lint warnings.\n- **Branches in `cond` / `case` that are never reachable.** If a branch was \"for backwards compat\" and the compat target is gone, the branch is gone.\n- **`if` arms that are always-true or always-false** after a feature flag flips permanently.\n- **Tests that were `^:disabled` or `(comment ...)`-wrapped \"for now\"** — fix or delete. Never leave them disabled across PRs.\n- **`TODO:` / `FIXME:` / `XXX:` comments older than the current PR** that the author isn't going to address — open an issue or delete the comment.\n- **`_unused_` or `_old_` prefix renames** — these are dead code wearing a hat.\n- **Shims that wrap a \"v1\" API to look like \"v2\"** when no caller depends on v1 anymore. The shim's existence is the smell.\n\n## Legacy / migration windows\n\nIf a deprecation truly needs a migration window (external consumers, persisted data shape):\n\n1. The deprecation is the change, not a \"for now.\"\n2. The PR includes the migration plan in the commit message: who removes it, when, what blocks the removal.\n3. There is a tracked work item (issue, spec, ticket) referenced from the comment that wraps the legacy code.\n4. The block carries the deprecation reason inline: `;; DEPRECATED 2026-06-01: removed when X completes — tracked in #1234`. A bare `;; legacy` is rejected.\n5. **Default to deletion.** A migration window is the exception, not the default; the burden is on the author to justify keeping the old path.\n\n## The user invariant\n\nSome users add a directive to their agent profile: **\"Never introduce legacy code without explicit direction.\"** Treat that as a hard rule in repos that include this standard.\n\nWhen in doubt: ask for explicit permission to leave a deprecation in place, with a removal date. Default to delete.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. Every PR that adds new functionality — check for the old functionality the new replaces. Did you delete it?\n2. Every PR that adds a function — grep for callers. If there are none, why is it being added?\n3. Every PR that renames a function — make sure no `def old-name new-name` shim is left behind unless the migration plan is documented.\n4. Lint warnings about unused symbols are not noise — they are dead code reports. Fix or delete.\n5. When the user says \"we don't need backwards compat\" — that is the explicit direction to delete the shim.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Commented-out code blocks of any size, in any file\n- `;; TODO: remove after X` comments where X has already happened\n- Disabled tests left in the suite (`^:disabled`, `(comment ...)`-wrapped, `(when false ...)`)\n- Helper fns that have zero call sites in the workspace\n- Renames-to-park (`old-x → x-legacy`) without a documented removal plan\n- \"Just in case\" branches in `cond` / `case` for inputs that no caller produces\n- Multiple parallel implementations of the same logic (`*-v1`, `*-v2`, `*-new`) — pick one and delete the others\n- \"For now\" / \"temporary\" comments older than 30 days",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/no-dead-code,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "008",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (008): No dead code, no legacy shims, no commented-out blocks — deleting unused code is part of the change, not a follow-up"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Apply Polylith architecture conventions for component organization and test placement",
               :agent-behavior
               "- **Bases** - Entry points (CLI, servers, API gateways)\n- **Components** - Reusable building blocks with clear interfaces\n- **Projects** - Deployable artifacts that compose bases + components\n- **Development** - Dev-time utilities, not deployed",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Apply Polylith architecture conventions for component organization and test placement"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["components/**/*.clj"
                 "bases/**/*.clj"
                 "projects/**/*.clj"
                 "test/**/*.clj"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Polylith Architecture Best Practices\n\n## Component Structure\n\n- **Bases** - Entry points (CLI, servers, API gateways)\n- **Components** - Reusable building blocks with clear interfaces\n- **Projects** - Deployable artifacts that compose bases + components\n- **Development** - Dev-time utilities, not deployed\n\n## Test Placement by Runtime ⏱️\n\n<critical>\nTests MUST be placed based on their runtime characteristics:\n\n- **< 100ms runtime** → Place in `components/<name>/test/` (unit tests)\n- **≥ 100ms runtime** → Place in `projects/<name>/test/` (integration tests)\n\nThis boundary typically aligns with:\n- Unit tests = fast, isolated, no I/O → bricks\n- Integration tests = slow, with I/O, dependencies → projects\n</critical>\n\n### Rationale\n\n- Pre-commit hooks run brick (component) tests only → must stay fast\n- CI runs full suite (`bb test:all`) → includes integration tests\n- Fast unit tests enable rapid local feedback loops\n- Integration tests verify cross-component behavior without slowing commits\n\n## Test Naming Conventions\n\n```clojure\n;; Unit tests (in components)\n(ns ai.miniforge.workflow.core-test)\n\n;; Integration tests (in projects)\n(ns ai.miniforge.workflow.configurable-integration-test)\n```\n\n<example>\n**Good**: Git operations test taking 300ms → `projects/miniforge/test/ai/miniforge/git_integration_test.clj`\n\n**Good**: Pure function validation taking 5ms → `components/workflow/test/ai/miniforge/workflow/core_test.clj`\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n**Bad**: Datalevin database test in brick → causes pre-commit failures\n\n**Bad**: 200ms test in component → slows down every commit\n</example>\n\n## Interface-Driven Design\n\n- Each component exposes public API via `interface.clj`\n- Internal implementation in `core.clj` or domain-specific namespaces\n- Tests should primarily target the interface contract\n- Components depend only on other component interfaces, never internals\n\n## Unique Interface Names (CRITICAL)\n\n<critical>\n**Components MUST NOT overload the same interface name.** Each component has\nits own unique `:poly/interface`. Runtime backend swapping happens via\nmultimethod dispatch or adapter selection — NEVER by having two components\nclaim the same interface.\n</critical>\n\nPolylith profiles exist to bundle dev/test variants, **not** to work around\noverloaded interface names. If `poly check` reports Error 106 (multi-impl\ninterfaces), the fix is to rename interfaces, not to add a profile.\n\n### Correct patterns for \"multiple implementations\"\n\n**Pattern A — Multimethod dispatch inside a canonical interface component**\n\nOne component owns the canonical interface and a `(defmulti ... dispatch-fn)`.\nOther components register backend methods against that multimethod. Each\nbackend-provider component still has its OWN unique interface name.\n\n```\ncomponents/tool-pubsub/             interface: :tool-pubsub  (canonical + defmulti)\ncomponents/tool-pubsub-coreasync/   interface: :tool-pubsub-coreasync\ncomponents/adapter-pubsub-gcp/      interface: :adapter-pubsub\n```\n\nNo two components share `:poly/interface`. Projects pick which backend\ncomponent to include; runtime dispatch resolves the active implementation.\n\n**Pattern B — Adapter components with distinct interfaces**\n\nEach adapter exposes its own interface; a consumer depends on the interface\nit actually wants. Swapping happens by changing which adapter the project\nincludes, not by overloading.\n\n**Pattern C — Extension, not replacement**\n\nA specialization component (e.g., `phase-software-factory`) gets its OWN\ninterface (`:phase-software-factory`) and depends on the canonical\n`:phase` interface. It extends, rather than claiming to re-implement, the\nbase interface.\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n**Bad**: `phase`, `phase-deployment`, and `phase-software-factory` all declare\n`:poly/interface :phase` in their `deps.edn`. `poly check` reports Error 106.\nAdding a profile does not fix this — it hides the conflict.\n</example>\n\n<example>\n**Good**: `phase` keeps `:phase` and owns shared multimethods. `phase-deployment`\ndeclares `:phase-deployment`. `phase-software-factory` declares\n`:phase-software-factory`. Projects include whichever they need; `poly check`\nreports zero multi-impl errors.\n</example>\n\n## Build Tasks\n\n```bash\nbb test             # Unit tests only (bricks)\nbb test:integration # Project integration tests\nbb test:all         # Full suite (unit + integration)\n```",
               :id :std/polylith,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "310",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (310): Apply Polylith architecture conventions for component organization and test placement"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Enforce Polylith architectural integrity — bricks hold production logic, interfaces are contracts, validation gates workflows",
               :agent-behavior
               "Preserve Polylith architectural integrity by enforcing brick-oriented production\ncode, interface-based composition, boundary isolation in bases, and\nproject-context validation/testing. Polylith is not just a folder layout — it\nencodes an architectural model that only holds if components are reusable,\nbases are boundary adapters, interfaces are stable contracts, and workflows are\ngated by structural validation.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Enforce Polylith architectural integrity — bricks hold production logic, interfaces are contracts, validation gates workflows"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["components/**"
                 "bases/**"
                 "projects/**"
                 "development/**"
                 ".github/workflows/**"
                 "workspace.edn"
                 "deps.edn"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Polylith Architecture and Composition (Rule `arch.polylith.structure-and-composition`)\n\n## Intent\n\nPreserve Polylith architectural integrity by enforcing brick-oriented production\ncode, interface-based composition, boundary isolation in bases, and\nproject-context validation/testing. Polylith is not just a folder layout — it\nencodes an architectural model that only holds if components are reusable,\nbases are boundary adapters, interfaces are stable contracts, and workflows are\ngated by structural validation.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Production code belongs in bricks\n\nProduction application logic MUST live in bases and components. Deployable\nprojects MUST NOT become general-purpose homes for production business logic.\n\n- Reusable business logic MUST be implemented in components.\n- Boundary and entrypoint logic MUST be implemented in bases.\n- Projects SHOULD primarily compose bricks and configure deployment/runtime concerns.\n\n<critical>\nDo NOT introduce production namespaces under `projects/*/src`. Do NOT use\nproject namespaces as a substitute for missing components. Do NOT duplicate\nproduction logic across projects instead of extracting a component.\n</critical>\n\n---\n\n## 2. Bases are boundary adapters\n\nA base MUST act as a bridge between the outside world and component interfaces.\n\nBases MAY own: application entrypoints, framework adapters, transport wiring,\nrequest/response translation, infrastructure-facing orchestration, side-effect\ninitiation at system boundaries.\n\n- Bases MUST delegate business behavior to components.\n- Bases SHOULD isolate framework-specific or runtime-specific concerns.\n\n<critical>\nBases MUST NOT become the primary home of reusable business logic. Bases MUST\nNOT depend on internals of other components. Bases MUST NOT be treated as\nshared libraries.\n</critical>\n\n---\n\n## 3. Components are accessed only through interfaces\n\nA component MUST be consumed through its interface namespace(s), not through\nits internal implementation namespaces. **This is an inviolable boundary.**\n\n- Cross-component calls MUST target interface namespaces only.\n- Component internals MAY change freely as long as interface contracts hold.\n- Interface namespaces SHOULD define a small, intentional public contract.\n\n<critical>\nNever reach into another brick's `src` namespaces. Never bypass an interface\nbecause it is \"simpler\" or \"faster\" during local development. Polylith's\n`poly check` Error 101 exists to catch this — it must pass.\n</critical>\n\n---\n\n## 4. Interfaces are contracts\n\nAn interface change MUST be treated as a contract change.\n\n- Interface changes SHOULD trigger broader review and impact analysis.\n- Compatibility-sensitive changes SHOULD be made explicit in review metadata.\n- Component public surface area SHOULD stay small and intentional.\n- Do NOT expose internal helpers as public interface for convenience.\n- Do NOT make silent interface changes without updating dependents.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Development code belongs in the development workspace\n\nScratch code, REPL support, exploratory integration, and temporary developer\nutilities SHOULD live under the `development/` workspace rather than being\nembedded in production bricks.\n\n- Experimental or workflow-driving dev code SHOULD start in `development/`.\n- Only promoted, intentional production code SHOULD move into bricks.\n- Do NOT leave temporary exploratory code inside production bricks.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation is a workflow gate\n\nWorkspace validation MUST succeed before deeper automation proceeds. A\nstructurally invalid workspace MUST stop downstream workflows.\n\n<critical>\n`poly check` (or equivalent structural validation) MUST pass before test,\nreview, release, or packaging workflows continue. Validation failures are\nhard failures, never informational warnings.\n</critical>\n\nPolicy pipelines SHOULD surface illegal dependencies and structural violations\nearly. Do NOT continue release preparation when validation fails.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Testing reflects project composition\n\nBrick tests MUST be evaluated in the context of the deployable projects that\ninclude those bricks. Testing only in local isolation is insufficient when\nthe shipped artifact is a project composition.\n\n- Brick tests MUST stay colocated with the brick they validate.\n- Test execution SHOULD reflect affected deployable project contexts.\n- Project-level tests SHOULD validate composed runtime behavior.\n\n**Prefer real composition over excessive mocking.** Mock only true externals,\nunstable boundaries, or impractical side effects. Use the workspace's\ncomposability to test integrated behavior directly where feasible.\n\nDo NOT rely exclusively on isolated unit tests when deployable composition is\nthe actual contract. Do NOT replace ordinary component collaboration with\nmocks by default.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Selective CI/release scope must be change-derived\n\nBuild, test, and release scope SHOULD be derived from changed and affected\nprojects relative to a **stable reference point** — a tag where the workspace\nwas known-good.\n\n- CI SHOULD use stable or release tags as comparison anchors.\n- Changed-or-affected projects SHOULD determine selective scope.\n- Do NOT deploy arbitrary project subsets without impact analysis.\n- Do NOT fall back to full-repo rebuilds when change analysis is available.\n- Do NOT use \"most recently touched file\" as a release heuristic.\n\n---\n\n## Enforcement\n\n### Hard-fail (errors)\n1. Production business logic introduced under `projects/*/src`.\n2. Cross-component dependency on non-interface namespaces (`poly check` Error 101).\n3. Validation failure prior to test/release workflow continuation.\n4. Illegal dependency edges between bricks.\n5. Release/build targeting that ignores changed-or-affected analysis where the workflow contract requires it.\n\n### Advisory (warnings, may escalate)\n1. Excessive domain logic in bases.\n2. Overgrown interfaces with convenience exports.\n3. High-mock tests where real composition is feasible.\n4. Exploratory code left in production bricks without promotion intent.\n5. Project-level orchestration that substitutes for a missing component.\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n<example>\n**Compliant**\n- A project composes `bases/api` with `components/user`, `components/auth`, `components/db`.\n- `bases/api` translates HTTP and delegates domain actions to component interfaces.\n- `components/user` exposes a small interface namespace; implementation stays private.\n- CI identifies affected projects since the last stable tag and tests those compositions.\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n**Non-compliant**\n- Business logic added under `projects/customer-app/src`.\n- `components/order` directly requires `components/inventory.src.core`.\n- `bases/web` contains large amounts of reusable domain logic that belongs in a component.\n- Release workflow proceeds despite failed workspace validation.\n- Test workflow validates only isolated namespaces without checking affected project compositions.\n</example>\n\n---\n\n## Agent implications\n\n### Implementation agent\n- MAY create or modify code in bases and components.\n- MUST NOT introduce business logic into deployable projects.\n- MUST preserve interface boundaries across components.\n\n### Review agent\n- MUST inspect dependency edges across bricks.\n- MUST fail changes that bypass component interfaces.\n- SHOULD elevate review scope when interfaces change.\n\n### Test agent\n- MUST validate affected bricks in project context.\n- SHOULD distinguish brick-level from project-level test intent.\n- SHOULD prefer real component composition where practical.\n\n### Release agent\n- MUST require successful validation before release progression.\n- SHOULD derive release scope from changed-and-affected projects relative to a stable tag.\n- MUST NOT treat structurally invalid workspaces as releasable.",
               :id :std/polylith-composition,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "311",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (311): Enforce Polylith architectural integrity — bricks hold production logic, interfaces are contracts, validation gates workflows"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Treat Polylith as an architectural control tool — preflight reasoning, canonical workflow, role-specific operational requirements",
               :agent-behavior
               "The Polylith tool governs **legal structural changes** in a Polylith workspace.\nAgents MUST treat Polylith as a structural reasoning and validation tool, not\nmerely as a code generation context.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Treat Polylith as an architectural control tool — preflight reasoning, canonical workflow, role-specific operational requirements"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["components/**"
                 "bases/**"
                 "projects/**"
                 "development/**"
                 "workspace.edn"
                 "deps.edn"
                 ".github/workflows/**"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Polylith Workspace Architecture Tool (MDC `mdc.tool.polylith`)\n\n## Purpose\n\nThe Polylith tool governs **legal structural changes** in a Polylith workspace.\nAgents MUST treat Polylith as a structural reasoning and validation tool, not\nmerely as a code generation context.\n\nIt is used to inspect workspace structure; identify bricks and projects;\ndetermine where new logic belongs; enforce interface-based composition;\nvalidate dependency legality; determine affected projects; support\nproject-context testing; and gate downstream review, release, and packaging\nworkflows.\n\nThis MDC is the **operational companion** to the normative rule at\n`frameworks/polylith-composition` (dewey 311). The rule defines what is\nnormatively true; this MDC defines how agents must behave to comply. If the\nMDC conflicts with the normative rule, the rule wins.\n\n---\n\n## When agents MUST invoke Polylith reasoning\n\nBefore any of the following, agents MUST use this MDC:\n\n- adding new production functionality\n- modifying component interfaces\n- introducing or changing cross-brick dependencies\n- creating a new component or base\n- deciding whether a change belongs in a base, component, project, or development\n- running workspace validation\n- determining test or release scope\n- explaining project impact of a change\n- reviewing PRs that modify workspace structure\n\n## When agents MUST NOT proceed without this reasoning\n\n<critical>\nAgents MUST NOT proceed to implementation when:\n- ownership of the change is unclear\n- a component interface might need to change\n- a new cross-brick dependency might be introduced\n- a deployable project appears to be gaining production code\n- the change may affect multiple projects\n- a release or test workflow depends on workspace validity\n</critical>\n\nIn any of these, perform the preflight reasoning below first.\n\n---\n\n## Inviolable invariants (summary)\n\nFull detail in `frameworks/polylith-composition` (dewey 311).\n\n1. Production logic belongs in bricks — not `projects/*/src`.\n2. Bases are boundary adapters — not homes for reusable domain logic.\n3. Components are consumed only through interfaces.\n4. Interface changes are contract changes.\n5. Structural validation is a hard gate before downstream workflows.\n6. Testing must reflect deployable project composition.\n7. Release scope must be change-derived against a stable reference point.\n\n---\n\n## Required preflight reasoning\n\nBefore implementing a non-trivial change, answer these questions. Include\nthe answers in planning metadata or review output when feasible.\n\n### Ownership\n- Which existing brick owns this responsibility?\n- If none does, is a new component or base warranted — or would that just be convenient?\n\n### Placement\n- Is this reusable production logic, boundary logic, project composition, or dev-only?\n- Should it live in a component, base, project, or `development/`?\n\n### Interface impact\n- Does this require a new interface function?\n- Is it purely internal to the component?\n- Will it alter a contract used by other bricks?\n\n### Dependency legality\n- Will this introduce a cross-brick dependency?\n- If so, does that dependency target an interface namespace?\n- Does this create an illegal architectural edge?\n\n### Project impact\n- Which deployable projects include the changed brick?\n- Which projects are affected transitively?\n- Which tests and workflows must run because of that impact?\n\n### Validation readiness\n- What structural checks must pass before the change is valid?\n- What evidence will demonstrate the workspace remains compliant?\n\n---\n\n## Canonical workflow\n\n1. **Inspect the workspace** — relevant components, bases, projects, interfaces, current dependency shape.\n2. **Determine ownership** — existing brick, new brick (with justification), `development/`, or project composition only.\n3. **Determine contract impact** — interface change, implementation-only change, composition change, or affected-project expansion.\n4. **Implement only in legal locations** — components for reusable behavior, bases for boundary concerns, `development/` for dev-only, projects for composition/configuration.\n5. **Validate structure** — run `poly check` (or equivalent) before progressing.\n6. **Validate behavior** — brick tests + affected-project tests + project-level runtime tests as appropriate.\n7. **Report impact** — changed bricks, interface changes, affected projects, validation status, test scope executed, residual risk.\n\n---\n\n## Operational requirements by agent role\n\n### Implementation agents\n- MUST determine owning brick before writing code.\n- MUST prefer modifying an existing brick over creating a new one without reason.\n- MUST preserve interface boundaries.\n- MUST NOT place production business logic in deployable projects.\n- MUST NOT import another component's implementation namespaces.\n- SHOULD minimize interface surface area.\n- SHOULD keep bases thin and delegating.\n\n### Review agents\n- MUST inspect new and changed cross-brick dependencies.\n- MUST fail changes that bypass component interfaces.\n- MUST flag production logic introduced under `projects/*/src`.\n- MUST elevate review scope when interfaces change.\n- SHOULD identify bases that have accumulated reusable domain logic.\n- SHOULD distinguish implementation-only changes from contract changes.\n\nReview output SHOULD state whether: interface boundaries were preserved,\nownership placement was correct, any illegal dependency edges exist, and\nproject impact scope is accurate.\n\n### Test agents\n- MUST validate changed bricks in the context of affected projects where material.\n- MUST distinguish brick-level tests from project-level tests.\n- SHOULD prefer real component composition over excessive mocking.\n- SHOULD mock only true external boundaries or impractical side effects.\n- MUST NOT claim completeness if only isolated namespace testing was performed when project composition is material.\n\nTest output SHOULD state: changed bricks tested, affected projects\nconsidered, whether project-context validation was performed, and known\ngaps between isolated and composed testing.\n\n### Release agents\n- MUST require successful structural validation before release progression.\n- SHOULD determine scope from changed-and-affected projects relative to a stable or release tag.\n- MUST NOT treat a structurally invalid workspace as releasable.\n- MUST NOT release arbitrary project subsets without impact reasoning.\n\nRelease output SHOULD state: comparison anchor used, changed projects,\naffected projects, tests/validation completed, and remaining release risk.\n\n---\n\n## Allowed and disallowed moves\n\n### Allowed\n- Add reusable domain logic to an existing component.\n- Add a new interface function to support intentional component collaboration.\n- Add boundary translation logic to a base.\n- Create a new component when responsibility does not fit an existing one.\n- Move exploratory code into `development/` first.\n- Derive release scope from affected-project analysis.\n- Widen review/test scope when an interface changes.\n\n### Disallowed\n- Add general production logic under `projects/*/src`.\n- Import another component's internal implementation namespace.\n- Use a base as a shared business-logic utility library.\n- Create a new brick without checking for an existing owner.\n- Bypass structural validation because local tests passed.\n- Assume isolated tests are sufficient when project composition is the delivery contract.\n- Release based only on touched files without affected-project reasoning.\n\n---\n\n## Tool-facing structured outputs\n\nWhen this MDC is used, agents SHOULD produce structured outputs that include\nas many of the following as are relevant. These are especially useful for\ncompilation into EDN policy or workflow evidence.\n\n- `owningBrick`\n- `changeKind` — one of `implementation` | `interface` | `base-boundary` | `project-composition` | `development-only`\n- `newDependencies`\n- `illegalDependencies`\n- `affectedProjects`\n- `requiresValidation`\n- `requiresProjectContextTesting`\n- `stableReference`\n- `releaseImpact`\n- `architecturalRiskNotes`\n\n---\n\n## Recommended prompt framing\n\nWhen an agent is about to make a change in a Polylith workspace, it SHOULD\ninternally frame the task as:\n\n> Determine the owning brick, determine whether the interface contract\n> changes, preserve legal dependency edges, validate workspace structure,\n> and verify affected project behavior.\n\nThat framing is architecturally correct. Do NOT frame as:\n\n> Add the requested code somewhere appropriate.\n\nThat framing erodes the model over time.\n\n---\n\n## Example decision patterns\n\n<example>\n**Adding new reusable business behavior**\n\nCorrect:\n- Identify owning component.\n- Extend component internals.\n- Add or modify interface only if needed.\n- Update affected callers through the interface.\n- Validate structure.\n- Test in affected project context.\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n**Adding new reusable business behavior — anti-pattern**\n\n- Add a helper directly to a project namespace.\n- Import component internals from another brick.\n- Skip impact analysis because local tests passed.\n</example>\n\n<example>\n**Adding a new HTTP endpoint**\n\nCorrect:\n- Add route and translation logic in the relevant base.\n- Delegate domain behavior to a component interface.\n- Keep business rules in a component.\n- Test endpoint behavior in project context.\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n**HTTP endpoint — anti-pattern**\n\n- Implement endpoint and business logic entirely in the base.\n- Duplicate domain behavior already present in another component.\n</example>\n\n<example>\n**Temporary integration logic during exploration**\n\nCorrect:\n- Place temporary orchestration or exploratory workflow code under `development/`.\n- Promote only intentional, production-worthy logic into a brick later.\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n**Exploration — anti-pattern**\n\n- Leave exploratory code embedded in a production component or project indefinitely.\n</example>\n\n---\n\n## Detection signals (for policy compilation)\n\nCompilers or downstream policy systems MAY derive enforcement signals such as:\n\n- new namespace under `projects/*/src`\n- import of non-interface namespace from another brick\n- interface namespace modification (elevates review scope)\n- base namespace accumulating excessive domain behavior\n- missing workspace validation step before downstream workflow\n- missing affected-project analysis in release/test pipeline\n- mismatch between changed bricks and executed project-context tests\n\nThese signals support enforcement of the architecture rule but do not\nsubstitute for architectural reasoning.",
               :id :std/polylith-tool,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "312",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (312): Treat Polylith as an architectural control tool — preflight reasoning, canonical workflow, role-specific operational requirements"}
        #:rule{:title "PR documentation guidelines",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. **Automatically create** a PR doc file in `docs/pull-requests/`\n2. **Use the current date** (YYYY-MM-DD format)\n3. **Transform branch name** (replace `/` with `-`)",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message "Standard: PR documentation guidelines"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Pull Request Documentation Convention\n\n## Purpose\n\nMaintain structured, version-controlled documentation for all pull requests to provide context for:\n\n- Future developers and AI assistants\n- Historical reference and pattern analysis\n- Migration tracking and decision documentation\n- Architectural evolution understanding\n\n## Directory Structure\n\n```zsh\ndocs/\n└── pull-requests/\n    ├── 2026-01-04-migrate-protocols-to-composition-pattern.md\n    ├── 2026-01-15-feat-add-fsm-component.md\n    └── 2026-02-01-refactor-workflow-unit-tests.md\n```\n\n## Naming Convention\n\n**Format**: `YYYY-MM-DD-branch-name.md`\n\n**Components**:\n\n- `YYYY-MM-DD`: Date when PR was created (ISO 8601 date format)\n- `branch-name`: Git branch name (replace `/` with `-`)\n\n**Examples**:\n\n- Branch `feat/add-module` → `2026-01-18-feat-add-module.md`\n- Branch `fix/security-issue` → `2026-01-18-fix-security-issue.md`\n\n## File Location\n\n**Standard path**: `docs/pull-requests/YYYY-MM-DD-branch-name.md`\n\n**Why this location?**\n\n- Clear semantic meaning (`docs/` for documentation)\n- Easy to find and browse\n- Automatically included in repository searches\n- Available as context for AI assistants\n- Version controlled alongside code changes\n\n## When to Create\n\n**AUTOMATIC**: Create a PR doc immediately when creating a new feature branch.\n\n**Trigger conditions** (any of these):\n\n1. Creating a new branch with prefix: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `migrate/`, `chore/`\n2. Running `git checkout -b <branch-name>`\n3. User mentions creating a branch for new work\n\n**Skip for**:\n\n- Branches named `main`, `nonprod`\n- Trivial typo fixes (single commit, <5 lines changed)\n- Automated dependency updates (dependabot, renovate)\n\n## Required Content\n\n### Minimum Sections\n\n```markdown\n# [Conventional Commit Prefix]: [Title]\n\n## Overview\nBrief summary of what this PR does and why.\n\n## Motivation\nWhy is this change needed? What problem does it solve?\n\n## Changes in Detail\nList of specific changes, organized by category or component.\n\n## Testing Plan\nHow will this be validated? What tests were run?\n\n## Deployment Plan\nHow and when will this be deployed? Any special considerations?\n\n## Related Issues/PRs\nLinks to related work, issues, or dependencies.\n\n## Checklist\n- [ ] Task 1\n- [ ] Task 2\n```\n\n### Optional Sections (Use When Relevant)\n\n- **Architecture Changes**: Before/after diagrams or explanations\n- **Breaking Changes**: What breaks and migration path\n- **Rollback Plan**: How to undo if needed\n- **Performance Impact**: Expected performance changes\n- **Security Considerations**: Security implications\n- **Documentation Updates**: What docs were updated\n- **Dependencies**: Other PRs that must merge first\n\n## Lifecycle: Living Documents\n\n**PR docs are living documents** that evolve throughout the PR lifecycle:\n\n| Phase | Action |\n|----|-----|\n| **Creation** | Create doc when starting significant work |\n| **During Development** | Update as scope changes, decisions are made, or new learnings emerge |\n| **Before Merge** | Ensure doc reflects final state of the PR |\n| **After Merge** | Doc becomes static historical record |\n\n**Key principle**: The PR doc should always reflect the current understanding of the PR. If you add a feature,\n                   fix a bug discovered during review, or change approach, update the doc.\n\n## Agent Behavior\n\n**CRITICAL**: When you see or execute `git checkout -b <branch>` for a feature branch, **immediately create the PR doc**\n              before any other work. This is automatic, not optional.\n\nWhen creating a PR or preparing to open one:\n\n1. **Automatically create** a PR doc file in `docs/pull-requests/`\n2. **Use the current date** (YYYY-MM-DD format)\n3. **Transform branch name** (replace `/` with `-`)\n4. **Include comprehensive content**:\n   - Clear overview and motivation\n   - Detailed changes list\n   - Testing and deployment plans\n   - Checklists for validation\n5. **Reference in commits**: Mention the PR doc in the final commit message\n6. **Continuously update**: Keep the doc current as the PR evolves - it's a living document until merged\n\n### Example Workflow\n\n```bash\n# When user says: \"Let's create a PR for this migration\"\n1. Create: docs/pull-requests/2026-01-04-migrate-protocols-to-composition-pattern.md`\n2. Write comprehensive PR description in that file\n3. Commit the file along with the changes:\n   git add docs/pull-requests/2026-01-04-migrate-protocols-to-composition-pattern.md`\n4. Reference in commit message:\n   \"feat: migrate protocols (see docs/pull-requests/2026-01-04-migrate-protocols-to-composition-pattern.md')\"\n```\n\n## Repository Maintenance\n\n### Cleanup Policy\n\n**Keep all PR docs** - they serve as historical record.\n\nIf the repository grows too large (>100 PR docs):\n\n- Move old docs to `docs/pull-requests/archive/YYYY/` by year\n- Keep recent year in root `docs/pull-requests/`\n\n### .gitignore\n\n**Do NOT ignore** these files. They should be committed to git.\n\nEnsure `.gitignore` does NOT contain:\n\n```zsh\ndocs/pull-requests/\n*.md\n```\n\n## Benefits\n\n✅ **Context for AI**: LLMs can read historical PRs to understand patterns\n✅ **Knowledge Transfer**: New team members understand past decisions  \n✅ **Pattern Documentation**: Repeated patterns become obvious\n✅ **Migration Tracking**: Easy to see what's been migrated and how\n✅ **Decision Record**: Why decisions were made, not just what changed\n✅ **Searchable**: Can grep through PR docs for specific topics\n✅ **Template Source**: Previous PRs serve as templates for similar work\n\n## Anti-Patterns to Avoid\n\n❌ **Don't** create PR docs in the PR description only (GitHub UI) - put them in the repo\n❌ **Don't** use arbitrary names like `PR_DESCRIPTION.md` without context\n❌ **Don't** skip the date prefix - chronological sorting is important  \n❌ **Don't** delete PR docs after merge - they're historical record\n❌ **Don't** make PR docs excessively long - aim for clarity over completeness\n\n## Examples\n\n### Good PR Doc Names\n\n- `2026-01-04-migrate-protocols-to-composition-pattern.md`\n- `2026-01-15-feat-add-fsm-component.md`\n- `2026-02-01-refactor-workflow-unit-tests.md`\n\n### Bad PR Doc Names\n\n- `PR.md` (no context)\n- `description.md` (no date, no branch)\n- `my-changes.md` (not descriptive)\n- `temp-pr-notes.md` (implies temporary)\n\n## Cross-Repository Consistency\n\nThis convention applies to **all** repositories\n\nUse the **same structure and naming** across all repos for consistency.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/pr-documentation,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "721",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (721): PR documentation guidelines"}
        #:rule{:title
               "PR Layering — decompose work into stratified, incremental pull requests",
               :agent-behavior
               "Once you understand all work needed, organize PRs as a stratified DAG. Each PR is small, incremental, and layers on top of more foundational work. Merge from the bottom up.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: PR Layering — decompose work into stratified, incremental pull requests"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# PR Layering (ALWAYS)\nOnce you understand all work needed, organize PRs as a stratified DAG. Each PR is small, incremental, and layers on top of more foundational work. Merge from the bottom up.\n\n## Core principle\n**Build the foundation before the floors.** Just as code dependencies flow downward through strata, PR dependencies should flow upward—foundations merge first, then domain, then application, then adapters.\n\n## PR decomposition strategy\n\n### 1. Map the work to strata\nBefore writing code, categorize all changes by stratum:\n- **Foundations PR(s)**: Types, schemas, constants, pure utilities\n- **Domain PR(s)**: Business rules, policies, invariants (pure functions)\n- **Application PR(s)**: Use-cases, orchestration, port definitions\n- **Adapter PR(s)**: Controllers, CLI handlers, event listeners\n- **Infrastructure PR(s)**: DB implementations, external service clients\n\n### 2. Order PRs bottom-up\nCreate PRs in dependency order so each can merge independently:\n\n```\nPR 1: Foundations  ──┐\nPR 2: Domain       ──┼──► PR 4: Adapters\nPR 3: Application  ──┘         │\n                               ▼\n                        PR 5: Integration / wiring\n```\n\n### 3. Keep each PR atomic\n- **One stratum per PR** (preferred) or closely related strata\n- **< 400 lines** of meaningful change (tests don't count toward limit)\n- **Single reviewable concern**: a PR should answer one question—\"Does this type work?\", \"Is this business rule correct?\", \"Does this adapter translate properly?\"\n- **Independently mergeable**: each PR compiles, passes tests, and leaves main in a working state\n\n## PR description template\nWhen opening a layered PR, include:\n\n```markdown\n## Layer\n[Foundations | Domain | Application | Adapter | Infrastructure]\n\n## Depends on\n- #<PR-number> (title) — [merged | open]\n\n## What this adds\nBrief description of the incremental capability.\n\n## Strata affected\n- `namespace.or.module` — what changed and why\n```\n\n## Agent behavior (Cursor)\nWhen planning a feature or refactor:\n\n1. **Produce a PR plan first** — before writing code, output:\n   - The list of PRs by stratum\n   - The dependency DAG between PRs\n   - Estimated size (S/M/L) for each\n\n2. **Work bottom-up** — implement and propose changes for the lowest stratum first. Don't start on adapters until domain is solid.\n\n3. **Flag scope creep** — if a change in one stratum requires touching a higher stratum, split it out into a follow-up PR.\n\n4. **Suggest branch strategy** — for stacked PRs, recommend:\n   ```\n   main\n    └── feat/user-auth-types       (PR 1: Foundations)\n         └── feat/user-auth-domain (PR 2: Domain)\n              └── feat/user-auth-api (PR 3: Adapter)\n   ```\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n- **Monolith PRs**: 1000+ line PRs touching multiple strata—split them.\n- **Top-down PRs**: Starting with adapters, then \"backfilling\" domain—invert the order.\n- **Circular PR dependencies**: PR A needs PR B, and B needs A—refactor the design.\n- **Orphan PRs**: PRs that can't merge because a dependency is stuck—unblock or re-scope.\n- **Speculative foundations**: Adding types/utilities \"just in case\"—only add what the next stratum needs.\n\n## Benefits\n- **Faster reviews**: Reviewers focus on one layer at a time.\n- **Lower risk**: Small merges = small blast radius.\n- **Parallel work**: Multiple devs can work on different strata once interfaces are defined.\n- **Clear rollback**: If adapters break, domain and foundations remain intact.\n- **Documentation**: The PR chain itself documents the architecture.\n\n## Example: Adding user authentication\n\n| PR | Stratum | Contents | Depends on |\n|----|---------|----------|------------|\n| 1 | Foundations | `User`, `Credentials`, `AuthToken` types | — |\n| 2 | Domain | `validate-credentials`, `hash-password`, `verify-token` | PR 1 |\n| 3 | Application | `AuthService` port, `LoginUseCase`, `RefreshTokenUseCase` | PR 2 |\n| 4 | Infrastructure | `PostgresUserRepo`, `JWTTokenService` | PR 3 |\n| 5 | Adapter | `/login`, `/refresh` HTTP handlers | PR 3, PR 4 |\n\nEach PR is reviewable in isolation. Foundations can merge to main immediately; each subsequent layer merges once its dependencies land.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/pr-layering,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "722",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (722): PR Layering — decompose work into stratified, incremental pull requests"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Pre-commit hook discipline and bypass constraints",
               :agent-behavior
               "**Pre-commit hooks enforce quality gates. Bypassing them violates miniforge's self-validation principle.**",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Pre-commit hook discipline and bypass constraints"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Pre-Commit Hook Discipline (ALWAYS)\n\n## Core Principle\n\n**Pre-commit hooks enforce quality gates. Bypassing them violates miniforge's self-validation principle.**\n\nThe pre-commit hook runs:\n- **Linting** (clj-kondo for Clojure)\n- **Formatting** (markdownlint for Markdown)\n- **Tests** (full test suite via `bb pre-commit`)\n- **Email validation** (must use @miniforge.ai)\n\nFailures indicate **real problems** that must be fixed, not bypassed.\n\n---\n\n## NEVER Bypass Without Investigation\n\n**CRITICAL**: When `bb pre-commit` or the git pre-commit hook fails, **NEVER** immediately use `git commit --no-verify`.\n\n### Required Investigation Steps (Before Bypass)\n\nWhen a pre-commit hook fails, you MUST:\n\n1. **Read the full error output** - Don't assume what's failing\n2. **Check what's staged** - Run `git diff --cached --stat` to see exactly what files are staged\n3. **Identify the root cause:**\n   - Are you staging unintended files? (e.g., `git add -A` in a dirty worktree)\n   - Are there legitimate test failures?\n   - Are there linting errors in your new code?\n   - Is there a pre-existing issue unrelated to your changes?\n4. **Fix the root cause:**\n   - If staging too many files: `git reset` and stage only your changes\n   - If test failures: Fix the tests or the code causing them\n   - If linting errors: Fix the code\n   - If pre-existing issues: Fix them in your PR (you're improving the codebase)\n\n### Bypass is Only Allowed When\n\nUse `git commit --no-verify` **ONLY** when **ALL** of these conditions are met:\n\n1. ✅ You've completed all investigation steps above\n2. ✅ You've documented the **specific reason** in the commit message with `[BYPASS-HOOKS: reason]`\n3. ✅ The failure is **environmental** (e.g., missing tools, permission issues) and **NOT** code quality\n4. ✅ Manual validation confirms:\n   - Tests pass: `clojure -M:poly test` (or equivalent)\n   - Linting passes: `bb lint:clj` for staged Clojure files\n   - Formatting passes: `bb fmt:md` for staged Markdown files\n5. ✅ You're committing to a feature branch (never bypass on main)\n6. ✅ CI will catch the issue if you're wrong\n\n### Bypass Commit Message Format\n\nWhen bypass is necessary, use this exact format:\n\n```bash\ngit commit --no-verify -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nfeat: your commit message here\n\n[BYPASS-HOOKS: reason]\nPre-commit hook failed due to: <specific environmental reason>\n\nManual validation:\n- Tests passed: clojure -M:poly test (exit code 0)\n- Linting passed: bb lint:clj (exit code 0)\n- Formatting passed: bb fmt:md (exit code 0)\n\nRoot cause: <what actually failed and why>\nWhy bypass: <why fixing it isn't possible right now>\nCI validation: Will be enforced by CI workflow\nEOF\n)\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## Anti-Patterns to Reject\n\n❌ **\"Pre-commit is broken in this worktree\"** - Investigate why, don't bypass\n❌ **\"Tests pass manually but hook fails\"** - Investigate what the hook is doing differently\n❌ **\"It's taking too long\"** - Speed is not a valid reason\n❌ **\"CI will catch it anyway\"** - That's wasteful; fix it now\n❌ **\"It's a minor warning\"** - Fix minor warnings; they accumulate into major debt\n❌ **\"I'll fix it in the next commit\"** - Fix it in THIS commit\n❌ **\"The hook suggests I can bypass\"** - The hook also says \"BUT YOU REALLY SHOULDN'T!\"\n\n---\n\n## Agent Behavior (Cursor)\n\n### When Pre-Commit Fails\n\n1. **STOP** - Don't immediately try `--no-verify`\n2. **READ** the full error output (don't truncate)\n3. **CHECK** staging: `git diff --cached --stat`\n4. **IDENTIFY** the specific failure:\n   - What command failed? (lint/format/test)\n   - What files are affected?\n   - Is it your new code or pre-existing?\n5. **FIX** the root cause:\n   - If staging issue: Reset and stage correctly\n   - If test failure: Fix the test or code\n   - If linting: Fix the code\n   - If pre-existing: Fix it in your PR\n6. **VERIFY** the fix: Re-run the specific failing command\n7. **COMMIT** normally (no `--no-verify`)\n\n### When to Alert the User\n\nIf you discover:\n- Pre-existing test failures blocking your commit\n- Linting errors in files you didn't touch\n- Environmental issues preventing hook execution\n\nAlert the user with:\n1. **What's failing** (specific command and error)\n2. **Why it's failing** (root cause analysis)\n3. **Proposed fix** (correct solution, not bypass)\n4. **Impact** (blocks commit until resolved)\n\nAsk the user for guidance on how to proceed.\n\n### Red Flags in Your Reasoning\n\nIf you find yourself thinking:\n- \"The hook might have environmental issues\"\n- \"Tests passed manually, so it's probably fine\"\n- \"We can bypass this once\"\n- \"CI will catch it\"\n\n**STOP.** You're about to make a poor decision. Go back to investigation steps.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-Commit Hook Failures are Learning Opportunities\n\nWhen the hook fails, it's teaching you:\n- You're staging too many files\n- Your code has quality issues\n- There are pre-existing problems to fix\n- Your git workflow needs refinement\n\n**Embrace the failure, investigate, and fix.** Don't bypass your way around learning.\n\n---\n\n## Exception: True Emergencies\n\nIn a true emergency (production down, security incident), document extensively:\n\n```bash\ngit commit --no-verify -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nhotfix: emergency fix for production outage\n\n[BYPASS-HOOKS: EMERGENCY]\nProduction is down. Incident: INC-12345\nImpact: 100% of users affected\nTime sensitivity: Every minute costs $$$\n\nThis commit:\n- Rolls back problematic deployment\n- Restores service immediately\n- Will be followed by proper fix with tests in PR #XXX\n\nPre-commit bypassed to restore service ASAP.\nPost-incident review will happen in INC-12345.\nEOF\n)\"\n```\n\nEmergencies are **RARE**. Feature development is never an emergency.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Situation | Action | Tool |\n|-----------|--------|------|\n| Pre-commit fails | Investigate root cause | Read errors, check staging |\n| Staging issue | Fix staging | `git reset`, stage correctly |\n| Test failure | Fix test/code | Debug, fix, re-test |\n| Linting error | Fix code | Address linter feedback |\n| Environmental issue | Document & bypass OR fix environment | `--no-verify` with extensive docs |\n| Emergency | Document extensively & bypass | `--no-verify` with incident details |\n| **Default** | **Fix the issue** | **Never bypass** |\n\n**When in doubt: Don't bypass. Investigate and fix.**",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/pre-commit-discipline,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "715",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (715): Pre-commit hook discipline and bypass constraints"}
        #:rule{:title "Python + Poetry rules",
               :agent-behavior
               "- Use Poetry and `pyproject.toml`; add console scripts for CLIs under\n  `[tool.poetry.scripts]`.\n- Source layout: `src/<package>/`; never implicit namespace packages.\n- Pin a minimum Python version in `pyproject.toml` (`python = \"^3.12\"`).\n- Separate runtime deps from dev deps (`[tool.poetry.group.dev.dependencies]`).\n- Lock file (`poetry.lock`) is committed; CI runs `poetry install --frozen`.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message "Standard: Python + Poetry rules"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.py"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Python style guide\n\n## Project structure\n\n- Use Poetry and `pyproject.toml`; add console scripts for CLIs under\n  `[tool.poetry.scripts]`.\n- Source layout: `src/<package>/`; never implicit namespace packages.\n- Pin a minimum Python version in `pyproject.toml` (`python = \"^3.12\"`).\n- Separate runtime deps from dev deps (`[tool.poetry.group.dev.dependencies]`).\n- Lock file (`poetry.lock`) is committed; CI runs `poetry install --frozen`.\n\n## Type annotations\n\n- All public functions carry complete type annotations.\n- `from __future__ import annotations` at the top of every file\n  (enables forward references without import cycles).\n- Run `mypy --strict` on production modules; disable only with a\n  per-line comment and a WHY comment explaining the exception.\n- Prefer `X | None` over `Optional[X]`; prefer `X | Y` over `Union[X, Y]`.\n\n## Imports\n\n- No star imports (`from module import *`).\n- One import per line inside each import block.\n- Order: stdlib → third-party → local. `ruff` enforces this.\n- Never use `importlib.import_module` or `__import__` for runtime\n  dynamic dispatch — fix the design instead (see\n  `languages/clojure-no-requiring-resolve` for the same principle in Clojure).\n\n## Naming\n\n- `snake_case` for modules, functions, variables, parameters.\n- `PascalCase` for classes and type aliases.\n- `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for module-level constants (see\n  `foundations/named-constants` — the same intent-bearing rule applies).\n- Private helpers: single leading underscore (`_helper`); no double\n  underscores except for genuine dunder methods.\n- No abbreviations unless universally understood (`url`, `id`, `db`).\n\n## Functions and pure design\n\n- Write pure functions wherever possible: same inputs → same outputs,\n  no implicit state reads.\n- Keep each function to one level of abstraction (Stratified Design —\n  `foundations/stratified-design`).\n- No mutable default arguments:\n\n  ```python\n  # BAD\n  def append(item, lst=[]):\n      lst.append(item)\n      return lst\n\n  # GOOD\n  def append(item: object, lst: list[object] | None = None) -> list[object]:\n      result = lst if lst is not None else []\n      result.append(item)\n      return result\n  ```\n\n- Prefer data classes over plain dicts for structured data:\n\n  ```python\n  from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n  @dataclass(frozen=True)\n  class JobRecord:\n      job_id: str\n      url: str\n      status: str\n  ```\n\n  `frozen=True` enforces immutability; drop it only when mutation is\n  genuinely required and document why.\n\n## Error handling\n\n- Raise specific exceptions, not bare `Exception` or `BaseException`.\n- Never silence exceptions with a bare `except:` or `except Exception: pass`. At minimum log the error and re-raise, or return an explicit failure value.\n- Chain exceptions with `raise NewError(\"...\") from original` so the\n  traceback is preserved.\n- At I/O boundaries, validate inputs before doing work and raise\n  `ValueError` / `TypeError` with a human-readable message (see\n  `foundations/validation-boundaries`).\n- For recoverable failures, prefer returning a value that encodes\n  success/failure over exceptions (aligns with\n  `foundations/result-handling`):\n\n  ```python\n  # Preferred at internal boundaries\n  def parse_config(raw: str) -> tuple[Config, None] | tuple[None, str]:\n      ...\n  ```\n\n- Do not use `try`/`except` for flow control.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Framework: `pytest -q`; no `unittest.TestCase` unless wrapping legacy code.\n- Test files mirror the source tree: `src/foo/bar.py` → `tests/foo/test_bar.py`.\n- One `assert` per logical assertion; use `pytest.raises` for expected exceptions.\n- Use `@pytest.fixture` for shared setup; keep fixtures small and focused.\n- No `#` commented-out test functions — delete them or open an issue.\n- Parameterize repeated cases with `@pytest.mark.parametrize` rather than\n  hand-rolled loops.\n- Never `@pytest.mark.skip` without a tracking issue reference in the reason string.\n- Test module-level constants and pure functions directly; mock only at\n  explicit I/O boundaries.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n| Avoid | Use instead |\n|-------|-------------|\n| `except Exception: pass` | explicit failure value or re-raise |\n| Mutable default arguments | `None` sentinel |\n| `print()` for logging | `logging` or `structlog` |\n| `os.system()` / `subprocess.call()` with shell=True | `subprocess.run([...])` with `shell=False` |\n| Global mutable state | pass state explicitly or use a context object |\n| `type()` equality check | `isinstance()` |\n| Magic string literals throughout code | module-level constants (Dewey 006) |\n| `raise NewError(...)` without chaining | `raise NewError(...) from original` |\n\n## Linting and formatting\n\n- `ruff` for linting and formatting (replaces flake8 + black + isort).\n- `mypy --strict` for type checking.\n- Add both to CI; pre-commit hooks enforce both locally.\n- `pyproject.toml` is the single config source for all tools:\n\n  ```toml\n  [tool.ruff]\n  line-length = 100\n  select = [\"E\", \"F\", \"I\", \"UP\", \"B\", \"SIM\"]\n\n  [tool.mypy]\n  strict = true\n  ```",
               :id :std/python,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "220",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (220): Python + Poetry rules"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Result handling — use succeeded?/failed? predicates and success/anomaly constructors",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Use `schema/success` or `schema/failure` — never `{:success? true ...}`.\n2. When checking a result from a called function, use `schema/succeeded?` or `schema/failed?` — never `:success?` key access.\n3. If the result type comes from a different component, check what predicates that component's interface exports. If none exist, that's a gap to flag.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Hand-built result map or structural success/failure check. Use the success/anomaly constructors and the succeeded?/failed? predicates; never inspect a result's shape."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Result Handling (ALWAYS)\n\nOperations that can fail return a **result map**. Always use the provided predicates and constructors — never build result maps by hand or reach into their structure directly.\n\n## Predicates over structure inspection\n\n**Never** examine the shape of a result to determine success or failure. Use the predicates.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — reaching into the result map's internals\n(if (:success? result)\n  ...)\n\n(when (= :ok (:status result))\n  ...)\n\n(when (nil? (:error result))\n  ...)\n\n;; GOOD — use predicates\n(if (schema/succeeded? result)\n  ...)\n\n(when (schema/succeeded? result)\n  ...)\n```\n\nThe internal shape of a result map is an **implementation detail**. Code that depends on `:success?` being a boolean key breaks when the result format evolves. Predicates are the stable API.\n\n## Constructors over hand-built maps\n\n**Never** construct result maps with `{:success? true ...}` or `{:status :error ...}` literals. Use the provided constructors.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — hand-building result maps\n{:success? true  :data value  :errors nil}\n{:success? false :error \"Something went wrong\"}\n{:success? false :errors [{:msg \"invalid\"}]}\n\n;; GOOD — use schema constructors\n(schema/success :data value {:errors nil})\n(schema/failure :data error-message)\n(schema/failure-with-errors :data [{:msg \"invalid\"}])\n```\n\nConstructors enforce the canonical shape. They are also self-documenting: `(schema/failure-with-errors :pack errors)` communicates intent; `{:success? false :pack nil :errors errors}` does not.\n\n### Factory functions for any repeated map shape\n\nThis rule generalizes beyond result/anomaly. **Any map shape constructed\nin more than one place must have a factory function.** Hand-built map\nliterals across files drift — keys get added in one site and forgotten\nin another, and grep can't find them.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — same event envelope built by hand in three sites\n;; src/agent/streaming.clj\n{:event/type :agent/chunk\n :event/id (random-uuid)\n :event/timestamp (now)\n :workflow/id workflow-id\n :chunk/delta delta}\n\n;; src/agent/tool_call.clj\n{:event/type :agent/tool-call\n :event/id (random-uuid)\n :event/timestamp (now)\n :workflow/id workflow-id\n :tool/name tool-name}\n\n;; src/agent/error.clj\n{:event/type :agent/error\n :event/id (random-uuid)\n ;; ↑ someone forgot :event/timestamp here. Compiler doesn't catch it.\n :workflow/id workflow-id\n :error/message msg}\n\n;; GOOD — factory function owns the envelope shape\n(defn- agent-event\n  \"Build an :agent/* event envelope. All :agent/* events share the\n   envelope keys; `body-map` carries the per-type payload and is\n   merged in last.\"\n  [event-type workflow-id body-map]\n  (merge {:event/type      event-type\n          :event/id        (random-uuid)\n          :event/timestamp (now)\n          :workflow/id     workflow-id}\n         body-map))\n\n(agent-event :agent/chunk     workflow-id {:chunk/delta delta})\n(agent-event :agent/tool-call workflow-id {:tool/name tool-name})\n(agent-event :agent/error     workflow-id {:error/message msg})\n```\n\nThe factory is the single place to add a new envelope key or change\nthe timestamp source. Drift across call sites is impossible. The\nfactory's signature also documents which keys are envelope and which\nare body-specific — information that's invisible in the duplicate\nliterals.\n\n## Propagation — thread results through, don't unwrap\n\nWhen passing a result to a caller, propagate the result map — don't unwrap it and re-wrap it.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — unwrapping and re-wrapping\n(let [{:keys [success? data error]} (load-pack path)]\n  (if success?\n    {:ok true :pack data}\n    {:ok false :msg error}))\n\n;; GOOD — propagate\n(let [result (load-pack path)]\n  (if (schema/succeeded? result)\n    (process (:pack result))\n    result))  ; return the failure as-is for the caller\n```\n\n## What counts as an \"anomaly\"\n\nUse anomaly constructors (failure forms) for:\n- Validation failures (invalid input at a boundary)\n- Missing required resources (file not found, entity not found)\n- Downstream errors caught at a boundary\n\nDo NOT wrap expected nil return values as anomalies. A function that legitimately returns nil for \"not found\" is not failing — it's answering the question.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing code that returns a result:\n1. Use `schema/success` or `schema/failure` — never `{:success? true ...}`.\n2. When checking a result from a called function, use `schema/succeeded?` or `schema/failed?` — never `:success?` key access.\n3. If the result type comes from a different component, check what predicates that component's interface exports. If none exist, that's a gap to flag.\n4. When propagating failures up the call stack, return the result map unchanged — don't add new keys or translate the error.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- `(when (:success? result) ...)` — use predicate\n- `(assoc result :success? false)` — use constructor\n- `(if (nil? (:error result)) ...)` — use predicate\n- `(not (:failed? result))` — use `(succeeded? result)`\n- Mixing result-map checks with exception handling in the same function — separate concerns",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/result-handling,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "003",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (003): Result handling — use succeeded?/failed? predicates and success/anomaly constructors"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Use ALWAYS when asked to CREATE A RULE or UPDATE A RULE or taught a lesson from the user that should be retained as a new rule",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Pick the right Dewey category (see `## Dewey Ranges` below) and put the\n2. Choose activation. Either `alwaysApply: true` (injected into every\n3. Author the body to the `## Core Structure` template. Keep it tight — see",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Use ALWAYS when asked to CREATE A RULE or UPDATE A RULE or taught a lesson from the user that should be retained as a new rule"},
               :applies-to {:phases #{}, :file-globs ["**/*.mdc"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# LLM Rules Format\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen asked to create or update a rule, or to capture a lesson learned as a\nnew rule:\n\n1. Pick the right Dewey category (see `## Dewey Ranges` below) and put the\n   numeric code in frontmatter (`dewey: \"NNN\"`), never in the filename or\n   path.\n2. Choose activation. Either `alwaysApply: true` (injected into every\n   prompt) or `globs: [...]` (scoped to matching files). A rule with\n   neither is never injected.\n3. Author the body to the `## Core Structure` template. Keep it tight — see\n   `## Formatting Guidelines` and `## AI Context Efficiency` for length and\n   token-density expectations.\n4. Add a `## Agent behavior` section yourself if the rule prescribes a\n   distinct agent workflow; the loader uses it (or the first body\n   paragraph) as the prompt-injected directive.\n5. Update the master index (`index.mdc`) and the consuming repo's\n   `CLAUDE.md` quick-reference table.\n\n## Core Structure\n\n```mdc\n---\ndewey: \"NNN\"\ndescription: ACTION when TRIGGER to OUTCOME\n# Activation: include at least one; both are allowed together.\nalwaysApply: true           # inject into every prompt regardless of file context\n# globs:\n#   - \"src/**/*.clj\"        # scope to matching files (combine with alwaysApply if needed)\n---\n\n# Rule Title\n\n## Context\n- When to apply this rule\n- Prerequisites or conditions\n\n## Requirements\n- Concise, actionable items\n- Each requirement must be testable\n\n## Examples\n<example>\nGood concise example with explanation\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\nInvalid concise example with explanation\n</example>\n```\n\n## File Organization\n\n### Location\n\n- Root: repo root (consumed as `.standards/` git submodule in product repos)\n- Extension: `.mdc`\n- Structure: `<category-slug>/<rule-slug>.mdc`\n- Dewey code: in frontmatter (`dewey: \"NNN\"`), not in the filename or path\n\n```\n<standards-root>/           # repo root (or .standards/ when used as submodule)\n├── index.mdc               # Master catalog\n├── foundations/            # dewey 000-099\n├── languages/              # dewey 200-299\n├── frameworks/             # dewey 300-399\n├── testing/                # dewey 400-499\n├── documentation/          # dewey 600-699\n├── workflows/              # dewey 700-799\n├── project/                # dewey 800-899\n└── meta/                   # dewey 900-999\n```\n\n### Dewey Ranges\n\n```\n000-099  Foundations & Core Principles\n100-199  Development Environment & Tools\n200-299  Languages\n300-399  Frameworks & Platforms\n400-499  Testing & Quality\n500-599  Operations & Infrastructure\n600-699  Documentation\n700-799  Workflows & Processes\n800-899  Project-Specific\n900-999  Meta & Templates\n```\n\nPrivate rules: `_name.mdc` (underscore prefix)\n\n### Glob Pattern Examples\n\nCommon glob patterns for different rule types:\n\n- All rules: `**/*.mdc`\n- Language rules: src/**/*.{js,ts}\n- Testing standards: **/*.test.{js,ts}\n- React components: src/components/**/*.tsx\n- Documentation: docs/**/*.md\n- Configuration files: *.config.{js,json}\n- Build artifacts: dist/**/*\n- Multiple extensions: src/**/*.{js,jsx,ts,tsx}\n- Multiple files: dist/**/*, docs/**/*.md\n\n## Frontmatter Fields\n\nEvery rule file opens with a YAML frontmatter block. The full set of recognised\nfields is listed below. Keep the block minimal — include only fields the rule\nactually needs.\n\n### Always required\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| `dewey` | string | Dewey classification number, e.g. `\"210\"`. Quoted. |\n| `description` | string | Activation hint in ACTION TRIGGER OUTCOME format. Aim for <120 chars when feasible; never truncate to the point of losing meaning. |\n\n### Activation — include at least one; both permitted together\n\n| Field | Type | When to use |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| `alwaysApply` | boolean | `true` — inject rule into every agent prompt regardless of file context. Use for universal principles (stratified design, result handling, etc.). `false` — explicit opt-out (rare; most rules without `alwaysApply` simply omit it). |\n| `globs` | string or list | Path patterns scoping the rule to matching files. Use when the rule is language- or file-type-specific. A rule with neither `alwaysApply: true` nor `globs` is effectively never injected. |\n\nRules may carry both `alwaysApply: true` and `globs` (e.g. `languages/clojure.mdc`\ncombines universal injection with a glob that helps IDE tooling narrow context).\n\n### Automated-fix fields (optional)\n\nUsed by lint runners and agent auto-fix pipelines. Only include when the rule\nhas a mechanical fix that can be applied programmatically.\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| `detection.mode` | `positive` \\| `negative` | `positive` — flag matches; `negative` — flag absences. |\n| `detection.pattern` | string | Regex the runner applies against file content. |\n| `detection.emailPattern` | string | Secondary regex (e.g. for copyright checks). |\n| `enforcement.action` | `hard-halt` \\| `require-approval` \\| `warn` \\| `audit` | How a pack-derived gate treats a violation. `hard-halt` **blocks** (fails the gate); the others are non-blocking (recorded / surfaced). Omit to default — `warn` for `alwaysApply` rules, `audit` for advisory ones. A blocking rule compiles at `:major` severity or higher. |\n| `enforcement.message` | string | Override the default `\"Standard: <title>\"` violation message. |\n| `remediation.strategy` | string | `mechanical` (auto-apply) or `manual`. |\n| `remediation.replacement` | string | Replacement expression (supports `$N` capture groups). |\n| `remediation.type` | string | `prepend`, `append`, `replace`, etc. |\n| `remediation.template` | string | Full text to insert when `remediation.type` is `prepend`/`append`. |\n| `remediation.autoFixable` | boolean | `true` if the runner may apply the fix without confirmation. |\n| `remediation.excludePathContains` | list | Skip files whose path contains any of these substrings. |\n| `remediation.excludeCurrentContains` | list | Skip when the matched line already contains any of these strings. |\n\n### Discouraged / non-standard fields\n\n`title`, `category`, `tags`, `confidence`, `impact` — occasionally appear in\nolder rules (e.g. `workflows/git-worktrees.mdc`). These are not processed by\nany loader; avoid adding them to new rules.\n\n### Body\n\n- <version>X.Y.Z</version>\n- context: Usage conditions\n- requirements: Actionable items\n- examples: Both valid and invalid\n\n## Formatting Guidelines\n\n- Use Concise Markdown primarily\n- XML tags limited to:\n  - <example>\n  - <danger>\n  - <required>\n  - <rules>\n  - <rule>\n  - <critical>\n  - <version>\n- Always indent content within XML or nested XML tags by 2 spaces\n- Keep rules as short as possible\n- Use Mermaid syntax if it will be shorter or clearer than describing a complex rule\n- Use Emojis where appropriate to convey meaning that will improve rule understanding by the AI Agent\n- Keep examples as short as possible to clearly convey the positive or negative example\n\n## Authoring discipline — self-consistency\n\nA rule is normative the moment it merges; an error in it propagates to every agent that\nreads it. Before opening a rule PR, verify the document against itself:\n\n- **Example code is normative — trace it as if compiled.** Readers copy it verbatim. Apply\n  the same scrutiny a real diff gets: walk the types, the nil/default paths, the return value.\n  A markdown fence is not an excuse to skip review. (PR #39: an `Int` example claimed an\n  `Int64` round-trip in prose but returned `n.intValue` — a doc/code drift inside the example.)\n- **Every summary, \"what not to do,\" and catalog line must agree with the detailed rule it\n  condenses.** Re-read the section as a whole and check each short restatement against the body;\n  a punchy summary that contradicts the rule is worse than no summary. (PR #36: a docstring rule\n  said both \"delete redundant docstrings\" and \"preserve docstrings.\" PR #39: a summary implied\n  `as? Bool`-first was a safe type test when the body said it was ambiguous.)\n- **No internal contradictions.** When a rule states a constraint in more than one place\n  (frontmatter description, body, examples, cross-references), they must say the same thing.\n\nThis is the rule-authoring application of `code-review-rigor` obligations 4 (\"First-run /\ndefault-value / nil-path scrutiny\") and 5 (\"Intent, not symptoms\"): the document is the artifact\nunder review.\n\n## AI Optimization Tips\n\n1. Use precise, deterministic ACTION TRIGGER OUTCOME format in descriptions\n2. Provide concise positive and negative example of rule application in practice\n3. Optimize for AI context window efficiency\n4. Remove any non-essential or redundant information\n5. Use standard glob patterns without quotes (e.g., *.js, src/**/*.ts)\n\n## AI Context Efficiency\n\n1. Aim for frontmatter description under 120 characters while maintaining clear intent for rule selection by AI Agent; never truncate to the point of losing meaning\n2. Limit examples to essential patterns only\n3. Use hierarchical structure for quick parsing\n4. Remove redundant information across sections\n5. Maintain high information density with minimal tokens\n6. Focus on machine-actionable instructions over human explanations\n\n<critical>\n  - NEVER include verbose explanations or redundant context that increases AI token overhead\n  - Keep file as short and to the point as possible BUT NEVER at the expense of sacrificing\n    rule impact and usefulness for the AI Agent.\n  - Frontmatter must contain only fields from the schema above. Do NOT invent new fields.\n  - Every rule must have `dewey` and `description`. Every rule must have either `alwaysApply`\n    or `globs` (or both) — a rule with neither is never injected.\n  - `detection.*` and `remediation.*` fields are only appropriate when a mechanical fix exists.\n</critical>",
               :id :std/rule-format,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "900",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (900): Use ALWAYS when asked to CREATE A RULE or UPDATE A RULE or taught a lesson from the user that should be retained as a new rule"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Reject bind mounts of host container-runtime sockets into the capsule",
               :agent-behavior
               "Bind-mounting `/var/run/docker.sock` (or an equivalent runtime socket)\ninto a capsule grants the container full control over the host's\ncontainer daemon. A task that escapes its capsule via the socket can\nlaunch arbitrary containers, read every other capsule's filesystem, and\nignore every other isolation control miniforge applies.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Reject bind mounts of host container-runtime sockets into the capsule"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Runtime: no host docker socket (HARD-STOP)\n\nBind-mounting `/var/run/docker.sock` (or an equivalent runtime socket)\ninto a capsule grants the container full control over the host's\ncontainer daemon. A task that escapes its capsule via the socket can\nlaunch arbitrary containers, read every other capsule's filesystem, and\nignore every other isolation control miniforge applies.\n\nThis is the single most catastrophic capsule-isolation failure mode.\nReject it on sight.\n\n## Detection\n\nReject any execution-plan `:mounts` entry whose `:host-path` matches:\n\n- `/var/run/docker.sock`\n- `/run/docker.sock`\n- `/var/run/podman/podman.sock`\n- `/run/podman/podman.sock`\n- `/var/run/containerd/containerd.sock`\n- `/run/containerd/containerd.sock`\n- Any path ending in `docker.sock`, `podman.sock`, or `containerd.sock`\n\nThe check is host-path-based; bind-mount target inside the capsule does\nnot matter. A user who renames the mount target to `/etc/passwd` is\nstill mounting the host socket.\n\n## Enforcement\n\n- **Action:** `:hard-stop`\n- **Phases:** `:plan`, `:implement`, `:verify`, `:release`\n- **Scope:** every execution plan; no opt-out, no exceptions\n\n## Why no opt-out\n\nIf a workflow legitimately needs to control the host's runtime, the\nruntime adapter is the wrong layer for that. Add a host-side capability\nexplicitly (e.g. an `:executor :host-runtime-broker` with its own\nauthentication and audit trail). Mounting the socket bypasses every\naudit point miniforge has.\n\n## Spec reference\n\n- N11-delta §6 `:runtime/no-host-docker-socket`\n- N11 §2.2 (capsule filesystem boundary)\n- N6 (evidence & provenance — host-socket access has no provenance story)",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/runtime-no-host-docker-socket,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "030",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (030): Reject bind mounts of host container-runtime sockets into the capsule"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Reject execution plans whose :image-digest is anything other than a sha256:<64-hex> content digest",
               :agent-behavior
               "Container image tags are mutable. `task-runner-clojure:latest` on\nMonday is not the same image as Tuesday. A workflow that runs against\na tag has no reproducibility story — re-running the same execution\nplan produces different artifacts; evidence bundles point at content\nthat might no longer exist.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Reject execution plans whose :image-digest is anything other than a sha256:<64-hex> content digest"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Runtime: require image digest pin\n\nContainer image tags are mutable. `task-runner-clojure:latest` on\nMonday is not the same image as Tuesday. A workflow that runs against\na tag has no reproducibility story — re-running the same execution\nplan produces different artifacts; evidence bundles point at content\nthat might no longer exist.\n\nPer N6 (Evidence & Provenance) and N11-delta §5, execution plans MUST\ncarry a content-addressed image digest in the plan's `:image-digest`\nfield. The schema (`ExecutionPlanSchema` in\n`components/dag-executor/.../execution_plan.clj`) types `:image-digest`\nas `:string`; the **value** convention — enforced by this rule — is\n`sha256:<64 hex chars>`.\n\nThis rule fires on the digest field, not on image references in\ngeneral. Image references with their registry/repo path (e.g.\n`docker.io/miniforge/task-runner-clojure@sha256:...`) are passed\nseparately to the runtime CLI as the image to pull/run. The\n`:image-digest` field is the bare digest captured for evidence and\nreproducibility.\n\n## Detection\n\nInspect the execution plan's `:image-digest`. The acceptable value is\nexactly a `sha256:` digest with 64 hex chars:\n\n- `:pass` — `sha256:<64 hex chars>`\n- `:violation` — anything else (tag, full image reference, blank, malformed)\n\n```clojure\n(let [d (:image-digest plan)]\n  (if (and (string? d)\n           (re-matches #\"^sha256:[a-f0-9]{64}$\" d))\n    :pass\n    :violation))\n```\n\n## Enforcement\n\n- **Action:** `:hard-stop`\n- **Phases:** `:plan`, `:release`\n- **Scope:** every execution plan; no opt-out\n\n## Why no opt-out\n\nReproducibility and the evidence-bundle audit trail are non-negotiable\nfor governed execution. A workflow that legitimately needs the latest\nbuild of an image should resolve the tag to a digest at plan-build\ntime and write the digest into `:image-digest`; running with anything\nother than a digest defeats the audit story.\n\n## Interaction with `runtime/images.edn`\n\nThe default image references in `runtime/images.edn`\n(e.g. `docker.io/miniforge/task-runner-clojure:latest`) are image\nreferences, not `:image-digest` values. This rule does NOT fire on\nthem. It fires on the `:image-digest` that the plan-build phase writes\ninto the execution plan after resolving the reference; the\nplan-builder is responsible for performing that resolution before the\nexecutor sees the plan.\n\n## Spec reference\n\n- N11-delta §5 (image references must be fully qualified)\n- N11-delta §6 `:runtime/require-image-digest-pin`\n- N6 (evidence & provenance — digest pinning is the audit primitive)",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/runtime-require-image-digest-pin,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "033",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (033): Reject execution plans whose :image-digest is anything other than a sha256:<64-hex> content digest"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Prefer rootless container runtimes; warn or hard-stop when the resolved runtime advertises no :rootless capability",
               :agent-behavior
               "A rootless container runtime maps the in-container UID/GID to a user\nnamespace on the host, so a container break-out lands the attacker on\nthe host as the unprivileged miniforge user rather than as root. Podman\nis rootless by default; Docker requires opt-in setup.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Prefer rootless container runtimes; warn or hard-stop when the resolved runtime advertises no :rootless capability"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Runtime: require rootless\n\nA rootless container runtime maps the in-container UID/GID to a user\nnamespace on the host, so a container break-out lands the attacker on\nthe host as the unprivileged miniforge user rather than as root. Podman\nis rootless by default; Docker requires opt-in setup.\n\nMiniforge does not refuse to run under a rooted runtime — that would\nbreak Docker users on day one. It does declare the difference visible\nin the descriptor's capability set, and policy-aware deployments can\ntreat the absence of `:rootless` as a hard-stop.\n\n## Detection\n\nRead the runtime descriptor's `:runtime/capabilities` set. The check\nis data-only — no probing required.\n\n```clojure\n(if (descriptor/capable? descriptor :rootless)\n  :pass\n  :violation)\n```\n\n## Enforcement\n\n- **OSS default:** `:warn`\n- **Governed Fleet workflows:** `:hard-stop` (configurable via policy\n  pack)\n- **Phases:** `:plan`, `:release`\n\n## Why split warn / hard-stop\n\nThe OSS posture is \"Podman is recommended; Docker works\". A hard-stop\non Docker would defeat that. The fleet posture is stricter — fleet\nworkflows process external PRs and the rootless requirement is a real\ndefense-in-depth gain. Policy packs differentiate.\n\n## Spec reference\n\n- N11-delta §6 `:runtime/require-rootless`\n- N11-delta §4 (capability set)",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/runtime-require-rootless,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "031",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (031): Prefer rootless container runtimes; warn or hard-stop when the resolved runtime advertises no :rootless capability"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Restrict bind mounts of host paths to an explicit allowlist; flag everything outside for review",
               :agent-behavior
               "Every bind mount from the host into a capsule expands the capsule's\nblast radius. Mounting `/Users/$USER` exposes the user's entire home\ndirectory; mounting `/` exposes the whole filesystem. Even mounts\nintended as read-only often turn out to be writable in practice via\ninode-level operations the runtime doesn't catch.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Restrict bind mounts of host paths to an explicit allowlist; flag everything outside for review"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Runtime: restrict host mounts\n\nEvery bind mount from the host into a capsule expands the capsule's\nblast radius. Mounting `/Users/$USER` exposes the user's entire home\ndirectory; mounting `/` exposes the whole filesystem. Even mounts\nintended as read-only often turn out to be writable in practice via\ninode-level operations the runtime doesn't catch.\n\nDefault to requiring human review for host mounts that aren't on a\nsmall allowlist; let the user opt in deliberately for the cases that\nneed it.\n\n## Allowlist\n\nA bind mount is allowed without review when its `:host-path` is one\nof:\n\n- The miniforge workspace directory (resolved at execution-plan build\n  time)\n- The miniforge secret-material mount points (resolved similarly)\n- The shared model cache directory (`~/.miniforge/model-cache` or the\n  configured equivalent)\n\nAnything else falls into review.\n\n## Detection\n\nFor each `:mounts` entry, check `:host-path` against the allowlist.\nMounts whose host path is outside the allowlist trigger this rule.\n\n```clojure\n(when (and (:host-path mount)\n           (not (allowlisted-host-path? (:host-path mount))))\n  :review)\n```\n\n## Enforcement\n\n- **Action:** `:review` (do not auto-block; surface to a human in the\n  control plane decision card)\n- **Phases:** `:plan`, `:release`\n\n## Why review, not hard-stop\n\nSome workflows legitimately mount custom paths — a build cache, a\nshared dataset, a fixture directory. Hard-stopping every non-allowlist\nmount would force every such workflow into the allowlist-extension\nprocess, which is high-friction. Review surfaces the decision to a\nhuman; the allowlist captures the patterns that recur.\n\n## Spec reference\n\n- N11-delta §6 `:runtime/restrict-host-mounts`\n- N11 §2.2 (capsule filesystem boundary)",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/runtime-restrict-host-mounts,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "032",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (032): Restrict bind mounts of host paths to an explicit allowlist; flag everything outside for review"}
        #:rule{:title "Rust style and idioms",
               :agent-behavior
               "See also: `foundations/stratified-design` and `foundations/simple-made-easy` — those rules apply in Rust\nwithout exception. This file covers Rust-specific idioms that sit on top of those foundations.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message "Standard: Rust style and idioms"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.rs" "**/Cargo.toml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Rust style guide\n\nSee also: `foundations/stratified-design` and `foundations/simple-made-easy` — those rules apply in Rust\nwithout exception. This file covers Rust-specific idioms that sit on top of those foundations.\n\nSister rules in this set:\n\n- `languages/rust-async` (231) — async/concurrency: blocking discipline, locks across `.await`, cancellation, bounded channels, task lifecycle ownership.\n- `languages/rust-unsafe` (232) — unsafe is denied by default; SAFETY comments; isolation behind safe APIs.\n- `languages/rust-observability` (233) — `tracing` over `println!`, correlation IDs, error source-chain preservation.\n- `languages/rust-wire-protocols` (234) — wire/domain separation, versioned envelopes, validate-on-deserialize.\n- `project/rust-miniforge-shape` (835) — Miniforge architectural Rust constraints.\n\n## Toolchain and edition\n\n- **New crates MUST use Rust 2024** unless constrained by dependency or MSRV. Rust 2024 stabilized\n  with Rust 1.85.0; Cargo defaults new manifests to edition 2024.\n- **Every repo MUST define `rust-toolchain.toml`** so contributors and CI agree on the channel and\n  components without out-of-band coordination:\n\n  ```toml\n  [toolchain]\n  channel = \"stable\"\n  components = [\"rustfmt\", \"clippy\"]\n  ```\n\n  For reproducible infra/core repos, pin a concrete stable version instead of `\"stable\"` when\n  release determinism matters.\n\n- **CI MUST run the following, with `-D warnings`:**\n\n  ```bash\n  cargo fmt --all --check\n  cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings\n  RUSTFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo test --workspace --all-targets --all-features\n  RUSTDOCFLAGS=\"-D warnings\" cargo doc --workspace --all-features --no-deps\n  ```\n\n  Feature-gated code is only seen by Clippy when invoked with `--all-features` (or the matching\n  feature combination). Drift between gated and ungated code is a recurring source of breakage —\n  enforce the gate in CI.\n\n- **Workspace lints** are centralized in the root `Cargo.toml`'s `[workspace.lints]` table; every\n  crate's `Cargo.toml` says `[lints] workspace = true`. A new clippy lint (or a tightened severity)\n  is a single edit, not a sweep across every crate. Recommended baseline:\n\n  ```toml\n  [workspace.lints.rust]\n  unsafe_code = \"deny\"\n  missing_docs = \"warn\"\n\n  [workspace.lints.clippy]\n  all = \"deny\"\n  cargo = \"warn\"\n  pedantic = { level = \"warn\", priority = -1 }\n  unwrap_used = \"warn\"\n  expect_used = \"warn\"\n  panic = \"warn\"\n  todo = \"warn\"\n  unimplemented = \"warn\"\n  dbg_macro = \"deny\"\n  print_stdout = \"warn\"\n  print_stderr = \"warn\"\n  wildcard_imports = \"warn\"\n  enum_glob_use = \"deny\"\n  await_holding_lock = \"deny\"\n  ```\n\n## Crate and module structure\n\n- Use Cargo workspaces with small crates and explicit boundaries.\n- Keep domain logic in crates that have no dependency on transport or framework crates (Axum, tonic,\n  etc.). Adapters live in their own crates.\n- Keep FFI surfaces narrow, stable, and JSON-schema aligned.\n- Prefer `struct`/`enum` data modeling over trait-heavy abstractions. Reach for traits when you need\n  open polymorphism; do not create trait hierarchies as a substitute for thinking about data shapes.\n- One major type per module is a useful default; group supporting types nearby, not scattered.\n\n### Thin orchestration index modules\n\nWhen a module is the public face of a bounded area with multiple sub-surfaces, the index file\n(`<name>.rs` or `mod.rs`) routes, delegates, and re-exports. It does not also own helpers.\nSub-surfaces live in named submodules; the split is by bounded concern, not by file size.\n\n```rust\n// Good — index file routes by mode, submodules own concerns\nmod chrome;\nmod detail;\nmod monitor;\n\npub fn render(app: &App, frame: &mut Frame) {\n    match app.mode {\n        Mode::Monitor => monitor::render(app, frame),\n        Mode::Detail  => detail::render(app, frame),\n    }\n}\n\n// Bad — index file accretes helpers across surfaces\npub fn render(app: &App, frame: &mut Frame) { /* 300 lines mixing modes */ }\nfn fmt_workflow_row(...) { ... }\nfn fmt_agent_row(...) { ... }\nfn correlate_pr_state(...) { ... }\n// ...\n```\n\nReasons:\n\n- Index files document the surface set. New reviewers find the entry points without scrolling.\n- Visibility and `pub use` discipline is a design choice, not an afterthought.\n- Submodules can be moved or split without rewriting orchestration.\n\nThe underlying \"no god modules\" principle is in `foundations/stratified-design` and\n`foundations/code-quality`. The Rust-specific addendum is the index-file shape and the\n`pub use` discipline.\n\nDo not create `utils.rs` or `helpers.rs` dumping grounds. The first extraction target is a\nnamed bounded concern (`encoding`, `correlation`, `formatting`), not an unnamed bucket. If\nyou cannot name what binds the helpers together, they do not yet belong in a shared module.\n\n## Naming\n\nFollow standard Rust conventions:\n\n| Item | Convention | Example |\n|------|-----------|---------|\n| Types (struct, enum, trait) | `UpperCamelCase` | `CautionContext` |\n| Functions and methods | `snake_case` | `evaluate_regime` |\n| Local variables | `snake_case` | `family_buckets` |\n| Modules | `snake_case` | `rule_engine` |\n| Constants and statics | `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_RETRIES` |\n| Type parameters | Short `UpperCamelCase` | `T`, `Err` |\n\nUse long, descriptive names for locals — code completion makes short names a false economy. Default\nvariable names to use consistently:\n\n- `res` — result being built\n- `ctx` — context bundle\n- `acc` — accumulator\n- `it` — iteration variable when the name does not matter\n\nAvoid ad-hoc abbreviations. Use the established set (`db`, `ctx`, `acc`) or spell it out.\n\n### Conversion-method semantics\n\nConversion methods follow the Rust API Guidelines naming pattern:\n\n| Prefix | Meaning |\n|--------|---------|\n| `as_` | cheap borrowed view — no allocation, no compute |\n| `to_` | conversion that may allocate or compute |\n| `into_` | consumes `self` (or moves into a lower-level representation) |\n\n```rust\nimpl WorkflowSpec {\n    pub fn as_str(&self) -> &str { &self.id }            // borrowed view\n    pub fn to_json(&self) -> Result<String, serde_json::Error> { serde_json::to_string(self) }  // allocates\n    pub fn into_compiled(self) -> CompiledSpec { /* consumes self */ }\n}\n```\n\nMixing the prefixes confuses callers about cost and ownership at the call site.\n\n### Names that clash with keywords\n\nMangle rather than use raw identifiers (`r#`):\n\n```\ncrate  → krate\nenum   → enum_\nfn     → func\nimpl   → imp\nmod    → module\nstruct → strukt\ntrait  → trait_\ntype   → ty\n```\n\n## Data-driven policy patterns\n\nCentralize business rules as data, not scattered conditionals — the Rust application of\nsimple-made-easy's \"rules live in one place\" principle.\n\n### Context structs\n\nBundle related evaluation inputs into a named struct. Functions that share the same inputs should\nshare the same context type.\n\n```rust\n// Good: context bundles related state\nstruct CautionContext<'a> {\n    style_id: &'a str,\n    regime: &'a str,\n    family_buckets: BTreeMap<&'a str, Option<&'a str>>,\n}\n\n// Bad: six loose parameters threaded through call chains\nfn evaluate(regime: &str, conc: bool, families: &[F], confidence: &str, ...) -> R\n```\n\n### Rule tables over conditional chains\n\nReplace `if`/`match` chains that encode policy with `const` rule tables. Each entry is a\npredicate + payload. This applies equally to large `match` expressions — when the arms are\nencoding policy, pull them into a table.\n\n```rust\n// Good: policy as data\nstruct CautionRule {\n    predicate: fn(&CautionContext) -> bool,\n    message: &'static str,\n}\n\nconst CAUTION_RULES: &[CautionRule] = &[\n    CautionRule { predicate: |ctx| ctx.family_is_red(\"valuation\"), message: CAUTION_VALUATION },\n    CautionRule { predicate: |ctx| ctx.regime == \"defensive\",     message: CAUTION_EDUCATIONAL },\n];\n\nfn cautions(ctx: &CautionContext) -> Vec<String> {\n    CAUTION_RULES.iter().filter(|r| (r.predicate)(ctx)).map(|r| r.message.to_string()).collect()\n}\n\n// Bad: scattered if-chains\nif family_is_red(\"valuation\") { cautions.push(...) }\nif family_is_red(\"yield_curve\") { cautions.push(...) }\nif regime == \"defensive\" { cautions.push(...) }\n```\n\n### Resolution structs\n\nWhen a function selects from alternatives, return a named struct — not a tuple.\n\n```rust\n// Good\nstruct PlaybookResolution { regime: String, thesis: String, targets: BTreeMap<String, f64> }\n\n// Bad: positional tuple leaking internal ordering\nlet (regime, thesis, targets, id, rationale) = resolve(...);\n```\n\n### String constants\n\nAll user-facing and advisory text lives in a `strings.rs` module as `pub const`. Domain logic\nreferences `crate::strings::*` — never inline string literals in business logic.\n\n## Error handling\n\n### Library crates: use `thiserror`\n\nDefine typed error enums for each crate. `thiserror` derives `std::error::Error`, `Display`, and\n`From` with minimal boilerplate:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]\npub enum ServiceError {\n    #[error(\"Database error: {0}\")]\n    Db(#[from] DbError),\n    #[error(\"Invalid data: {0}\")]\n    InvalidData(String),\n    #[error(transparent)]\n    Serialization(#[from] serde_json::Error),\n}\n```\n\nFor layered systems, nest error enums with `#[from]` to preserve context at each boundary.\n\n### Binary crates: `anyhow` is acceptable\n\n`anyhow::Result` is appropriate in `main`, CLI entry points, and test helpers where ergonomic\npropagation matters more than precise error types. Do not use `anyhow` in library crates — it\nerases type context that callers need.\n\nUse `anyhow::format_err!` rather than `anyhow::anyhow` (less stuttering).\n\n### General error rules\n\n- Prefer `Result`; avoid `panic!` except for genuine programmer errors (`unreachable!`, `todo!`,\n  test assertions).\n- Do not use `.unwrap()` or `.expect()` in production code paths. Prefer `let Ok(x) = ... else`\n  for early returns, `?` for bubbling, or combinator methods (`unwrap_or`, `unwrap_or_else`).\n- Use `?` to propagate errors rather than verbose `match` chains.\n- Async errors that cross `.await` boundaries must implement `Send + Sync + 'static`.\n- Separate parse failures, transport failures, and stale-cache behavior explicitly — do not collapse\n  them into a single opaque error variant.\n- Test error paths: check `err.to_string()` when `PartialEq` is not available.\n\n## Type idioms\n\n### Domain newtypes at boundaries\n\nPrefer domain newtypes over raw primitives at every public boundary:\n\n```rust\npub struct WorkflowId(Uuid);\npub struct PhaseId(String);\npub struct RetryBudget(u32);\npub struct RepoRoot(Utf8PathBuf);\n\npub fn assign(workflow: WorkflowId, agent: AgentId) { /* ... */ }\n```\n\nThe compiler now refuses `assign(some_agent_id, some_workflow_id)` (arguments swapped). With raw\n`Uuid` parameters both directions compile — the type system was off duty when it mattered most.\n\nThe same principle applies to function arguments that would otherwise be bare `bool` or ambiguous\n`Option`:\n\n```rust\n// Good\nenum ExecutionMode { DryRun, Apply }\nenum ConflictPolicy { Fail, Force }\nfn run_workflow(id: WorkflowId, mode: ExecutionMode, conflicts: ConflictPolicy)\n\n// Bad — caller writes run_workflow(id, true, false) and has to remember what's what\nfn run_workflow(id: WorkflowId, dry_run: bool, force: bool)\n```\n\nClosed state machines are enums with data on the variants — not strings, not bitfields:\n\n```rust\nenum PhaseState {\n    Pending,\n    Running { started_at: OffsetDateTime },\n    Succeeded { evidence: EvidenceBundleId },\n    Failed { error: PhaseError },\n    Cancelled { reason: CancellationReason },\n}\n```\n\nInternal matches MUST be exhaustive — no `_ =>` catch-all unless the enum is `#[non_exhaustive]`\nfrom an external crate. A new variant should produce compiler pressure at every existing call\nsite; that's the type system doing its job.\n\n### Prefer general borrowed types on function boundaries\n\nUse the more general borrowed form:\n\n| Prefer | Over |\n|--------|------|\n| `&str` | `&String` |\n| `&[T]` | `&Vec<T>` |\n| `Option<&T>` | `&Option<T>` |\n| `&Path` | `&PathBuf` |\n\n### `Copy` types\n\nDerive `Copy` for small plain-data structs (up to ~3 words / 24 bytes) whose fields are all `Copy`.\nDo not derive `Copy` on types containing `String`, `Vec`, or other heap-owning fields.\n\n### Constructors\n\nPrefer `#[derive(Default)]` over a zero-argument `new()` when the zero value is correct. Avoid\ninventing \"dummy\" default states just to satisfy `Default`.\n\n### Getters\n\nIf a field has an invariant, make it `private`, enforce it in the constructor, and expose a getter\nthat returns a borrowed form:\n\n```rust\n// Good\nfn first_name(&self) -> &str { self.first_name.as_str() }\nfn middle_name(&self) -> Option<&str> { self.middle_name.as_ref() }\n\n// Bad — clones unnecessarily, or leaks container type\nfn first_name(&self) -> String { self.first_name.clone() }\nfn middle_name(&self) -> &Option<String> { &self.middle_name }\n```\n\nNever provide setters. If mutation is needed after construction, provide a builder or use\n`pub` fields when there are no invariants to protect.\n\n## Boundary commands and pipeline selection\n\nRules that govern how requests cross a boundary into a Rust crate, and how phases or modes\nselect among pure pipelines. Both rules sit on top of the data-driven policy patterns above\n— they are the boundary-layer applications of the same principle.\n\n### Typed boundary commands\n\nA boundary that accepts more than one operation MUST express the operation set as an enum\nin `contracts`. The dispatch site MUST be an exhaustive `match` over that enum with no `_`\narm. JSON or other wire formats are decoded into the typed enum at the boundary, once.\n\n```rust\n// Good — typed boundary, exhaustive match\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(tag = \"command\", content = \"params\", rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\npub enum Command {\n    Ping,\n    ListPortfolios(ListPortfoliosParams),\n    EvaluateAlerts(EvaluateAlertsParams),\n}\n\npub fn route(ctx: &Ctx, cmd: Command) -> Response {\n    match cmd {\n        Command::Ping => ping(ctx),\n        Command::ListPortfolios(p) => portfolio::list(ctx, p),\n        Command::EvaluateAlerts(p) => alerts::evaluate(ctx, p),\n    }\n}\n\n// Bad — stringly-typed dispatch with non-exhaustive match\nmatch envelope.command.as_str() {\n    \"ping\"             => ping(ctx),\n    \"list_portfolios\"  => portfolio::list(ctx, &envelope.params),\n    other              => Response::error(format!(\"unknown: {other}\")),\n}\n```\n\nReasons:\n\n- Adding a command becomes a compile error in every dispatch site until handled.\n- Parameter decoding is centralized in `Deserialize` impls, not scattered across\n  `params.get(\"...\").and_then(|v| v.as_str())` chains in handlers.\n- The wire-format string set is single-sourced from the `#[serde(rename_all)]` derive.\n\nWhen the operation list is itself dynamic (plugins, packs, pluggable adapters), prefer a\ntyped envelope wrapping a registry lookup over an open string match.\n\n### Pipeline selection by enum\n\nWhen a phase, mode, tier, or policy determines which sequence of pure functions runs,\nencode the selector as an enum and define one pipeline function per variant. Stages\nthemselves do not branch on the selector — the *pipeline function* picks the stage variant.\n\n```rust\n// Good — pipeline selection lives in one place\npub enum Phase { IngestOnly, Score, Full }\n\npub fn run(phase: Phase, ctx: &Ctx, input: Raw) -> Result<Out, PipelineError> {\n    match phase {\n        Phase::IngestOnly => ingest_only(ctx, input).map(Out::Ingest),\n        Phase::Score      => score(ctx, input).map(Out::Scored),\n        Phase::Full       => full(ctx, input).map(Out::Signal),\n    }\n}\n\nfn full(ctx: &Ctx, input: Raw) -> Result<Signal, PipelineError> {\n    let a = ingest::run(ctx, input)?;\n    let b = extract::run(ctx, a)?;\n    let c = canonicalize::run(ctx, b)?;\n    score::run(ctx, c).and_then(|s| signal::run(ctx, s))\n}\n\n// Bad — phase branching scattered through stages\nfn extract(ctx: &Ctx, x: A) -> Result<B, PipelineError> {\n    if ctx.phase == Phase::IngestOnly { return Ok(B::skipped()); }\n    if ctx.policy == InferencePolicy::LocalOnly { ... } else { ... }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nReasons:\n\n- Adding a phase = one new variant + one new function + one new arm. No grep across stages.\n- Stages stay pure and composable: their type signature is `(&Ctx, In) -> Result<Out, _>`,\n  not `(&Ctx, In, Phase, Tier, Policy) -> Result<Out, _>`.\n- Test surface is tractable — each pipeline function is a tested unit; stages are tested\n  independently of phase composition.\n\n### Bounded data-driven content\n\nWhen a static table holds *operational content* that is tuned by domain owners (numeric\nthresholds, weights, metric definitions, layout/composition metadata, selectors), it lives\nin a RON or TOML file under `<crate>/resources/`, loaded once and cached. When a table\nholds *structural* identifiers referenced from code (cluster IDs, source-type tags,\nschema-version strings), it stays as `pub const &'static str` in a Rust module.\n\nUser-facing or advisory copy is **out of scope** for this rule and stays in `strings.rs`\n(see \"String constants\" earlier). Content files reference stable keys or enum variants\ninto `strings.rs` rather than embedding display text directly.\n\nHeuristic: if a non-engineer would reasonably want to edit the value without changes to\nhandlers, it is content; move it. If editing it requires changes to handlers that key on\nit, it is structure; keep it as `const`.\n\nThe two viable load mechanisms — pick deliberately:\n\n- `include_str!` — embeds the file at compile time. The owning crate rebuilds when the\n  file changes; consumers do not re-source against new code. Use when the binary must be\n  self-contained (desktop apps, signed bundles).\n- Filesystem read at startup — file ships alongside the binary and is read at process\n  start. Edits do not require a rebuild of the owning crate. Use when ops can swap the\n  resource file without redeploying the binary.\n\nEither mechanism MUST validate at startup and fail loudly on malformed input via a typed\nerror, not via `panic`/`expect`. Lazy initialization via `OnceLock::get_or_init` is\nacceptable, but eager validation at process start is preferred so format errors surface\nbefore the first request.\n\n```rust\n// Good — typed error, eager startup validation, no expect/panic\n#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]\npub enum RegistryLoadError {\n    #[error(\"registry: malformed TOML: {0}\")]\n    Parse(#[from] toml::de::Error),\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug, Deserialize)]\nstruct RegistryFile { metric: Vec<SourceEntry> }\n\npub fn load_registry() -> Result<&'static [SourceEntry], RegistryLoadError> {\n    static REGISTRY: OnceLock<Vec<SourceEntry>> = OnceLock::new();\n    if let Some(loaded) = REGISTRY.get() {\n        return Ok(loaded.as_slice());\n    }\n    let raw = include_str!(\"../resources/registry.toml\");\n    let parsed: RegistryFile = toml::from_str(raw)?;\n    Ok(REGISTRY.get_or_init(|| parsed.metric).as_slice())\n}\n\n// Process startup calls load_registry() once and bails on error before serving traffic.\n\n// Bad — 36 hand-typed `SourceEntry { ... }` literals inside a fn\npub fn default_registry() -> Vec<SourceEntry> {\n    vec![\n        SourceEntry { id: \"shiller_pe\", warn_threshold: 30.0, alert_threshold: 35.0, ... },\n        SourceEntry { id: \"vix\", warn_threshold: 20.0, alert_threshold: 30.0, ... },\n        // 34 more\n    ]\n}\n\n// Bad — panics on malformed input, violates the \"no expect in production\" rule\npub fn registry() -> &'static [SourceEntry] {\n    static REGISTRY: OnceLock<Vec<SourceEntry>> = OnceLock::new();\n    REGISTRY.get_or_init(|| {\n        let raw = include_str!(\"../resources/registry.toml\");\n        toml::from_str::<RegistryFile>(raw).expect(\"malformed\").metric  // ← do not\n    })\n}\n```\n\nReasons:\n\n- Tunable content edits are localized to the resource file. No handler / API surface\n  changes ripple into consuming crates.\n- Domain owners can review/diff a TOML file without reading Rust.\n- Format errors fail at startup with a typed error, not at the first call site and not\n  via panic.\n- Pick of `include_str!` vs filesystem read is an explicit deployment decision rather\n  than an implicit one.\n\nConstraints:\n\n- Content files MUST be loaded once and cached. Do not re-read on every call.\n- Content files MUST be validated against the deserialized type. A malformed file is a\n  startup error returned via `Result`, not an `unwrap()`/`expect()` panic.\n- Content files MUST be checked into the repo alongside their consumer. They are not\n  configuration; they are data.\n- The load mechanism (compile-time embed vs runtime read) MUST be a deliberate choice,\n  documented at the loader.\n\n### Derived context at boundary seams\n\nWhen a boundary needs correlated data from several entity families and more than one\nsibling consumer would otherwise repeat the same join, build one immutable derived context\nonce at the seam and pass it downward.\n\nTrigger (both must hold):\n\n- response or surface shape spans **two or more entity families**, AND\n- **two or more sibling consumers** would otherwise repeat the same correlation logic.\n\nBelow the trigger, this pattern is overkill — a three-field response does not need a\nparameter object.\n\n```rust\n// Good — one builder owns the correlation; renderers take the derived view\npub struct RunDossier {\n    pub anchor: WorkflowRun,\n    pub workflows: Vec<WorkflowRun>,\n    pub agents: Vec<AgentSession>,\n    pub pending_decisions: Vec<DecisionCard>,\n}\n\npub fn build_run_dossier(anchor: &WorkflowRun, state: &SupervisoryState) -> RunDossier {\n    // collect once, sort once, correlate once\n}\n\npub fn render_summary(dossier: &RunDossier, theme: &Theme) -> Vec<Line<'static>> { /* ... */ }\npub fn render_agents(dossier: &RunDossier, theme: &Theme)  -> Vec<Line<'static>> { /* ... */ }\n\n// Bad — sibling renderers each rescan root state, each invent fallback joins\npub fn render_summary(state: &SupervisoryState, run_id: Uuid, theme: &Theme) -> Vec<Line<'static>> {\n    let workflows = state.workflows.values().filter(|w| w.correlation_id == run_id).collect();\n    let agents    = state.agents.values().filter(|a| /* same join, slightly different */).collect();\n    /* ... */\n}\npub fn render_agents(state: &SupervisoryState, run_id: Uuid, theme: &Theme)  -> Vec<Line<'static>> {\n    let agents    = state.agents.values().filter(|a| /* same join, drifted */).collect();\n    /* ... */\n}\n```\n\nConstraints:\n\n- The builder is a pure function over the authoritative state plus a small anchor input.\n- Join policy lives in the builder. Sibling consumers do not invent fallback correlation rules.\n- Downstream consumers take the context object, not root state.\n- Context types stay boundary-local. They do not become domain state, contract DTOs, or\n  cross-crate types — those have stricter compatibility rules and a derived view is the\n  wrong shape for either.\n\nReasons:\n\n- Single source of truth for the join produces consistent results across siblings; drift\n  between renderers (one filters by `correlation_id`, another by `workflow_key`) becomes\n  impossible.\n- Builder is the natural test seam: a few fixture states, a few expected dossiers, done.\n- Adding a section to the surface adds a field to the context, not a fourth full state scan.\n\nWatch for the god-context anti-shape: if the context grows fields used by exactly one\nunrelated consumer, or starts replacing domain/state boundaries, split it.\n\n## `impl Trait` vs generics vs `dyn Trait`\n\n- Prefer `impl Trait` in function arguments when the caller's concrete type is unimportant and you\n  want zero-cost static dispatch without spelling out a type parameter.\n- Use explicit `<T: Trait>` when you need to name `T` elsewhere in the signature (return type,\n  multiple parameters of the same type).\n- Reserve `dyn Trait` for cases where you genuinely need runtime polymorphism: heterogeneous\n  collections, plugin boundaries, or abstracting internals behind a stable interface. Avoid boxing\n  too early inside structs.\n- Prefer `&dyn Trait` over `Box<dyn Trait>` when ownership is not required.\n- Avoid `AsRef` polymorphism in non-library code — it inflates compile times without meaningful\n  benefit at call sites.\n\n## Functions and control flow\n\n### Preconditions in types\n\nExpress preconditions in types; do not check in the callee what the caller should have provided:\n\n```rust\n// Good — caller must supply a Walrus\nfn frobnicate(walrus: Walrus) { ... }\n\n// Bad — callee silently no-ops on None\nfn frobnicate(walrus: Option<Walrus>) {\n    let Some(walrus) = walrus else { return };\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n### Early returns\n\nUse early returns to reduce cognitive nesting. Use `return Err(...)` explicitly rather than `Err(...)?`:\n\n```rust\n// Good\nfn foo() -> Option<Bar> {\n    if !condition() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    Some(...)\n}\n```\n\n### Functions with many parameters\n\nReplace boolean flags and long parameter lists with a `Config` struct:\n\n```rust\n// Good\npub struct AnnotationConfig {\n    pub binary_target: bool,\n    pub annotate_runnables: bool,\n    pub annotate_impls: bool,\n}\n\npub fn annotations(db: &RootDatabase, file_id: FileId, config: AnnotationConfig) -> Vec<Annotation>\n\n// Bad\npub fn annotations(db: &RootDatabase, file_id: FileId, binary_target: bool,\n                   annotate_runnables: bool, annotate_impls: bool) -> Vec<Annotation>\n```\n\nDo not implement `Default` for `Config` structs; require callers to be explicit about every field.\n\n### Avoid \"doer\" objects\n\nPrefer a plain function over a struct constructed solely to call one method:\n\n```rust\n// Good\ndo_thing(arg1, arg2);\n\n// Bad\nThingDoer::new(arg1, arg2).do();\n```\n\nWhen an operation has genuine internal state, hide the struct behind a public function:\n\n```rust\npub fn do_thing(arg1: Arg1, arg2: Arg2) -> Res {\n    Ctx { arg1, arg2 }.run()\n}\n```\n\n### Helper variables\n\nIntroduce named variables for complex conditions rather than inline expressions. Single-use\nvariables are cognitively cheap and aid debugging.\n\n## Linting\n\n- Keep `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings` clean.\n- Fix warnings; do not silence them with `#[allow(...)]` unless you understand the lint and document\n  why suppression is correct. Prefer `#[expect(...)]` with a comment — it re-warns if the lint\n  becomes inapplicable.\n- Do not add `#[allow(dead_code)]` to suppress incomplete work; remove the dead code or add a\n  tracked issue.\n- Configure lint levels in `Cargo.toml` `[workspace.lints]` rather than per-file attributes.\n\n## Memory and allocation\n\n- Borrow (`&T`) rather than clone (`T::clone()`) by default. Clone deliberately when you need\n  an owned copy.\n- Do not clone inside iterator chains — use `.cloned()` or `.copied()` at the chain boundary.\n- Avoid collecting into a `Vec` when an iterator suffices.\n- For recursive collection-building functions, use an accumulator parameter instead of returning\n  and merging fresh collections on each recursion.\n- Avoid monomorphizing large functions across crate boundaries — use a thin generic wrapper that\n  delegates to a `&dyn Fn` implementation.\n\n## Dependency policy\n\nDependencies require a reason. Adding one without justification gets pushed back at review.\n\nAcceptable reasons, in order of preference:\n\n1. **Ecosystem standard** — `serde`, `tokio`, `tracing`, `thiserror`, `clap`. Removing them is\n   harder than adding them; the cost-benefit is clearly in favor.\n2. **Security-reviewed and actively maintained** — has releases, has commits, has a maintainer\n   responding to security advisories.\n3. **Large complexity reduction** — replaces hundreds of lines of fiddly logic. Document the\n   complexity-reduction claim in the dep's section of `Cargo.toml`.\n4. **Protocol or ecosystem necessity** — needed to speak HTTP/2, gRPC, Cap'n Proto, etc.\n5. **Internal-only dev/test utility** — `proptest`, `insta`, `tempfile`, `serial_test`. Put under\n   `[dev-dependencies]`, not the main set.\n\nPrefer mature crates for infra/core paths. Clever micro-crates with a half-dozen GitHub stars are\nnot the right risk profile for the workflow engine. New-crate decisions should consider:\nmaintenance signal, license, transitive-dep surface, MSRV, and feature flags.\n\n**Public dependencies are API commitments.** If a public type exposes a dependency's type\n(`pub fn parse() -> Result<Foo, third_party::Error>`), that dependency becomes part of your\nsemver surface. Either re-export it through your own type, or wrap the error in your own enum.\n\n`cargo update` cadence: lockfile updates ship via PR like any other change. Don't pin transitive\ndeps unless there's a known incompatibility; let `^` ranges do their work.\n\n## Import style\n\nGroup imports separated by blank lines in this order: `std`, external crates, `crate::`, `super::`\n/ `self::`. Re-exports should be rare and placed after imports. Avoid local `use MyEnum::*`.\n\n## Documentation\n\n- Write `///` doc comments on all public items (functions, structs, traits, enums, constants).\n- Include a `# Errors` section when a function returns `Result`, a `# Panics` section when it can\n  panic, and a `# Safety` section when it contains `unsafe`.\n- Write runnable `# Examples` in doc comments — they become `cargo test` test cases.\n- Use `//!` module/crate-level comments at the top of `lib.rs` or `mod.rs`.\n- Inline `//` comments explain *why*, not *what*. Start comments with a capital letter and end with\n  a period. Named comment kinds (`// SAFETY:`, `// CONTEXT:`, `// INVARIANT:`) signal intent.\n- Do not leave `// TODO` without a tracked issue reference.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe factory-function principle and \"tests are production code\" rule live in\n`testing/standards` (Dewey 400). This section covers the Rust mechanics on top of those.\n\n- Prefer fixture-based parity tests for contract compatibility (golden files, snapshot testing).\n- Add focused unit tests for pure domain functions: reducers, evaluators, regime logic.\n- Keep synthetic tests small and deterministic. Use `cargo insta` for snapshot tests.\n- Tests must exercise error paths, not just happy paths.\n- Do not use `#[should_panic]`; check `Err`/`None` explicitly.\n- Do not `#[ignore]` tests; assert the wrong behavior with a `// FIXME` if it is not yet correct.\n- Test snippets should be minimal — remove boilerplate not relevant to what is being tested.\n\n### `test_support` modules\n\nWhen a second test module in a crate starts hand-building the same entity shape, extract\na `test_support` module that owns canonical valid fixtures.\n\n```rust\n// crate/src/test_support.rs (or pub(crate) mod test_support; in lib.rs under #[cfg(test)])\npub(crate) fn workflow_run(id: Uuid) -> WorkflowRun { /* valid defaults */ }\npub(crate) fn pr_fleet_entry(repo: &str, number: u64) -> PrFleetEntry { /* valid defaults */ }\n```\n\nMechanics:\n\n- `pub(crate)` visibility is the default; cross-crate fixture sharing is rare and the\n  default is **not** to expose factories beyond the crate.\n- Factories produce valid defaults. Tests override only the fields relevant to the scenario,\n  using struct-update syntax: `WorkflowRun { status: WorkflowRunStatus::Failed, ..test_support::workflow_run(id) }`.\n- Shared status / priority / glyph vocabularies live in helpers, not duplicated `match`\n  ladders across test modules.\n- `test_support` is production-quality code: named constants, no copy-pasted constructors,\n  no giant anonymous maps. The same standards apply.\n- Place `test_support.rs` at the crate root for crate-wide reuse. Use `#[cfg(test)] mod\n  test_support;` if the fixtures must not be reachable from non-test builds.\n- Cross-crate test fixtures only become a separate `<crate>-test-support` workspace member\n  once duplication has persisted across two or more crates long enough to justify it.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not scatter policy conditionals across modules. See rule tables above.\n- Do not use `unwrap`/`expect` in production call paths.\n- Do not use `anyhow` in library crates.\n- Do not add `#[allow(dead_code)]` to paper over incomplete work.\n- Do not create trait hierarchies as a substitute for reasoning about data shapes.\n- Do not clone prematurely inside loops or iterator chains.\n- Do not use `Box<dyn Trait>` inside structs unless runtime polymorphism is genuinely required.\n- Do not mix domain logic with transport concerns in the same crate (see stratified-design).\n- Do not dispatch boundary commands by `match s.as_str()`. Use a typed `Command` enum in\n  `contracts` with an exhaustive match.\n- Do not branch on phase / mode / tier / policy inside individual stage functions. Encode\n  the selector as an enum and select the pipeline once at the top.\n- Do not adopt an ECS (legion, bevy_ecs, hecs, specs) for service code. Service entity\n  counts and capability shapes do not justify it. Reserve ECS for genuine real-time\n  simulation; if it appears, prefer Bevy.\n- Do not encode \"capability by component presence\". Use Rust typestate (`PhantomData<Tag>`\n  or distinct types) when behavior depends on lifecycle state.\n- Do not add a crate-wide `prelude` module that pub-uses everything. Explicit imports are\n  the convention; rust-analyzer auto-import handles the ergonomic case.\n- Do not create `utils.rs` / `helpers.rs` / `common.rs` dumping grounds. The first\n  extraction target is a named bounded concern (`encoding`, `correlation`, `formatting`).\n- Do not let an index module (`mod.rs` / `<name>.rs`) own helpers across surfaces. Index\n  modules route, delegate, and re-export.\n- Do not promote a derived context object (e.g. `RunDossier`) into domain state, contract\n  DTOs, or cross-crate types. Derived contexts stay boundary-local.\n- Do not hand-build the same entity shape across test modules. Extract a `test_support`\n  factory the second time you reach for it.",
               :id :std/rust,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "230",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (230): Rust style and idioms"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Rust async and concurrency — blocking discipline, locks across .await, cancellation, bounded channels, task lifecycle ownership",
               :agent-behavior
               "See also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. This file covers the specific failure modes\nthat arise when a Rust crate spawns tasks, awaits I/O, or shares state across threads.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Rust async and concurrency — blocking discipline, locks across .await, cancellation, bounded channels, task lifecycle ownership"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.rs" "**/Cargo.toml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Rust async and concurrency\n\nSee also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. This file covers the specific failure modes\nthat arise when a Rust crate spawns tasks, awaits I/O, or shares state across threads.\n\nApply to: every async function, every `tokio::spawn` / `tokio::select!` call site, every channel,\nevery lock, every `Arc<T>` / `Arc<Mutex<T>>`. Service code that handles control-plane semantics\n(agent supervision, phase transitions, PR monitoring, workflow cancellation) is where these rules\nmatter most — the bugs are silent, only manifest under load, and tend to cost a debugging weekend.\n\n## Do not block the async runtime\n\nNo synchronous file, network, or process I/O inside async code unless wrapped in `spawn_blocking`\nor isolated behind a documented blocking boundary. `std::thread::sleep`, blocking file reads,\nsynchronous database drivers, and CPU-bound loops longer than a few hundred microseconds all\nstarve the runtime.\n\n```rust\n// Good — file read happens on the blocking pool\nlet contents = tokio::task::spawn_blocking(move || std::fs::read_to_string(&path)).await??;\n\n// Good — async-native I/O\nlet contents = tokio::fs::read_to_string(&path).await?;\n\n// Bad — blocks the executor thread; under load, parks N tasks behind this one\nlet contents = std::fs::read_to_string(&path)?;\n```\n\nCPU-bound work that can't be made async (parsing, compression, cryptography) belongs on\n`spawn_blocking` or `rayon`. Async is for I/O wait, not for compute.\n\n## Do not hold locks across `.await`\n\nA `std::sync::Mutex` guard held across an `.await` deadlocks the moment a second task tries to\nacquire the same lock. `tokio::sync::Mutex` is await-safe but pays an allocation per acquire —\nuse it only when the lock guard genuinely needs to survive an await.\n\n```rust\n// Good — snapshot under the lock, release before awaiting\nlet snapshot = {\n    let guard = state.lock().expect(\"poisoned\");\n    guard.clone()\n};\nsome_async_io(&snapshot).await;\n\n// Bad — std lock held across .await; deadlock-prone, runtime-stalling\nlet guard = state.lock().expect(\"poisoned\");\nsome_async_io(&*guard).await;\n```\n\nWorkspace lints should enable `clippy::await_holding_lock = \"deny\"` to catch this mechanically.\n\n## Cancellation awareness\n\n`tokio::select!`, dropped futures, and `CancellationToken` can interrupt at any await point. Code\nthat uses these MUST be cancellation-aware: it must not lose data, leak state, or deadlock when\ncancellation happens.\n\nA future that's dropped mid-execution may never be polled again. Synchronous `Drop` glue on owned\nlocals **does** run (Rust drops the future, which drops owned values in scope); what's skipped is\nany cleanup that needs to be driven by the task itself — `async fn finalize()`, awaits that come\nafter the cancellation point, side effects guarded by `defer`-style patterns. Partial side effects\nalready committed before the cancellation point don't roll back.\n\n```rust\n// Good — cooperative shutdown via a oneshot signal; async finalize runs on the cooperative path\nlet join = tokio::spawn(async move {\n    loop {\n        tokio::select! {\n            _ = &mut shutdown_rx => {\n                finalize().await;       // requires the task to keep being polled\n                break;\n            }\n            tick = next_event() => { handle(tick).await; }\n        }\n    }\n});\n\n// Bad — abort() skips the task's own async cleanup path (e.g. `finalize().await`).\n// Sync `Drop` on owned locals still runs; what's lost is anything that required\n// the task itself to make progress past the cancellation point.\njoin.abort();\n```\n\nWhen you must use `abort()` as a fallback (e.g. cooperative-shutdown timeout), document **what\nasync cleanup is skipped** and what's lost as a result. Don't claim \"all cleanup runs\" — that's\nwrong — or \"no cleanup runs\" — also wrong. The precise statement is: sync `Drop` runs; async\nfinalize doesn't.\n\n## Bounded channels and backpressure\n\nUse bounded channels by default for agent/workflow orchestration. An unbounded queue with no\nbackpressure becomes the canonical \"we ran out of memory under sustained load\" failure mode.\n\n```rust\n// Good — explicit bound; producer experiences backpressure\nlet (tx, rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::channel::<Event>(1024);\n\n// Bad — unbounded; one slow consumer + a fast producer = OOM\nlet (tx, rx) = tokio::sync::mpsc::unbounded_channel::<Event>();\n```\n\nUnbounded queues are acceptable only when a documented upper bound exists elsewhere — a per-workflow\nbudget, a per-request input size limit, or a process-lifetime bound. State that bound in a comment\nnext to the channel construction.\n\n## Spawned task lifecycle ownership\n\nEvery `tokio::spawn` produces a `JoinHandle`. That handle has an owner who decides when the task\nends. The owner is one of:\n\n- **awaited** — the spawning function `.await`s the handle (or stores it in a supervisor that does).\n- **joined by a supervisor** — a long-lived owner holds the handle and joins on graceful shutdown.\n- **cancelled by token or drop** — the handle is held until a `CancellationToken` fires or the\n  owner is dropped.\n- **intentionally detached** — `drop(handle)` with a comment explaining why fire-and-forget is OK\n  (rare; typically reserved for fault-tolerant best-effort I/O like metrics emission).\n\nA spawned task with no documented owner is a bug. It WILL outlive its useful scope, log misleading\nerrors after the system has moved on, or hold open a database connection past shutdown.\n\n```rust\n// Good — task has a handle, a timeout, and a documented abort fallback\nlet mut handle = tokio::spawn(run_tailer(args));\nmatch tokio::time::timeout(SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT, &mut handle).await {\n    Ok(_) => {}\n    Err(_) => handle.abort(),  // documented fallback: cooperative finalize did not complete\n}\n\n// Bad — fire-and-forget, no handle, no termination path\ntokio::spawn(run_tailer(args));\n```\n\n## Shared state ownership\n\n`Arc<T>` means \"this value has shared ownership across multiple owners,\" not \"I couldn't satisfy\nthe borrow checker.\" Appropriate uses:\n\n- Shared immutable config or runtime handles.\n- Shared caches with internal interior mutability (`Arc<DashMap<K, V>>`).\n- Task graphs where multiple workers hold the same plan.\n\n`Arc<Mutex<T>>` is the canonical \"I've got a concurrency problem I haven't designed for\" red flag.\nBefore reaching for it, consider:\n\n1. **Message passing** — does the data flow one direction? Use channels.\n2. **Task ownership** — does one task own the state? Pass it in, return updates.\n3. **Immutable snapshots** — does the consumer just need a read view? `Arc<T>` (no Mutex).\n4. **Narrow locks** — if a lock is genuinely needed, scope it to the smallest critical section.\n\nWhen `Arc<Mutex<T>>` is the right answer, document why in a comment next to the type alias and\nkeep the held region tiny.\n\n```rust\n// Good — narrow critical section, clear ownership story\ntype SessionRegistry = Arc<Mutex<HashMap<Uuid, SessionState>>>;\n//                          ^-- shared because the supervisor + many task handlers need it\n//                              Mutex because mutation happens from multiple tasks.\n//                              Held only during insert/remove, never across .await.\n\n// Bad — Arc<Mutex<T>> as the default architecture for ordinary data flow\nstruct App {\n    config: Arc<Mutex<Config>>,           // config is immutable — drop the Mutex\n    users: Arc<Mutex<Vec<User>>>,         // append-mostly — channel + log structure\n    cache: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<K, V>>>,     // use DashMap if concurrency is real\n}\n```\n\n## Async errors\n\nErrors crossing `.await` boundaries must implement `Send + Sync + 'static` — usually a `Box<dyn\nstd::error::Error + Send + Sync>` is the easiest baseline; named error enums via `thiserror` are\npreferred when you control the type.\n\nDo not stringify errors at the await boundary. Preserve the typed error all the way to the\nreporting layer; see `languages/rust-observability` (233) for the source-chain preservation rule.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not call blocking I/O from async functions without `spawn_blocking`.\n- Do not hold `std::sync::Mutex`/`std::sync::RwLock` guards across `.await`.\n- Do not spawn tasks without a documented lifecycle owner.\n- Do not use unbounded channels for cross-task or cross-component messaging unless the bound is\n  documented elsewhere.\n- Do not reach for `Arc<Mutex<T>>` as a default architecture; message passing and task ownership\n  cover most cases.\n- Do not assume an aborted task completed its own cleanup — `abort()` drops the future, so owned\n  locals' destructors do run, but cooperative shutdown paths and async cleanup after the\n  cancellation point do not; document what's lost.\n\n## References\n\n- [Tokio `sync::Mutex` docs](https://docs.rs/tokio/latest/tokio/sync/struct.Mutex.html) — when to\n  prefer it over `std::sync::Mutex` (specifically: when a guard must survive `.await`).\n- [Comprehensive Rust — Async cancellation](https://google.github.io/comprehensive-rust/concurrency/async-pitfalls/cancellation.html) —\n  the failure modes that motivate cancellation-awareness.",
               :id :std/rust-async,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "231",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (231): Rust async and concurrency — blocking discipline, locks across .await, cancellation, bounded channels, task lifecycle ownership"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Miniforge-specific Rust shape — typed workflow state, PolicyDecision as value, structured findings, adapter traits but domain concrete, append-only evidence, core never imports UI",
               :agent-behavior
               "Project-specific architectural constraints for Rust code in Miniforge product-family repos\n(miniforge, miniforge-control, thesium-* products, fleet, etc.).",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Miniforge-specific Rust shape — typed workflow state, PolicyDecision as value, structured findings, adapter traits but domain concrete, append-only evidence, core never imports UI"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.rs" "**/Cargo.toml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Miniforge Rust shape\n\nProject-specific architectural constraints for Rust code in Miniforge product-family repos\n(miniforge, miniforge-control, thesium-* products, fleet, etc.).\n\nSits on top of the language rules:\n\n- `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms.\n- `languages/rust-async` (231), `rust-unsafe` (232), `rust-observability` (233), `rust-wire-protocols` (234).\n\nThe frame: Clippy catches idiom violations; these rules catch **semantic Rust design drift** that's\nspecific to a governed workflow-orchestration runtime. They're the layer between linting and\narchitecture review.\n\n## Workflow execution state is typed, never stringly\n\nNo `\"running\"` / `\"failed\"` / `\"done\"` state transitions in core logic. Workflow phases, agent\nsessions, policy evaluations, PR train slots — every closed state machine is an enum with data on\nthe variants. See `languages/rust` (230) \"Domain newtypes at boundaries\" for the general rule;\nthis is the Miniforge-specific application.\n\n```rust\n// Good\nenum PhaseState {\n    Pending,\n    Running { started_at: OffsetDateTime },\n    Succeeded { evidence: EvidenceBundleId },\n    Failed { error: PhaseError },\n    Cancelled { reason: CancellationReason },\n}\n\n// Bad — stringly state with implicit cross-field invariants\nstruct Phase {\n    state: String,                 // \"pending\" | \"running\" | \"done\" | \"failed\" | ...\n    error: Option<String>,         // populated only if state == \"failed\"\n    evidence_id: Option<String>,   // populated only if state == \"done\"\n}\n```\n\nThe stringly form encodes invariants (\"error present iff failed\", \"evidence present iff done\") as\nruntime contracts you have to remember in every code path. New states get added by typoing a new\nstring. Exhaustive matching on the enum form gives you compiler pressure at every existing call\nsite.\n\n## Policy decisions are values\n\nA policy decision is a structured value carrying its own justification or rejection reason. Not\n`bool`. Not `(bool, String)`. Not `Option<String>`. Not `Result<Allow, Deny>` (because review\nisn't an error).\n\n```rust\npub enum PolicyDecision {\n    Allow {\n        evidence: Vec<EvidenceRef>,\n    },\n    Deny {\n        violations: Vec<PolicyViolation>,\n    },\n    RequiresReview {\n        questions: Vec<ReviewQuestion>,\n    },\n}\n```\n\nThree first-class outcomes — `Allow`, `Deny`, `RequiresReview` — with the data each needs.\nDownstream consumers match exhaustively; adding a fourth outcome (e.g. `Defer`) lights up every\ncall site at compile time.\n\nOperator visibility: every decision serializes losslessly to evidence; the audit trail can replay\nexactly which decision was made and why.\n\n## Mechanical detection returns structured findings\n\nLinters, policy checks, drift detectors, attention derivers — anything that produces a \"finding\"\nabout an artifact — emits a structured `Finding`, not a string.\n\n```rust\npub struct Finding {\n    pub rule_id: RuleId,\n    pub severity: Severity,\n    pub span: Option<SourceSpan>,\n    pub message: DiagnosticMessage,\n    pub evidence: Vec<EvidenceRef>,\n}\n```\n\nRenderers downstream (TUI, web dashboard, FFI to native app, exported reports) format the finding\nper the consumer's needs. The producer's contract is the struct shape, not the rendered text.\n\n`DiagnosticMessage` is itself localized via the project's `mfc-strings` framework — never a raw\nEnglish literal. See `foundations/localization` (050).\n\n## Adapters behind traits; domain policy concrete\n\nUse traits for I/O boundaries:\n\n```rust\npub trait GitProvider: Send + Sync {\n    async fn fetch_pr(&self, repo: RepoId, pr: PrNumber) -> Result<Pr>;\n    async fn open_pr(&self, draft: PrDraft) -> Result<Pr>;\n}\n\npub trait LlmClient: Send + Sync { /* ... */ }\npub trait ArtifactStore: Send + Sync { /* ... */ }\npub trait AgentAdapter: Send + Sync { /* ... */ }   // Miniforge agent supervision adapters\n```\n\nThese have multiple real implementations (GitHub vs GitLab vs fake-for-tests; Claude vs Codex vs\nOpenCode; S3 vs local fs; claude-code-session vs codex-session vs bash-shell). The trait is the\nseam where alternative implementations actually exist.\n\n**Domain policy is concrete.** Avoid abstracting core domain logic behind traits unless multiple\nreal implementations exist. A `PolicyClassifier` trait with one production impl and a test mock is\na smell — it adds dispatch overhead, indirection in every call site, and a fake in every test, for\nzero benefit. Production has one classifier. The \"what if we want to swap\" is hypothetical and\nwould be cheap to add later if it ever became real.\n\n```rust\n// Good — pure function, no trait\npub fn classify_fix(violation: &PolicyViolation) -> FixClassification { /* ... */ }\n\n// Bad — speculative trait surface with one implementation\npub trait PolicyClassifier: Send + Sync {\n    fn classify_fix(&self, violation: &PolicyViolation) -> FixClassification;\n}\npub struct DefaultPolicyClassifier;  // the only real impl\n```\n\n## Evidence and audit structs are append-only compatible\n\nThe Miniforge audit trail is the operator's proof of what the system did. Reinterpreting fields in\nan existing version of an evidence/audit struct corrupts that proof silently — a v1 bundle written\nsix months ago must decode cleanly under today's code.\n\n```rust\n// Good — additive evolution\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct EvidenceBundleV1 {\n    pub bundle_id: BundleId,\n    pub created_at: OffsetDateTime,\n    pub findings: Vec<Finding>,\n    // Added in v1.1 — optional, default keeps v1.0 decodable:\n    #[serde(default, skip_serializing_if = \"Vec::is_empty\")]\n    pub remediation_attempts: Vec<RemediationAttempt>,\n}\n\n// Bad — same name, different shape\npub struct EvidenceBundle {              // v1\n    pub bundle_id: BundleId,\n    pub findings: Vec<Finding>,\n}\n\npub struct EvidenceBundle {              // v2 — same name, repurposed shape\n    pub bundle_id: BundleId,\n    pub findings: Vec<EnrichedFinding>,  // different element type\n}\n```\n\nWhen the audit shape genuinely needs to break, bump to a new versioned wire type (`EvidenceBundleV2`)\nand keep the v1 decoder. See `languages/rust-wire-protocols` (234) for the general rule.\n\nThis applies to every persisted artifact: evidence bundles, policy evaluation records, agent\nsession transcripts, workflow snapshots, decision cards.\n\n## Rust core does not leak UI / client assumptions\n\nThe dependency arrow points UI → core, never the reverse. CLI / web / native protocol structs can\nadapt to core models; core models do not import CLI / web / TUI / SwiftUI concepts.\n\n```rust\n// Good — core model is UI-agnostic\nmod domain {\n    pub struct AttentionItem {\n        pub id: AttentionId,\n        pub severity: AttentionSeverity,\n        pub summary: DiagnosticMessage,\n        pub source: AttentionSource,\n    }\n}\n\n// UI layers adapt at the boundary\nmod tui {\n    use crate::domain::AttentionItem;\n    fn render_row(item: &AttentionItem, theme: &Theme) -> Line { /* ... */ }\n}\n\n// Bad — core model knows about TUI rendering\nmod domain {\n    pub struct AttentionItem {\n        pub id: AttentionId,\n        pub severity: AttentionSeverity,\n        pub summary: String,\n        pub tui_glyph: char,          // ← TUI concern leaked into domain\n        pub tui_color: Color,         // ← so has color\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe same applies to web and native app surfaces: SwiftUI types, web-component descriptors, HTML\nattributes, CSS classes — none of those belong in core. They get derived at the boundary by the\nrenderer that knows about them.\n\nThis rule is what makes the FFI / web / TUI / native shells interchangeable: they all consume the\nsame core model and produce their own representation. A core that imports `tui` (or `web`, or\n`swiftui`) ceases to be reusable across the fleet of surface shells.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not use string-typed phase / state / status fields in core logic.\n- Do not collapse a policy decision into `bool` or `Result<(), String>`.\n- Do not return string messages from a detector — emit a structured `Finding`.\n- Do not trait-abstract a domain function that has one real implementation.\n- Do not change the on-wire shape of an existing evidence/audit struct version; bump the version.\n- Do not import UI / TUI / native-shell types from a core domain crate.\n\n## References\n\n- `languages/rust` (230) — base style; in particular \"Domain newtypes at boundaries\" (the\n  language-level frame these Miniforge-specific rules extend).\n- `languages/rust-wire-protocols` (234) — wire/domain separation + versioned envelopes apply to\n  every audit-trail evolution here.\n- `foundations/localization` (050) — `DiagnosticMessage`, finding text, and every other\n  operator-facing string flows through the localization framework.\n- `frameworks/api-surface-classes` (332) — the three API-surface tiers (internal EDN/Transit,\n  cross-language JSON/SSE, customer extension REST/webhooks) the UI-vs-core boundary must respect.",
               :id :std/rust-miniforge-shape,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "835",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (835): Miniforge-specific Rust shape — typed workflow state, PolicyDecision as value, structured findings, adapter traits but domain concrete, append-only evidence, core never imports UI"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Rust observability — tracing over println, correlation IDs on workflow/agent paths, error source-chain preservation",
               :agent-behavior
               "See also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. `foundations/localization` (050) — every\nemitted string is localized; log messages target the system locale.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Rust observability — tracing over println, correlation IDs on workflow/agent paths, error source-chain preservation"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.rs" "**/Cargo.toml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Rust observability\n\nSee also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. `foundations/localization` (050) — every\nemitted string is localized; log messages target the system locale.\n\nApplies to every library, adapter, and service crate that emits diagnostic output. CLIs and tests\nhave narrow exemptions noted below.\n\n## `tracing`, not `println!`\n\nLibraries MUST use the `tracing` crate. `println!` and `eprintln!` are acceptable only in:\n\n- Small CLI binaries that emit deliberate operator-facing output.\n- Examples and test fixtures.\n- `Display` / `Debug` impls themselves (where `tracing` would be circular).\n\n```rust\n// Good — structured event, queryable in any tracing subscriber\ntracing::info!(workflow_id = %plan.id(), phase = ?plan.phase, \"workflow phase entered\");\n\n// Bad — unstructured stdout; invisible to Grafana, Loki, journald, or test capture\nprintln!(\"workflow {} entered phase {:?}\", plan.id(), plan.phase);\n```\n\n`println!` from inside a library is a workspace-level lint:\n\n```toml\n[workspace.lints.clippy]\nprint_stdout = \"warn\"\nprint_stderr = \"warn\"\ndbg_macro = \"deny\"\n```\n\n`dbg!` is always denied — it's a development aid, not production telemetry.\n\n## Correlation IDs on workflow/agent paths\n\nFunctions on workflow/agent/PR execution paths SHOULD carry correlation IDs via\n`#[tracing::instrument]` so every emitted event can be filtered to a single run end-to-end. This\nis non-negotiable for any code path the operator might need to debug from logs.\n\n```rust\n#[tracing::instrument(\n    skip(executor, plan),\n    fields(\n        workflow_id = %plan.workflow_id(),\n        run_id      = %plan.run_id(),\n        phase_id    = tracing::field::Empty,  // populated when entering a phase\n    ),\n)]\nasync fn execute_plan(executor: &Executor, plan: WorkflowPlan) -> Result<ExecutionReport> {\n    /* ... */\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n\n- `skip(...)` keeps large arguments (executor handle, full plan) out of every event payload.\n- `fields(...)` declares the correlation surface up front. Fields populated later use\n  `tracing::field::Empty` as a placeholder, then\n  `tracing::Span::current().record(\"phase_id\", tracing::field::display(&id))` inside the body.\n- Use `%foo` for `Display` formatting, `?foo` for `Debug`. Pick the one the operator will read.\n\nCross-task spans: `tracing::Span::current()` captures the active span; `instrument` on the spawned\nfuture propagates it. Tasks created without `instrument` lose the parent context — every spawn that\nmatters for diagnostics MUST be instrumented.\n\n## Error source-chain preservation\n\nDo not stringify errors at intermediate layers. Preserve the typed `source()` chain all the way to\nthe reporting boundary; consumers downstream (log scrapers, alerting, support runbooks) extract the\nchain.\n\n```rust\n// Good — typed error, source chain preserved\n#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]\npub enum WorkflowError {\n    #[error(\"policy evaluation failed for {workflow_id}\")]\n    PolicyEval {\n        workflow_id: WorkflowId,\n        #[source]\n        cause: PolicyEvalError,\n    },\n}\n\n// At the reporting boundary (CLI / FFI / log line):\ntracing::error!(error = ?err, \"workflow failed\");\n// `error = ?err` records the error's `Debug` representation as a field.\n// If you need each `source()` level rendered, use a formatter/layer or helper that\n// explicitly formats or records the source chain.\n\n// Bad — early stringification erases the cause\nreturn Err(WorkflowError::Generic(format!(\"policy eval failed: {e}\")));\n```\n\nThe `thiserror` `#[source]` attribute marks the wrapped error so `Error::source()` returns it.\n`anyhow::Error` preserves source chains automatically — its `Debug` impl walks them.\n\nAt the actual log emission point, the structured field carries the error; never serialize the\nerror chain into the message text:\n\n```rust\n// Good\ntracing::error!(error = ?err, workflow_id = %id, \"workflow failed\");\n\n// Bad — error becomes a string in the message; cause chain is lost\ntracing::error!(\"workflow {} failed: {:?}\", id, err);\n```\n\n## Spans for long-running operations\n\nUse `tracing::span!` (or `#[instrument]`) for any operation that lasts long enough to be visible\nin latency graphs — handle of a request, evaluation of a policy pack, drainage of an event stream.\nSpans give the operator a duration measurement and a parent/child tree without per-event timestamp\nmath.\n\n**Async functions: use `#[instrument]` or `Instrument::instrument`. Do not hold an `Entered`\nguard across `.await` points.** `tracing` explicitly warns against this — when a task yields at\nan `.await`, the executor may schedule a different task on the same thread while the `Entered`\nguard remains live, polluting that other task's span context.\n\n```rust\n// Good — #[instrument] correctly enters/exits the span around every poll\n#[tracing::instrument(skip(pack, target), fields(pack = %pack.id(), target = %target.id()))]\nasync fn evaluate_policy_pack(pack: &PolicyPack, target: &Artifact) -> PolicyOutcome {\n    /* ... evaluation ... */\n}\n\n// Also good — explicit Instrument::instrument on a future\nuse tracing::Instrument;\nlet span = tracing::info_span!(\"policy.evaluate\", pack = %pack.id());\nlet outcome = evaluate(pack, target).instrument(span).await;\n\n// Bad — Entered guard held across .await; pollutes other tasks' span contexts\nasync fn evaluate_policy_pack(pack: &PolicyPack, target: &Artifact) -> PolicyOutcome {\n    let _span = tracing::info_span!(\"policy.evaluate\", pack = %pack.id()).entered();\n    something_else().await;  // ← span remains \"entered\" while another task may run on this thread\n}\n```\n\nFor synchronous blocks, `.entered()` is fine — the RAII guard drops at end of scope and there's\nno executor yielding to worry about:\n\n```rust\nfn evaluate_sync(pack: &PolicyPack, target: &Artifact) -> PolicyOutcome {\n    let _span = tracing::info_span!(\"policy.evaluate\", pack = %pack.id()).entered();\n    /* ... sync work ... */\n}\n```\n\n## Log-message strings are localized\n\nPer `foundations/localization` (050): every emitted string is localized through the project's\nlocalization framework, including log messages. Log messages come from the **system catalog**\n(for example `messages/system.edn`, with no operator override) so engineering tooling — Grafana\nqueries, Loki filters, alert templates — keeps working regardless of which operator-facing locale\nis active.\n\nConcretely: a project's `mfc-strings` (or equivalent) crate declares log-message constants under a\n`logs` module; tracing call sites reference the const via the `\"{}\"` placeholder (`tracing` macros\nrequire the message be a format-string literal at the macro site):\n\n```rust\nuse mfc_strings as s;\n\ntracing::warn!(\n    error = %e,\n    path = %p.display(),\n    \"{}\", s::LOG_TAILER_EXIT_ON_ERROR,\n);\n```\n\nThe constant lives in one place; future locale additions or string audits find it via `grep -r\nmfc_strings::LOG_`.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not use `println!` / `eprintln!` / `dbg!` in library or service code.\n- Do not stringify errors at intermediate layers — preserve `source()` chains.\n- Do not skip `#[instrument]` on workflow/agent execution paths.\n- Do not embed structured data inside the message string — use named fields.\n- Do not emit hardcoded English log strings without going through the localization layer (see\n  `foundations/localization`).\n- Do not log at the wrong level — `info` is for routine events the operator wants to see, `debug`\n  is for engineers, `warn` is for unexpected-but-recoverable, `error` is for things that need\n  attention.\n\n## References\n\n- [`tracing` crate documentation](https://docs.rs/tracing/) — structured spans/events.\n- [`tracing::instrument` macro](https://docs.rs/tracing/latest/tracing/attr.instrument.html) — the\n  canonical way to attach correlation IDs to async functions.\n- [`thiserror` `#[source]` attribute](https://docs.rs/thiserror/) — preserving error source chains\n  in typed error enums.\n- `foundations/localization` (050) — the all-strings-are-localized policy that log messages must\n  also satisfy.",
               :id :std/rust-observability,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "233",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (233): Rust observability — tracing over println, correlation IDs on workflow/agent paths, error source-chain preservation"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Rust unsafe discipline — forbidden by default, isolated behind safe APIs, SAFETY comments required, never \"it's faster\"",
               :agent-behavior
               "See also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. This file is the narrow but load-bearing\npolicy on every `unsafe` block, `unsafe fn`, or `unsafe trait impl`.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Rust unsafe discipline — forbidden by default, isolated behind safe APIs, SAFETY comments required, never \"it's faster\""},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.rs" "**/Cargo.toml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Rust unsafe discipline\n\nSee also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. This file is the narrow but load-bearing\npolicy on every `unsafe` block, `unsafe fn`, or `unsafe trait impl`.\n\n## Forbidden by default\n\n`unsafe` is denied at the workspace level. Set this in the root `Cargo.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[workspace.lints.rust]\nunsafe_code = \"deny\"\n```\n\nPer-crate exceptions are allowed only when the crate's purpose requires unsafe. The whitelist is\nsmall in practice: FFI bindings, memory-layout primitives, OS-platform glue, performance-critical\nlow-level abstractions where safe alternatives are demonstrably insufficient.\n\nA crate that needs unsafe declares the exception explicitly:\n\n```toml\n[lints.rust]\nunsafe_code = \"warn\"  # or \"allow\" with a justification comment\n```\n\nService code, business logic, adapters, and protocol crates do not need unsafe. The default is\nthe policy.\n\n## Isolated behind a safe API\n\nRequired unsafe MUST be isolated behind a safe API. The unsafe block is small; the safe wrapper\ndocuments the invariants that justify it. Callers of the wrapper get a normal Rust API.\n\n```rust\n// Good — unsafe is a narrow implementation detail; the public API is safe\npub fn split_at_mut(s: &mut [u8], mid: usize) -> (&mut [u8], &mut [u8]) {\n    assert!(mid <= s.len(), \"split index out of bounds\");\n    let ptr = s.as_mut_ptr();\n    let len = s.len();\n    // SAFETY: mid is bounds-checked above; the two slices are non-overlapping by construction\n    // (one starts at ptr, length `mid`; the other starts at ptr+mid, length `len-mid`).\n    unsafe { (std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr, mid),\n              std::slice::from_raw_parts_mut(ptr.add(mid), len - mid)) }\n}\n\n// Bad — unsafe leaks into the public surface; callers inherit the obligation\npub unsafe fn split_at_mut(s: &mut [u8], mid: usize) -> (&mut [u8], &mut [u8]) { /* same */ }\n```\n\nThe Rust Book's guidance: unsafe blocks are small; safe abstractions wrap them; the public API\ndoes not require the caller to know the safety story.\n\n## SAFETY comment on every unsafe site\n\nEvery `unsafe` block and every `unsafe fn` MUST carry a `// SAFETY:` comment explaining the\ninvariant being upheld. The comment names what is true at this site that makes the unsafe call\nlegal — not what the unsafe call does mechanically.\n\n```rust\n// Good — SAFETY comment explains the invariant\nlet value = unsafe {\n    // SAFETY: ptr is non-null and properly aligned; we just checked `!ptr.is_null()` above\n    // and the allocator returns aligned blocks. The lifetime ends inside this block.\n    *ptr\n};\n\n// Bad — comment restates the syntax\nlet value = unsafe {\n    // Dereference the pointer\n    *ptr\n};\n```\n\nFor `unsafe fn`, the `// SAFETY:` documentation lives in the rustdoc `# Safety` section and\nstates the preconditions the caller must establish before calling. Every call site of an\n`unsafe fn` then carries its own `// SAFETY:` comment proving those preconditions hold at the\ncall site.\n\n## \"It is faster\" is not enough\n\n`unsafe` for performance requires:\n\n1. **Profiling evidence** showing the safe version is materially insufficient at production load —\n   not in a microbenchmark of a tight loop with no contention.\n2. **A benchmark in the repo** that exercises the safe vs unsafe versions and demonstrates the gap.\n3. **An invariant analysis** documenting why the unsafe version is correct, written as if it were\n   a code review.\n\nMicrobenchmark deltas that don't translate to operator-observable latency do not justify unsafe.\nThe cost is real — every unsafe site is one more thing on the human reviewer's audit list, and\nthe benefit needs to clear that bar.\n\n## Common unsafe pitfalls\n\nEven with the discipline above, certain patterns are recurring sources of UB:\n\n- **Aliasing mutable references through raw pointers.** Constructing two `&mut T` (directly or via\n  `&mut *ptr`) that overlap is undefined behavior — even if you never write through both. Use\n  `std::ptr` operations or restructure the code.\n- **Sending `!Send` types across threads via transmute or hand-rolled raw-pointer tricks.** If a\n  type is `!Send`, the compiler is telling you something. Wrapping it in `Arc` and a transmute\n  does not change the underlying memory-model story.\n- **Dropping or freeing memory you don't own.** `Box::from_raw` only consumes a pointer that came\n  from `Box::into_raw`. Mixing allocator boundaries (Rust `Box`, malloc, custom allocators) is a\n  silent data corruption channel.\n- **Trusting alignment or layout assumptions.** `#[repr(Rust)]` (the default) makes no layout\n  guarantees. Use `#[repr(C)]` for FFI; use `mem::align_of` / `mem::size_of` at runtime; don't\n  hand-roll struct offsets.\n\nWhen in doubt, write the safe version, profile it, then revisit.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not add `unsafe` because the borrow checker rejected your design — that's a signal to redesign,\n  not to escape into raw pointers.\n- Do not use `unsafe` to avoid a `Result` — that's `unwrap_unchecked`, which removes a runtime\n  check the optimizer probably already eliminated.\n- Do not put `unsafe` in a public function signature without a `# Safety` rustdoc section.\n- Do not leave a `// SAFETY:` comment off any unsafe site — even a one-line block.\n- Do not silence `unsafe_code = \"deny\"` per-file with `#[allow(unsafe_code)]` without an\n  invariant comment that justifies why this specific exception is correct.\n\n## References\n\n- [Rust Book — Unsafe Rust](https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch20-01-unsafe-rust.html) — when and\n  how unsafe is meant to be used; small blocks behind safe APIs.\n- [Canonical — Unsafe discipline](https://canonical.github.io/rust-best-practices/unsafe-discipline.html) —\n  the \"isolate, document, justify\" frame this rule adopts.",
               :id :std/rust-unsafe,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "232",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (232): Rust unsafe discipline — forbidden by default, isolated behind safe APIs, SAFETY comments required, never \"it's faster\""}
        #:rule{:title
               "Rust wire protocols — separate wire structs from domain structs, version envelopes explicitly, validate invariants on deserialize",
               :agent-behavior
               "See also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + the \"domain newtypes at boundaries\" type idiom this\nrule extends. `frameworks/api-surface-classes` (332) — the broader wire-format taxonomy this rule\napplies to in Rust.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Rust wire protocols — separate wire structs from domain structs, version envelopes explicitly, validate invariants on deserialize"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.rs" "**/Cargo.toml"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Rust wire protocols\n\nSee also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + the \"domain newtypes at boundaries\" type idiom this\nrule extends. `frameworks/api-surface-classes` (332) — the broader wire-format taxonomy this rule\napplies to in Rust.\n\nApplies to every (de)serialization boundary: FFI payloads, event-stream JSON, gRPC / Cap'n Proto /\nMessagePack, HTTP request bodies, persisted artifacts. The rules are the same for all wire formats;\nserde derives are the canonical example.\n\n## Wire structs are not domain structs\n\nA wire struct's job is bytes-on-the-wire stability. A domain struct's job is in-memory invariant\nenforcement. Conflating them locks the wire format to whatever the domain happens to look like\ntoday — every refactor becomes a breaking protocol change.\n\n```rust\n// Good — separate types, explicit conversion\nmod domain {\n    pub struct WorkflowPlan {\n        pub id: WorkflowId,                      // newtype, validated\n        pub phases: Vec<Phase>,                  // domain Phase enum\n        pub budget: RetryBudget,                 // newtype with invariants\n    }\n}\n\nmod protocol {\n    #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\n    pub struct WorkflowPlanV1 {\n        pub id: Uuid,                            // raw uuid on the wire\n        pub phases: Vec<PhaseV1>,                // wire-format Phase\n        pub budget: u32,                         // raw u32 on the wire\n    }\n}\n\nimpl TryFrom<protocol::WorkflowPlanV1> for domain::WorkflowPlan {\n    type Error = WorkflowPlanError;\n    fn try_from(wire: protocol::WorkflowPlanV1) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> { /* validate */ }\n}\n\n// Bad — one type serves both surfaces; the wire is locked to the domain shape\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct WorkflowPlan {\n    pub id: Uuid,\n    pub phases: Vec<Phase>,\n    pub budget: u32,\n}\n```\n\nThe exception: when stability semantics are genuinely identical — e.g. a primitive config struct\nthat's just a serde-derived bag of fields and is also the in-memory shape. In that case one type\nis fine, but the moment you want to add a domain invariant (`budget > 0`, `phases` non-empty), the\ntypes split.\n\nNaming convention: domain types live in `domain/` or directly in their owning module. Wire types\nlive in `protocol/` (or `wire/`) and carry an explicit version suffix (`PlanV1`, `EnvelopeV2`).\n\n## Version wire formats explicitly\n\nEvery wire format has an explicit version field. New consumers detect old payloads and old\nconsumers fail predictably on new ones.\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct EnvelopeV1<T> {\n    pub version: ProtocolVersion,\n    pub payload: T,\n}\n\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\n#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\npub enum ProtocolVersion {\n    V1,\n    V2,\n}\n```\n\nMigration shape:\n\n- Adding optional fields to an existing version is additive; mark them `#[serde(default,\n  skip_serializing_if = \"...\")]`.\n- Removing fields, reinterpreting fields, or changing field types is a new version. Bump the\n  version enum, keep the old variant decodable, add a converter.\n- Never silently drop unknown fields from versions newer than the consumer expects — log + reject,\n  or pass-through if the consumer can tolerate it.\n\n`#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` is the right default for strictly-validated payloads (control-plane\ncommands). Open-set payloads (telemetry events, debug snapshots) can tolerate unknown fields with\nexplicit logging.\n\n## Deserialization validates domain invariants\n\nA wire struct deserializes into the wire type, not directly into the domain type. The conversion\nfrom wire to domain is where invariants get checked. This is where `TryFrom` earns its keep.\n\n```rust\n// Good — wire round-trips serde; domain enforces invariants\nmod protocol {\n    #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\n    pub struct WorkflowIdWire(pub String);\n}\n\nmod domain {\n    pub struct WorkflowId(String);\n\n    impl TryFrom<protocol::WorkflowIdWire> for WorkflowId {\n        type Error = WorkflowIdError;\n        fn try_from(wire: protocol::WorkflowIdWire) -> Result<Self, Self::Error> {\n            validate_workflow_id(&wire.0)?;\n            Ok(Self(wire.0))\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n// Bad — wire and domain are the same type; serde accepts anything that parses\n#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct WorkflowId(pub String);\n```\n\nFor inline validation during deserialization (one-shot enums or value objects), implement a custom\n`Deserialize` via the visitor pattern or use a typed-wrapper crate (`serde_with`, `garde`,\n`validator`). The principle is the same: invariants are enforced before the domain type exists.\n\n## Optional field discipline\n\nOptional wire fields are tolerated; defensive matching at every read site is not. Push the optional\ninto the conversion layer:\n\n```rust\n// Good — conversion layer resolves the optional once\nmod protocol {\n    #[derive(Deserialize)]\n    pub struct AgentReportV1 {\n        pub agent_id: Uuid,\n        pub status: String,\n        pub metrics: Option<MetricsV1>,        // optional on the wire\n    }\n}\n\nmod domain {\n    pub struct AgentReport {\n        pub agent_id: AgentId,\n        pub status: AgentStatus,\n        pub metrics: Metrics,                  // domain has a default\n    }\n\n    impl From<protocol::AgentReportV1> for AgentReport {\n        fn from(wire: protocol::AgentReportV1) -> Self {\n            AgentReport {\n                agent_id: AgentId(wire.agent_id),\n                status: wire.status.parse().unwrap_or(AgentStatus::Unknown),\n                metrics: wire.metrics.map(Into::into).unwrap_or_default(),\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n`Option<T>` in the domain type is reserved for cases where absence is genuinely meaningful (the\nreport covers a partial window; the field is N/A for this agent kind). Don't propagate every\n\"the wire might omit this\" optional all the way through.\n\n## Roundtrip + compatibility tests\n\nEvery wire format ships with roundtrip and compatibility tests. See `languages/rust` (230)\nTesting section for the general principle; the protocol-specific cases:\n\n- `encode(decode(x)) == x` — wire is fixed-point under round-trip.\n- `decode(old_fixture) == expected_current_model` — old payloads continue to decode under the\n  current consumer.\n- Unknown fields are handled per the format's stated policy (rejected, ignored, or pass-through).\n- Malformed bytes fail with a typed error, not a panic.\n\nFixtures live in `tests/fixtures/<protocol>/v<N>/` and are committed. A protocol change that\nbreaks a committed fixture is a deliberate version bump, not a silent regression.\n\n## Backward-compatibility ratchet\n\nOnce a wire version is observed in production, that version's encoding is permanent. Adding\noptional fields is allowed; changing the meaning of existing fields is not.\n\nThe audit trail (`foundations/result-handling`, event streams, evidence bundles) is especially\nsensitive: a v1 evidence bundle written to durable storage must decode forever under any later\nconsumer, or the operator loses the ability to prove what the system did. Reinterpreting fields in\nexisting versions corrupts the audit trail silently.\n\nWhen a version becomes obsolete, mark it deprecated in the code but keep the decoder; remove only\nafter a documented retention window plus a migration that re-encodes any stored payloads.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not use one struct for both the wire format and the domain type unless stability semantics\n  are demonstrably identical and you accept that domain refactors are wire-breaking changes.\n- Do not omit the `version` field from wire envelopes — even the first version.\n- Do not deserialize directly into the domain type without a validation hop.\n- Do not propagate every wire-side `Option` all the way through the domain. Resolve optionals at\n  the conversion layer.\n- Do not change the encoding of an existing version after it has shipped. Bump.\n- Do not assume `#[serde(default)]` is free — confirm the default value is semantically correct in\n  every existing call path, not just convenient at the deserialization site.\n\n## References\n\n- [Serde derive docs](https://serde.rs/derive.html) — `#[serde(default)]`, `#[serde(rename_all)]`,\n  `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`, version-tolerant patterns.\n- [Rust API Guidelines — Dependability](https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/dependability.html) —\n  static-validation-where-possible, dynamic-at-boundaries.\n- `frameworks/api-surface-classes` (332) — the three API-surface tiers (internal EDN/Transit,\n  cross-language JSON/SSE, customer-facing REST/webhooks) that govern which protocol shape applies.",
               :id :std/rust-wire-protocols,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "234",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (234): Rust wire protocols — separate wire structs from domain structs, version envelopes explicitly, validate invariants on deserialize"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Code is the artifact — comments explain WHY when non-obvious, never restate WHAT; self-document first; document the public boundary, keep the impl light",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. After writing a function, delete every comment, then re-add only the ones whose removal lost a real \"why.\"\n2. Never narrate your own reasoning in the code. The diff and PR description are for that.\n3. When a comment is needed, prefer fixing the code (rename, extract, constant) so the comment becomes unnecessary.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Comment restates WHAT the code does instead of WHY. Remove narrating comments and make the code self-evident; reserve comments for non-obvious rationale, and document the public boundary's contract."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Self-Documenting Code (ALWAYS)\n\n**The code is the primary artifact. A comment is an admission that the code could not say it itself.** Reach for a better name, a smaller function, a named constant, or a type *before* reaching for a comment. When a comment is warranted, it explains **why** — a reason not visible in the code — never **what** the code already states.\n\nThis is the operational complement to `foundations/code-quality` (002), `foundations/named-constants` (006), and `foundations/no-dead-code` (008, no commented-out blocks).\n\n## The rule\n\n1. **Default to no comment.** First make the code self-evident. A comment is the last resort, not the first.\n2. **Comment WHY, never WHAT.** If a comment restates what the next line does, delete it. If it captures a reason, constraint, or tradeoff the code cannot express, keep it.\n3. **Self-document first.** Before writing a comment, try: a clearer name, extracting a well-named function, a named constant (006), an intermediate `let` binding, or a more precise type.\n4. **Test before keeping a comment:** would the code lose meaning if this comment were deleted? If no → delete it. If yes → it is a real \"why.\"\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — narrates the code; every line is noise\n;; loop over the users\n(doseq [u users]\n  ;; if the user is active\n  (when (:active? u)\n    ;; send them an email\n    (send-email u)))\n\n;; GOOD — the code says what; no comment needed\n(doseq [u (filter :active? users)]\n  (send-email u))\n\n;; GOOD — a real WHY the code cannot show\n;; Datalevin returns :db.cardinality/one as a bare value, not a set,\n;; so we must wrap before set/union — see datalevin#412.\n(update entity :tags (comp set wrap-single))\n```\n\n```rust\n// BAD — restates the call\n// acquire the lock\nlet guard = state.lock().unwrap();\n\n// GOOD — WHY this ordering matters, not visible locally\n// Lock `accounts` before `ledger` everywhere; the reverse order\n// deadlocks with the settlement task (see ADR-021).\nlet _a = accounts.lock();\nlet _l = ledger.lock();\n```\n\n## Comments that are NOT noise — keep these\n\n- **File / license headers.** Governed by `project/header-copyright` (810) for OSS and `.thesium-standards/project/header-proprietary` for proprietary repos. **Never strip or compress these.** They are required, not commentary.\n- **Docstrings on public namespaces, fns, and vars.** State the contract (purpose, args, return, failure mode) and the why — do not restate the body. A docstring that only echoes the name carries no contract; rewrite it to express the why, or remove it if there is no why to express (a self-evident one-liner like `(defn cargo-target-dir [config] ...)` does not need a docstring just to have one).\n- **Genuine \"why\" notes:** non-obvious invariants, rationale for a surprising choice, performance tradeoffs, security constraints, workarounds with a linked cause (issue/ADR/spec).\n- **Rust `SAFETY:` comments** on `unsafe` blocks — required by `languages/rust-unsafe` (232).\n- **Named-constant docstrings** — required by `named-constants` (006) to carry intent.\n\n## Where documentation lives — the published boundary, not the impl\n\nDocumentation density follows **visibility**, not effort. The published boundary is the contract every consumer reads; the implementation is internal and changes freely. So:\n\n- **Document the public boundary.** Every exported var/fn carries a docstring stating the contract: purpose, args, return shape, failure mode, and any why a caller must know. This is where a reader should learn the API without opening the internals.\n- **Keep the impl light.** In an internal namespace (`core`, `impl/*`, `defn-`, or a `def` re-exported through an interface), **remove a docstring that merely duplicates the boundary's contract doc** — the boundary now carries it. **Keep** an impl docstring that carries information the boundary does not: a non-obvious invariant, an implementation tradeoff, or a roadmap note. The boundary holds the contract; the impl holds only what is genuinely additional.\n- **The common inversion to reject:** a thin, undocumented public interface re-exporting a heavily-docstringed `core`. The contract ends up hidden inside the implementation; consumers must read internals to use the component. Flip it — lift the contract doc to the boundary, leave the impl with only its *why*.\n\nIn **Polylith**, the `interface` namespace is the boundary; `core` and other impl namespaces are internal (see `frameworks/polylith`). A bare passthrough export carries no docstring, so it documents nothing:\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — interface.clj: undocumented passthrough; the contract is hidden in core\n(def create-envelope core/create-envelope)\n\n;; core.clj: the deep contract doc lives here, where no consumer looks\n(defn create-envelope\n  \"Create an event envelope with sequence numbering. When the stream\n   carries a :snowflake-generator, :event/id is a snowflake-encoded UUID …\"\n  [stream opts] …)\n\n;; GOOD — interface.clj: the contract is documented at the boundary\n(defn create-envelope\n  \"Create an event envelope with sequence numbering. When the stream\n   carries a :snowflake-generator, :event/id is a snowflake-encoded UUID …\"\n  [stream opts]\n  (core/create-envelope stream opts))\n\n;; core.clj: impl keeps only a why-note, if any\n(defn create-envelope [stream opts] …)\n```\n\nWhen a passthrough must stay a `def` (re-exporting a value, not wrapping a fn), give it a docstring: `(def create-envelope \"…contract…\" core/create-envelope)`.\n\n## Reject in review — comment noise\n\n- **What-comments:** any comment that paraphrases the line(s) below it.\n- **Step narration:** `;; step 1`, `// now we validate`, `;; return the result`.\n- **Process / changelog commentary in code:** `;; Wave 6 PR adds this`, `;; renamed from foo`, `;; added 2026-05-01`. Git history and the PR carry this, not the source.\n- **Decorative banners** that dwarf the code (`;; ===== HUGE SECTION =====` repeated, ASCII art). A sparse section divider in a genuinely large file is fine; prefer splitting the file.\n- **Redundant docstrings** that restate the fn name (`\"Gets the user.\"` on `get-user`).\n- **Commented-out code** of any size — see `no-dead-code` (008).\n- **Agent reasoning narrated in comments.** Explanation of *how you arrived at* the code belongs in the PR description, not the source. Do not leave a trail of \"first I tried X, then Y.\"\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. After writing a function, delete every comment, then re-add only the ones whose removal lost a real \"why.\"\n2. Never narrate your own reasoning in the code. The diff and PR description are for that.\n3. When a comment is needed, prefer fixing the code (rename, extract, constant) so the comment becomes unnecessary.\n4. When editing existing files, leave them less commented than you found them — compress what-comments, keep the why.\n5. **File / license headers**: preserve verbatim — never compress or strip.\n6. **Docstrings**: do not mechanically strip. Rewrite them to capture contract + why; remove only when the function name plus signature already says everything a caller needs to know.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- A file where comments outnumber or visually dominate the code\n- Comments that must be updated whenever the adjacent line changes (they restate it)\n- \"Why\" written as \"what\" — `;; set x to 5` instead of `;; 5 = max retries the gateway tolerates before lockout`\n- Section banners used to navigate a file that should have been split into smaller namespaces\n- Reintroducing a deleted comment because \"it might help\" — apply the deletion test",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/self-documenting-code,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "009",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (009): Code is the artifact — comments explain WHY when non-obvious, never restate WHAT; self-document first; document the public boundary, keep the impl light"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Simple Made Easy — choose constructs with simpler artifacts, not easier authoring",
               :agent-behavior
               "- When proposing a design, include:\n- When editing code that’s overly complex:\n  - Suggest a “data first” rewrite of the hot path.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Simple Made Easy — choose constructs with simpler artifacts, not easier authoring"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Simple Made Easy (ALWAYS)\n\nOptimize for **simple artifacts** (unbraided, decoupled), not for what is **easy** (familiar, at hand).\nUsers live with the artifact, not the authoring experience.\n\n> “Simplicity is a prerequisite for reliability.” — Dijkstra  \n> Simple = one braid (uncomplected). Easy = familiar/near. Choose simple.\n\n## Decision rubric\n\nWhen choosing designs/constructs, prefer the option that **reduces intertwining** of concerns—even if it’s less familiar.\n\n- **Values over state**: prefer immutable values and pure functions; make state explicit, local, and small.\n- **Data over syntax**: represent information as plain data (maps, vectors, sets). Avoid DSL-ish micro-syntaxes unless necessary.\n- **Functions over methods**: favor small, stateless functions and namespaces over deep OO hierarchies.\n- **A-la-carte polymorphism**: choose open, decoupled dispatch (protocols/typeclasses/interfaces) over\n                               inheritance and giant enums/switches.\n- **Declarative over imperative**: use set/relational operations, queries, and rules for “what,” not “how.”\n- **Queues over calls (when/where)**: decouple time/place with message queues or async boundaries; don’t make A know B unnecessarily.\n- **Rules live in one place (why)**: centralize policy in data/rules tables or a rules engine, not scattered conditionals.\n\n## Canonical data authority\n\nInternal data contracts need one producer-owned authority for each entity and field. When producer and\nconsumer live in this codebase, the consumer reads the canonical path only; it does not search historical\naliases, arbitrary top-level keys, or “shape-looking” maps to guess the same value.\n\nCompatibility is allowed only at an explicit boundary adapter or migration: name the shim, document the\ncanonical target, add tests for both the accepted legacy input and the canonical output, and remove it when\nthe migration window closes. If the display layer, CLI, dashboard, or report code needs data that is not at\nthe canonical path, fix the producer or add the boundary migration rather than adding consumer fallback\nchains.\n\nTests should pin this: canonical shape succeeds; stale aliases are ignored or rejected unless the adapter\ncontract explicitly says otherwise.\n\n## What / Who / How / When / Where / Why — keep separate\n\n- **What**: operations (names and contracts); small specs (inputs/outputs) live with the function/module.\n- **Who**: concrete implementers/adapters wired via DI; no leaking details across boundaries.\n- **How**: implementations chosen behind interfaces/ports; keep replaceable.\n- **When/Where**: scheduling, retries, concurrency, and topology belong at edges (adapter/config).\n- **Why**: policies and rules centralized; treat as data.\n\n## State & concurrency\n\n- Treat state as hazardous material:\n  - Justify it, minimize scope, isolate at the edge.\n  - Model with managed references/STM/actors only when true concurrency requires it.\n- Prefer idempotent, deterministic operations; make time and randomness injectable.\n\n## Change & debugging\n\n- Smaller surface area ⇒ fewer joints to break. Strive for modules you can reason about locally.\n- Reasonability beats tooling: if understanding requires a global tour, simplify the design.\n- Tests are nets, not wings: don’t rely on them to compensate for tangled designs.\n\n## PR checklist (Author & Reviewer)\n\n- **Simplicity**: Does this reduce entanglement vs alternatives? What did we *remove*?\n- **Boundaries**: Are What/Who/How/When/Where/Why separated?\n- **Data**: Is information represented as plain data, not hidden in code constructs?\n- **State**: Is state minimized, localized, and explicit? Why must it exist?\n- **Policy**: Are rules centralized and declarative?\n- **Swapability**: Can we replace implementations without touching callers?\n- **Evidence**: Include a short note comparing the “easy but complex” option you rejected.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\n- When proposing a design, include:\n  1) A short “simple vs easy” comparison (2–3 bullets).  \n  2) The minimal set of pure functions and data structures.  \n  3) Where any state/concurrency is confined and why it’s necessary.  \n  4) A rules/policy section expressed as data (table/map) if applicable.\n- When editing code that’s overly complex:\n  - Suggest a “data first” rewrite of the hot path.\n  - Extract policy into a dedicated rules module or data file.\n  - Replace switch/if jungles with open polymorphism where beneficial.\n\n## Red flags to push back on\n\n- Deep inheritance trees; giant God objects/services.\n- Conditionals scattered across the codebase encoding policy ad hoc.\n- Consumer-side fallback chains or broad map walks that look in several places for the same internal entity\n  instead of using one producer-owned data authority.\n- ORM-driven domain models leaking persistence concerns everywhere.\n- Actor systems chosen “by default” where simple queues/calls suffice.\n- Tight coupling to frameworks in core logic (non-replaceable code).\n\n## Accept that simple ≠ fewer things\n\nIt’s okay if we end up with *more but smaller* modules. We’re minimizing braiding, not counting files.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/simple-made-easy,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "010",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (010): Simple Made Easy — choose constructs with simpler artifacts, not easier authoring"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Follow normative specs (N1-N6) for all implementation and maintain spec governance",
               :agent-behavior "<version>0.1.0</version>",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Follow normative specs (N1-N6) for all implementation and maintain spec governance"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement},
                :file-globs ["specs/**/*.md"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Specification Standards\n\n<version>0.1.0</version>\n\n## Context\n\nminiforge uses 6 normative specifications (N1-N6) that define ALL contractual requirements. All\nimplementation MUST conform to these specs. Specs extracted from strategic vision, NOT from code.\n\n**Entry point:** `specs/SPEC_INDEX.md`\n\n## Hierarchy\n\n```zsh\nspecs/\n├── SPEC_INDEX.md          # START HERE - authoritative entry point\n├── README.md              # Implementation guide\n├── normative/             # N1-N6 (MUST/SHALL only)\n├── informative/           # Guidance (no MUST/SHALL)\n├── examples/              # Reference implementations\n└── deprecated/            # Superseded (ignore)\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n### When Reading Specs\n\n<required>\n- ALWAYS start with `specs/SPEC_INDEX.md` for overview\n- Read normative specs (N1-N6) for MUST/SHALL requirements\n- NEVER use deprecated/ directory content\n- Cross-reference: specs link to each other\n- Use examples/ for concrete patterns\n</required>\n\n### When Implementing\n\n<required>\n- Implementation MUST conform to all 6 normative specs\n- Event emission MUST follow N3 (Event Stream)\n- Evidence bundles MUST follow N6 (Evidence & Provenance)\n- Workflow execution MUST follow N2 (Workflows)\n- Policy packs MUST follow N4 (Policy Packs)\n- CLI/TUI/API MUST follow N5 (Interface Standard)\n- Architecture MUST follow N1 (Core Architecture)\n</required>\n\n### When Amending Specs\n\n<required>\n- NO new normative spec files (only amend N1-N6)\n- New concepts MUST land in N1 or they're not real\n- New runtime data MUST land in N3 or N6 or it's unobservable\n- New UX features MUST point to contract in N3/N5/N6\n- Normative specs MUST use RFC 2119 keywords (MUST/SHALL/SHOULD/MAY)\n- Informative docs MUST NOT use RFC 2119 keywords\n</required>\n\n### Spec Extraction Source\n\n<critical>\n- Extract specs from strategic documents (software-factory-vision.md, oss-paid-roadmap.md)\n- NEVER extract specs from implementation code\n- Specs define what MUST be built, not what happens to exist\n- Code conforms to specs, not the reverse\n</critical>\n\n## Six Normative Specs\n\n| Spec | File | Purpose | Key Contracts |\n|------|------|---------|---------------|\n| N1 | `N1-architecture.md` | Core concepts, layering | 12 nouns, 3-layer architecture, Polylith boundaries |\n| N2 | `N2-workflows.md` | Workflow execution | Phase graph, inner/outer loops, gate contract |\n| N3 | `N3-event-stream.md` | Event stream & observability | 9 event types, SSE/WebSocket, ordering guarantees |\n| N4 | `N4-policy-packs.md` | Policy packs & gates | Semantic intent validation, check/repair contract |\n| N5 | `N5-cli-tui-api.md` | CLI/TUI/API interface | Command taxonomy, TUI views, streaming endpoints |\n| N6 | `N6-evidence-provenance.md` | Evidence & provenance | Evidence bundles, artifact provenance, semantic validation |\n\n**Most leverageful:** N3 (Event Stream) and N6 (Evidence & Provenance) - everything builds on these.\n\n## Examples\n\n<example>\n✅ GOOD: Implementing event emission\n\n```clojure\n;; Following N3 event schema\n(emit-event {:event/type :agent/status\n             :event/id (uuid)\n             :event/timestamp (Instant/now)\n             :event/version \"1.0.0\"\n             :workflow/id workflow-id\n             :event/sequence-number (next-seq)\n             :agent/id :implementer\n             :status/type :generating\n             :message \"Generating Terraform code...\"})\n```\n\nAgent emits events following N3 schema exactly.\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"valid\">\n✅ GOOD: Semantic intent validation\n\nSemantic validation catches intent violations before any resource change is committed.\n\n```clojure\n;; Following N6 semantic validation\n(validate-intent {:declared :import\n                  :actual-creates 0\n                  :actual-destroys 0})\n;; => {:passed? true}\n\n(validate-intent {:declared :import\n                  :actual-creates 3   ; VIOLATION\n                  :actual-destroys 0})\n;; => {:passed? false :violation \"Declared IMPORT but creates 3 resources\"}\n```\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n❌ BAD: Extracting spec from code\n\n```clojure\n;; Reading implementation to write spec\n(read-file \"miniforge/components/agent/core.clj\")\n;; Then writing spec based on what code does\n```\n\nNEVER extract specs from code. Extract from strategic documents (software-factory-vision.md, oss-paid-roadmap.md).\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\n❌ BAD: Creating new normative spec\n\n```zsh\nspecs/normative/N7-new-feature.md  # WRONG!\n```\n\nNO new normative specs. Amend existing N1-N6 only.\n</example>\n\n## Related Rules\n\n- `foundations/stratified-design.mdc` - Layering principles (complements N1)\n- `workflows/pr-documentation.mdc` - PR docs (includes spec conformance notes)",
               :id :std/specification-standards,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "020",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (020): Follow normative specs (N1-N6) for all implementation and maintain spec governance"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Testing standards — factory functions, same code quality as production, no duplication",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Before writing the first `deftest`, ask: what factory functions does this test file need?\n2. Extract them immediately at Layer 0, not after the fact.\n3. Apply the same code review lens to tests as to production code — no magic numbers, no nested conditionals, no raw string literals.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Testing standards — factory functions, same code quality as production, no duplication"},
               :applies-to {:phases #{:verify :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Testing Standards (ALWAYS)\n\n**Test code is production code.** Every standard that applies to implementation code applies to test code. No exceptions.\n\n## Factory functions over inline maps\n\nTest data construction must use factory functions. Never build event maps, entity maps, or request fixtures inline.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — inline map construction repeated in every test\n(def event1\n  {:event/type      :workflow/started\n   :event/id        (random-uuid)\n   :event/timestamp (java.util.Date/from (java.time.Instant/parse \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\"))\n   :workflow/id     \"wf-123\"})\n\n;; Same map rebuilt again in another test\n{:event/type      :workflow/completed\n :event/id        (random-uuid)\n :event/timestamp (java.util.Date/from (java.time.Instant/parse \"2026-01-01T01:00:00Z\"))\n :workflow/id     \"wf-123\"}\n\n;; GOOD — factory function\n(defn- parse-ts [s]\n  (java.util.Date/from (java.time.Instant/parse s)))\n\n(defn- wf-event\n  [event-type wf-id timestamp & {:as extra}]\n  (merge {:event/type      event-type\n          :event/id        (random-uuid)\n          :event/timestamp (parse-ts timestamp)\n          :workflow/id     wf-id}\n         extra))\n\n;; Usage — readable and DRY\n(wf-event :workflow/started    \"wf-123\" \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\")\n(wf-event :workflow/completed  \"wf-123\" \"2026-01-01T01:00:00Z\" :workflow/status :success)\n(wf-event :workflow/phase-started \"wf-123\" \"2026-01-01T00:05:00Z\" :workflow/phase :implement)\n```\n\n**When to create a factory:**\n- Any test data map constructed more than once — extract immediately\n- Any map with more than 3 fields — factory even if used once (for readability)\n- Any map that varies by a few fields across tests — factory with keyword args for the varying parts\n\n## Fixtures mirror the producer's real shape\n\nA factory's job is not just to be DRY — it must construct data in the **same shape the real producer emits**: same field *types*, key namespaces, and enum domains. A fixture you reshape to make a test pass is not a simplification — it is the bug surfacing in the code under test. Reshaping the input to dodge a failing path makes the test green while production stays broken.\n\n- When a test builds an input that another component or function produces, mirror that producer's **actual output**. Reuse the producer's factory (or a captured real artifact) where you can; across a brick boundary where you cannot import it, replicate the exact types and reference the producer in a comment.\n- Loose schemas (`any?`, open maps) do **not** pin the shape — they make this rule *more* important, because nothing mechanical will catch the drift.\n- If, while choosing test inputs, you reason \"this would be a bug\" — stop. That divergence is the bug. Fix the code under test, or commit the failing test. Never route a fixture around a known-bad path.\n\n```clojure\n;; BAD — the producer emits a keyword track-id (:track/manager), but the\n;; consumer's normalize fn mishandles keywords. Making the fixture a string\n;; gets a match — green test, broken production.\n(defn- recon-row [overrides]\n  (merge {:reconciliation/track-id \"em\"} overrides))        ; producer never emits a string here\n;; GOOD — mirror the keyword shape the pipeline actually emits; the test then\n;; FAILS until the normalize fn is fixed to handle keywords.\n(defn- recon-row [overrides]\n  (merge {:reconciliation/track-id :track/manager} overrides))\n```\n\n## Same structural standards as production code\n\nTests are organized by the same stratified-design and code quality rules:\n\n- **Layered structure**: Layer 0 for helpers and factories, Layer 1 for individual tests, Layer 2 for integration scenarios\n- **No nested conditionals** in test helpers\n- **No magic numbers**: extract constants (`(def ^:private timeout-ms 5000)`)\n- **No raw strings** in test data that represents user-facing copy — use the same message keys\n- **Named test helpers**: a function that sets up a specific scenario should have a clear, descriptive name\n- **DRY**: shared test helpers for a component go in a `test_helpers.clj` or `fixtures.clj` alongside the tests\n\n## Test file layout\n\n```clojure\n(ns ai.miniforge.foo.bar-test\n  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n            [ai.miniforge.foo.bar :as sut]))\n\n;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0\n;; Test fixtures and factories\n\n(def ^:private sample-id \"test-wf-001\")\n\n(defn- make-event [type & {:as extra}]\n  (merge {:event/type type :event/id (random-uuid)} extra))\n\n;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 1\n;; Unit tests\n\n(deftest test-specific-behavior\n  (testing \"given condition → expected result\"\n    (is (= expected (sut/fn-under-test input)))))\n```\n\n## What tests must NOT do\n\n- **No LLM calls**: mock or stub at the interface boundary\n- **No filesystem reads** (except integration tests explicitly marked as such): use `with-open` on temp files or in-memory fixtures\n- **No real network calls**: stub HTTP clients\n- **No sleep**: use deterministic time injection or fast-forward mechanisms\n- **No `println` in test bodies**: use `testing` blocks with descriptive strings\n\n## Naming conventions\n\n- Test ns: `<component-ns>-test` or `<component-ns>.<concern>-test`\n- Test fn: `test-<behavior-under-test>` (not `test-function-name`)\n- `testing` description: `\"given <setup> → <expected outcome>\"` format\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing tests:\n1. Before writing the first `deftest`, ask: what factory functions does this test file need?\n2. Extract them immediately at Layer 0, not after the fact.\n3. Apply the same code review lens to tests as to production code — no magic numbers, no nested conditionals, no raw string literals.\n4. If a test helper is used in more than one test file in the same component, move it to a shared `fixtures.clj`.\n5. Build each fixture in the shape the real producer emits. If you have to reshape an input to make the test pass, fix the code under test instead — never paper over a known-bad path with a convenient fixture.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Inline map construction that repeats more than once\n- `(Thread/sleep 100)` in tests — eliminate or use test-controlled time\n- `(str \"test-\" (random-uuid))` repeated everywhere — extract to a factory\n- Tests named after functions: `test-wf-status` → name for behavior: `test-stale-workflow-detection`\n- 100-line `deftest` functions — split into smaller, focused tests\n- Reshaping a fixture's types or shape away from what the real producer emits, to get a green test instead of fixing the code under test",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/standards,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "400",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (400): Testing standards — factory functions, same code quality as production, no duplication"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Stratified Design — build each layer as a small language on the one below; a one-way DAG of abstraction",
               :agent-behavior
               "Structure a system as a sequence of layers, each a small **language** built on\nthe one below (SICP §2.2.4 — Abelson & Sussman). Every layer has primitives,\nmeans of combination, and means of abstraction; the constructs you define at one\nlayer become the primitives of the next.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Stratified Design — build each layer as a small language on the one below; a one-way DAG of abstraction"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Stratified Design (ALWAYS)\n\nStructure a system as a sequence of layers, each a small **language** built on\nthe one below (SICP §2.2.4 — Abelson & Sussman). Every layer has primitives,\nmeans of combination, and means of abstraction; the constructs you define at one\nlayer become the primitives of the next.\n\n> \"A complex system should be structured as a sequence of levels that are\n> described using a sequence of languages. Each level is constructed by combining\n> parts that are regarded as primitive at that level, and the parts constructed at\n> each level are used as primitives at the next level.\" — SICP\n\n## What it is — and is not\n\n- It **is** a one-way dependency **DAG of abstraction**. Higher layers are\n  expressed in the vocabulary of lower layers. The graph **branches**: one\n  construct combines several lower primitives, and a single primitive is shared\n  by many constructs above it.\n- It is **not** a fixed, universal stack of named layers. Strata are\n  **discovered from the problem** — they are *this* system's vocabularies, not a\n  checklist. The coarse module-level stack (Foundations → Domain → Application →\n  Adapters → Infrastructure) is the **Dependency Rule**; see\n  `foundations/layered-architecture` (011). That is *one instance* of one-way\n  dependencies, not the design discipline itself.\n- It is **not a linear chain.** A line of N named boxes is a degenerate special\n  case; real strata fan out and reconverge.\n\n## Principles\n\n- **Layer = language.** Each layer exposes primitives + means of combination + a\n  way to name new constructs. Build the next layer using only that vocabulary.\n- **One direction, no cycles.** A construct may use any layer at or below it,\n  never above. Reject cycles and upward references.\n- **Closure.** Prefer combinators whose results are the same kind as their\n  inputs, so results recompose (`beside`/`below` take painters and return a\n  painter).\n- **Discover the strata.** Find the vocabulary the problem wants at each level;\n  don't impose a template. Name a layer for what it *means*, not by ordinal alone.\n- **Robustness is the payoff.** A spec change should touch one layer behind its\n  vocabulary. If a small change ripples across layers, the strata are wrong.\n\n## Worked shape — SICP picture language\n\nprimitive painters → geometric combinators (`beside`, `below`, `flip-vert`,\n`rotate`) → schemes of combination (`right-split`, `corner-split`,\n`square-limit`).\n\n`square-limit` rests on `corner-split`, which rests on `right-split` / `up-split`,\nwhich rest on the combinators, which rest on painters — and those lower constructs\nare **shared** by many above them. That sharing is exactly why it is a DAG, not a\nchain.\n\n## When designing / implementing\n\n1. **Name the vocabulary at each level** — what is primitive here, and what\n   combines it?\n2. **Build up, never reach down** — express a layer only in the terms of the\n   layer below; don't skip levels into raw primitives.\n3. **Prove the DAG** — sketch the call/dependency graph; show no upward edges and\n   no cycles. Branching is expected; a single chain usually signals\n   under-abstraction.\n4. **Apply the instances** — per Clojure file, the `Layer N` headings in\n   `languages/clojure` (210); per module dependency direction, the Dependency\n   Rule in `foundations/layered-architecture` (011).\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Calling a **fixed five-name stack** (Foundations/Domain/Application/Adapters/\n  Infrastructure) \"stratified design.\" That is the Dependency Rule (011) — a\n  coarse instance — and it is a chain, not this discipline.\n- **Decorative strata** — `Layer 0/1/2` headings or section banners that don't\n  correspond to real one-way vocabularies; labels the code then crosses (a \"top\"\n  layer inlining work that belongs below).\n- **Skip-level reaches** — a high construct dipping several layers down into\n  primitives instead of going through the intermediate vocabulary.\n- **Cycles, upward calls, god modules** mixing levels in one unit.\n\n## Related\n\n- `foundations/layered-architecture` (011) — the Dependency Rule: one common,\n  coarse, module-level instance of one-way dependencies.\n- `languages/clojure` (210) — the per-file instance (`Layer N` headings,\n  reverse-DAG within a namespace).\n- `foundations/simple-made-easy` (010) — strata keep each level unbraided.\n- `workflows/pr-layering` (722) — the same DAG discipline applied to PRs.",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/stratified-design,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "001",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (001): Stratified Design — build each layer as a small language on the one below; a one-way DAG of abstraction"}
        #:rule{:title "Swift and SwiftUI style and idioms",
               :agent-behavior
               "See also: `foundations/stratified-design` and `foundations/simple-made-easy` — those rules apply in\nSwift without exception. This file covers Swift-specific idioms that sit on top of those foundations.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Swift and SwiftUI style and idioms"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :implement},
                :file-globs ["**/*.swift" "**/Package.swift"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Swift and SwiftUI style guide\n\nSee also: `foundations/stratified-design` and `foundations/simple-made-easy` — those rules apply in\nSwift without exception. This file covers Swift-specific idioms that sit on top of those foundations.\n\n## General\n\n- Prefer value types (`struct`, `enum`) and immutable data flow. Reach for `class` when identity\n  semantics or reference sharing are genuinely required.\n- Use `async`/`await` for network and refresh flows; avoid callback pyramids.\n- Keep `swift build` and `swift test` green.\n- Mark all classes `final` unless you intend subclassing. Only use `open` when subclassing outside\n  the module is intentional.\n\n## Stratified view decomposition\n\nOrganize SwiftUI code into a one-way DAG of layers. Each file belongs to exactly one layer; imports\nflow downward only. (This is the Swift application of `foundations/stratified-design`.)\n\n| Layer | Contents | Example files |\n|-------|----------|---------------|\n| L0 Foundations | Pure display-mapping functions, data tables, formatters, design tokens | `DashboardDisplayMappers.swift`, `DesignSystem.swift` |\n| L1 Domain | Pure domain logic (assessment, classification, risk evaluation) | `HoldingRiskAssessment.swift` |\n| L2 Views | Small, single-purpose views composing L0/L1 | `SummaryCard.swift`, `FamilyViews.swift` |\n| L3 Workspaces | Tab-level containers composing L2 views | `ProfileWorkspace.swift`, `ContentView.swift` |\n\n### Concrete rules\n\n- **No God files**: a single `.swift` file should not contain all views for a tab or feature.\n  Decompose into focused files by domain concept.\n- **Extract computed properties**: break complex view bodies into named computed properties\n  (`portfolioDetailCard`, `sleeveBreakdown`). Each should fit in a single screen.\n- **Pure display mappers**: color selection, badge labels, formatting, and name lookups belong in\n  stateless functions or data tables — not inline in view bodies.\n- **Data tables over switch jungles**: replace `switch sleeve { case \"equities\": ... }` with\n  `Dictionary` lookups. Centralize the mapping. (See simple-made-easy \"rules live in one place\".)\n\n```swift\n// Good: data table\nprivate let sleeveNames: [String: String] = [\n    \"equities\": \"Equities\",\n    \"defensive_equities\": \"Defensive Equities\",\n    \"bonds\": \"Core Bonds\",\n]\nfunc sleeveDisplayName(_ key: String) -> String {\n    sleeveNames[key] ?? key.capitalized\n}\n\n// Bad: scattered switch\nswitch sleeve {\ncase \"equities\": return \"Equities\"\ncase \"defensive_equities\": return \"Defensive Equities\"\n// ...\n}\n```\n\n## Naming\n\nFollow the [Swift API Design Guidelines](https://swift.org/documentation/api-design-guidelines/#naming).\n\n- Include type information in UI component names to disambiguate at usage sites:\n  `settingsViewController: SettingsViewController`, `nameTextField: UITextField`.\n- Names should read as grammatical phrases at call sites. Omit redundant type names that are\n  already clear from context.\n- Use `lowerCamelCase` for properties and functions; `UpperCamelCase` for types and protocols.\n- Prefer American English spelling throughout.\n\n## Access control\n\n- **Default to `private`**. Anything that can be fully private should be. Widen access only when\n  there is a concrete need.\n- Use `private(set)` when external read access is needed but mutation should stay internal.\n- Use `fileprivate` only to share access between types in the same file, not as a substitute for\n  thinking about module boundaries.\n- Never write `internal` explicitly — it is the default and the annotation adds noise.\n- Only use `public` when a symbol must be used outside its defining module.\n\n## Optionals\n\n- Use optionals when the absence of a value has distinct meaning or when initialization is\n  genuinely deferred. Avoid them otherwise.\n- Do not make `Bool`s optional — a tri-state belongs in a named `enum`.\n- Do not make `Array`s optional unless an empty array has different semantics from nil.\n- Always handle unwrapping safely. Prefer `guard let` for early exit when a value is required;\n  prefer `if let` for branching when both paths have substance.\n- Avoid `flatMap` for optional unwrapping; use conditional binding instead.\n- Force-unwrap (`!`) is only acceptable when nil is a programmer error (e.g., storyboard outlets\n  that must exist). Add a comment explaining the invariant.\n\n```swift\n// Good: guard for required value, early exit\nguard let user = session.currentUser else {\n    return showLoginScreen()\n}\n\n// Bad: nested if-let pyramid\nif let session = session {\n    if let user = session.currentUser {\n        // ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Control flow\n\n- Use `guard` for early returns when a condition must hold for the function to proceed. This keeps\n  the happy path unindented.\n- Prefer `guard let` / `guard let ... else` over nested `if let` chains.\n- Keep `switch` exhaustive; avoid `default:` clauses when the enum is under your control —\n  exhaustive switches catch future cases at compile time.\n\n## Protocols and extensions\n\n- Declare protocol conformance in a separate `extension` in the same file as the type:\n\n```swift\n// Good\nclass UserViewModel { ... }\n\nextension UserViewModel: Equatable {\n    static func == (lhs: UserViewModel, rhs: UserViewModel) -> Bool { ... }\n}\n\n// Bad — mixed in with the type body\nclass UserViewModel: Equatable {\n    static func == (...) { ... }\n    // ... other members ...\n}\n```\n\n- Use `MARK: - ProtocolName` inside conformance extensions for navigability.\n- Use protocols to wrap dependencies and enable substitution in tests.\n- Avoid `Any` and `AnyObject` — they discard type information and push errors to runtime. Prefer\n  a protocol with the required interface instead.\n- Only declare a protocol `open` when subclassing outside the module is intentional.\n\n## Computed properties vs methods\n\nUse a computed property when:\n- The computation is O(1) with no side effects.\n- The result depends only on instance state.\n- There is no meaningful failure mode.\n\nUse a method when:\n- The computation is expensive or has side effects.\n- The operation involves I/O, mutation of external state, or can fail.\n- The semantics are more \"do something\" than \"what is this thing\".\n\n## Closures\n\n- Use trailing closure syntax when the closure is the last argument and its purpose is clear from\n  context.\n- Always include explicit capture lists (`[weak self]`, `[unowned self]`) when capturing `self` in\n  a closure stored by the captured object, to prevent retain cycles.\n- Prefer `[weak self]` over `[unowned self]` unless you have a strong proof of lifetime.\n\n```swift\n// Good: trailing syntax with explicit capture list\nnetworkClient.fetch(request) { [weak self] result in\n    guard let self else { return }\n    self.handleResult(result)\n}\n```\n\n## File and type organization\n\n### One major type per file\n\nA file should contain one major type declaration. Supporting types (small enums, helper structs,\nassociated protocols) may accompany the primary type in the same file when they are not reused\nelsewhere.\n\n### Property and function ordering within a type\n\n```\n1. Property wrappers (@StateObject, @State, @AppStorage, @Binding, @ObservedObject)\n   grouped by wrapper kind, marked private\n2. Other stored properties — public/internal, then private\n3. Initializers\n4. deinit (if present, after last initializer)\n5. Overridden declarations (MARK: - SuperclassName)\n6. Protocol conformance (MARK: - ProtocolName)\n7. Type-specific declarations (MARK: - TypeName)\n   — public/internal, then private\n8. Extension conformances (each in a separate extension block)\n```\n\nUse `MARK: -` to separate the above groups when a file has overrides or conformances. Use `MARK:`\n(no dash) for secondary groupings inside a section.\n\n### Extension file naming\n\nGeneral-purpose extensions on external types live in their own files named\n`ExtendedType+Purpose.swift` (e.g., `Array+MergeSort.swift`).\n\n### SwiftUI file order\n\nWithin a SwiftUI `View`:\n1. Property wrappers grouped by type\n2. Other stored properties\n3. `init` (when explicitly written)\n4. `// MARK: - View` then `var body: some View`\n5. Computed property helpers\n\n## State management\n\n- Keep view models thin and explicit. Views receive data; they do not compute policy.\n- If a view needs to decide \"is this risky?\" or \"which regime applies?\", that logic belongs in L1\n  domain or in a lower-level core (e.g., Rust core over FFI).\n- Do not duplicate engine logic in Swift when another layer owns the policy.\n- Keep UI state (`@State`, `@StateObject`) scoped as locally as possible; lift state only when\n  multiple views genuinely share it.\n\n## Localization\n\nEvery user-facing string must go through a `Strings.*` namespace (backed by `NSLocalizedString` +\n`Localizable.strings`). No hardcoded English strings in views or view models. This is the Swift\napplication of simple-made-easy \"rules (and data) live in one place\".\n\n## JSON scalar extraction (Bool vs NSNumber)\n\n`JSONSerialization` bridges every JSON number AND every JSON boolean to `NSNumber` — a\nJSON `true` becomes an `__NSCFBoolean`, which *is* an `NSNumber`. Two casts are therefore\nambiguous on any value decoded from JSON (FFI payloads, `[String: Any]` dictionaries):\n\n- `value as? NSNumber` **succeeds for booleans** — `true` pulled out as a number is `1`.\n- `value as? Bool` **succeeds for `0`/`1`** — the integer `1` pulled out as a bool is `true`.\n\nRules:\n\n1. **Known-boolean key** (a field that is always a JSON bool, e.g. `attention/resolved?`):\n   `as? Bool` is correct and safe. The ambiguity only bites when the slot could also hold a\n   number.\n2. **Extracting an integer**: a bare `as? NSNumber` is wrong — a boolean slips through.\n   Reject booleans by exact CoreFoundation type and require integrality:\n\n   ```swift\n   func jsonInt(_ value: Any) -> Int? {\n       guard let n = value as? NSNumber,\n             CFGetTypeID(n) != CFBooleanGetTypeID(),   // reject __NSCFBoolean\n             n == NSNumber(value: n.int64Value)         // reject non-integers (1.5 → nil)\n       else { return nil }\n       return Int(exactly: n.int64Value)               // nil if it doesn't fit in Int\n   }\n   ```\n\n3. **Generic value renderer** (type unknown): test boolean BEFORE number, again via\n   `CFGetTypeID(x) == CFBooleanGetTypeID()`, so booleans render as `true`/`false`, not `1`/`0`.\n\nOn an **unknown** JSON scalar, neither `as? NSNumber` nor `as? Bool` is a reliable type test —\nboth are ambiguous (`true` casts to `NSNumber`; `0`/`1` cast to `Bool`), so reordering them does\nnot help. Discriminate the boolean first with `CFGetTypeID(x) == CFBooleanGetTypeID()`, then\ntreat the rest as a number. `as? Bool` is only safe on a key that is *always* a JSON boolean.\n\nThis class recurred across miniforge-control PR #132 (a value renderer printed booleans as\n`1`/`0`) and PR #134 (a PR-number parser accepted `[\"repo\", true]` as `repo#1`) — both caught\nby Copilot, not self-review. Treat every `as? NSNumber` / `as? Bool` on a decoded payload as\nsuspect, and test the bool-and-float cases, not just structurally-malformed input.\n\n## Documentation and comments\n\n- Document all `internal` and higher access-level declarations with `///` doc comments.\n- `private` declarations may be documented for clarity but it is not required.\n- Document `enum` cases with a single line each. Document associated values as parameters.\n- Write comments to explain *why*, not *what*. Code that needs a comment to explain what it does\n  should usually be refactored into a named function or property.\n- Start comments with `//` followed by a space. Write complete sentences.\n- Do not leave `// TODO:` without a corresponding issue reference.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Use dependency injection (protocol types) to make view models and controllers testable in\n  isolation.\n- Test error and edge-case paths, not only happy paths.\n- Prefer small, focused unit tests for domain logic (L1 layer).\n- Integration tests cover the wiring between layers.\n\n## What NOT to do\n\n- Do not scatter policy conditionals across view files. Pull them into L0/L1 tables.\n- Do not use `Any` or `AnyObject` — use typed protocols.\n- Do not pull an integer from decoded JSON with a bare `as? NSNumber`, and do not use `as? Bool`\n  or `as? NSNumber` as a type test on an unknown JSON scalar — both are ambiguous\n  (`__NSCFBoolean` bridges to `NSNumber`; `0`/`1` cast to `Bool`). Discriminate with\n  `CFBooleanGetTypeID` first (see JSON scalar extraction).\n- Do not force-unwrap optionals in production code without documenting the invariant.\n- Do not put business logic into view bodies or computed view properties.\n- Do not mix UI state and network/storage concerns in the same type (see stratified-design).\n- Do not use `default:` in `switch` statements over enums you control.\n- Do not introduce retain cycles by capturing `self` strongly in stored closures.\n- Do not hardcode user-facing strings; route all copy through `Strings.*`.",
               :id :std/swift,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "240",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (240): Swift and SwiftUI style and idioms"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Code changes ship with test changes — a PR that touches production code without touching tests is a smell worth re-examining",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. After every implement step, before declaring done — re-read the diff. Did production code change? Did test code change to match? If not, return to the implement step.\n2. When a CI / lint / format step changes code automatically and a test wasn't touched — verify the formatter didn't change behavior. If it did, add tests.\n3. Reviewer agents must surface \"no test changes\" as a blocking issue unless the PR description claims pure restructuring.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :warn,
                :message
                "Standard: Code changes ship with test changes — a PR that touches production code without touching tests is a smell worth re-examining"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Tests Ship With Code (ALWAYS)\n\n**A PR that modifies production code without modifying tests is the exception, not the rule.** When reviewing or authoring a change, treat the absence of test changes as a question that must be answered, not as a default.\n\n## The rule\n\nEvery code change falls into one of three categories. Each has a different test expectation; pick deliberately, document the choice.\n\n| Category | Expected test change |\n|---|---|\n| **New behavior** — a new function, branch, decision path, side effect | New tests pinning the new behavior. No exception. |\n| **Changed behavior** — a fix, optimization, refactor that changes observable output | Tests updated to pin the new behavior; the old assertion must change or be replaced. |\n| **Pure restructuring** — rename, extract-fn, move-ns, reformat, comment | Tests likely unchanged. State this in the PR body. |\n\nThe third category is the only one where \"no test changes\" is a valid PR. If the PR description doesn't explicitly claim it's pure restructuring, the absence of test changes is a defect.\n\n## Why this rule exists\n\nWhen agents (or humans) write code without writing tests:\n\n- The change is **unverified** — the agent claims it works, but no executable proof exists.\n- The next change risks **silently breaking the behavior** — there's no test to fail.\n- The reviewer has no **independent specification** of what the code is supposed to do.\n- The bug-fix PR has no **regression pin** — the same bug can return tomorrow.\n\nA test is a second statement of intent. Two statements that have to agree catches a class of misunderstanding that one statement cannot.\n\n## Diagnostic questions\n\nWhen you author or review a PR with `+N -M` in source and `+0 -0` in tests:\n\n1. **Did you add a new function or branch?** Then a new test must pin it. No exception.\n2. **Did you fix a bug?** A regression test must reproduce the bug on `main` and pass on this branch. The bug report is incomplete without it.\n3. **Did you change observable behavior?** The existing test for that behavior must change. If no test exists, the absence of one is itself a finding — open a follow-up to add coverage *now*, not later.\n4. **Is this truly pure restructuring?** State it in the PR body: \"No test changes — rename only / formatting only / comment only.\" If the PR author can't make that claim with conviction, the change isn't pure restructuring.\n\nIf none of the answers explain the missing test change, the PR is incomplete.\n\n## What \"test change\" means\n\nNot just new `deftest` forms. Counts as a test change:\n\n- New assertions in an existing test\n- Test fixtures updated to cover the new shape\n- Property-based test generators extended for the new domain\n- Snapshot / golden-file regeneration\n- Removed obsolete tests when the behavior they pinned is genuinely gone\n- Test scaffolding (mocks, stubs, helpers) extended for new code paths\n\nDoes NOT count:\n\n- Whitespace / formatting in test files\n- Renames following a production-code rename (mechanical, not specification)\n- Disabling a test (`^:disabled`, `(comment ...)`) — see [no-dead-code](../foundations/no-dead-code.mdc); this is dead-code production, not test work\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing or reviewing code:\n1. After every implement step, before declaring done — re-read the diff. Did production code change? Did test code change to match? If not, return to the implement step.\n2. When a CI / lint / format step changes code automatically and a test wasn't touched — verify the formatter didn't change behavior. If it did, add tests.\n3. Reviewer agents must surface \"no test changes\" as a blocking issue unless the PR description claims pure restructuring.\n4. When the bug-fix prompt arrives (\"fix X\"), the deliverable is two artifacts: the fix AND the regression test. One without the other is not done.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- PR that adds a feature with \"tests in a follow-up\" as the justification — there is no follow-up\n- PR that fixes a bug without a regression test reproducing the bug on `main`\n- PR that touches business logic and only updates whitespace in test files\n- \"I tested it locally\" as a substitute for executable test coverage\n- A growing list of `(comment ...)` blocks \"for now\" in test files\n- New `defmethod` / `defmulti` cases without a test exercising the new dispatch\n- Schema changes without a test exercising the new shape (and rejection of the old one)",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/tests-with-code,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "716",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (716): Code changes ship with test changes — a PR that touches production code without touching tests is a smell worth re-examining"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Validation at boundaries — schemas and validation only at interface/external entry points",
               :agent-behavior
               "When writing code inside a component:\n1. Do NOT add `(when (nil? x) ...)` or schema checks on internal data.\n2. If you feel the urge to validate internal data, ask: is this arriving from outside the component? If no, delete the check.\n3. If arriving from outside (via `interface.clj`), add the validation there — and remove it from the internals.\n4. The schema lives in `schema.clj` or `spec.clj` — not scattered across `core.clj` with `when-let` guards.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :hard-halt,
                :message
                "Schema or input validation in the interior. Validate only at interface boundaries and external entry points; trust data inward by construction."},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement}},
               :severity :high,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Validation at Boundaries (ALWAYS)\n\n**Validate once, at the edge. Trust everything inside.**\n\n## The rule\n\nSchemas and input validation belong at **two places only**:\n\n1. **Interface boundaries** — the `interface.clj` of a Polylith component. Data entering a component is validated here. Inside the component, trust the data.\n2. **External entry points** — HTTP handlers, CLI argument parsing, event ingestion, file reads. Anything arriving from outside the JVM process.\n\nEverywhere else — inside components, between internal namespaces — **no validation**. Data flowing through internal calls is already valid by construction.\n\n## Why\n\nValidation is expensive to read and to run. Redundant internal validation:\n- Adds noise that obscures business logic\n- Creates false confidence (\"everything is validated\") while actually missing real boundaries\n- Leads to defensive programming anti-patterns (`when (some? x) ...` everywhere)\n- Diverges: schemas at the top and bottom of a call chain often disagree silently\n\nIf internal data is invalid, the bug is in the caller — not in the callee's missing check. Fix the caller. Don't hide it with a check.\n\n## Interface boundary pattern\n\n```clojure\n;; components/task/src/ai/miniforge/task/interface.clj\n;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0\n;; Public API — validate here, then forward\n\n(defn create-task\n  \"Create a new task. Returns an anomaly map when input is invalid;\n   callers check with anomaly/anomaly?. Does not throw — per rule 005,\n   throws are reserved for absolute process boundaries.\"\n  [task-data]\n  (if-let [errors (m/explain TaskSchema task-data)]\n    (anomaly/anomaly :invalid-input\n                     \"Invalid task-data\"\n                     {:data task-data :errors (malli.error/humanize errors)})\n    (core/create-task-impl task-data)))  ; inside core: no re-validation\n```\n\n```clojure\n;; components/task/src/ai/miniforge/task/core.clj\n;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0\n;; Internal implementation — no validation\n\n(defn create-task-impl\n  \"Create a task record. Input is pre-validated by interface.\"\n  [task-data]\n  (-> task-data\n      (assoc :task/id (random-uuid))\n      (assoc :task/created-at (Instant/now))))\n```\n\n## External boundary pattern\n\n```clojure\n;; bases/api/src/ai/miniforge/api/handlers.clj\n;------------------------------------------------------------------------------ Layer 0\n;; HTTP boundary — validate incoming JSON here\n\n(defn handle-create-task [req]\n  (let [body (parse-body req)]\n    (if-let [errors (validate-create-task-request body)]\n      (bad-request errors)\n      (task/create-task body))))  ; component interface takes it from here\n```\n\n## What does NOT need validation\n\n- Function calls between files within the same component\n- Function calls from `core.clj` to other internal namespaces\n- Data threading through a pipeline (`->`, `->>`, `as->`)\n- Return values of pure functions (they can't be invalid by construction)\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen writing code inside a component:\n1. Do NOT add `(when (nil? x) ...)` or schema checks on internal data.\n2. If you feel the urge to validate internal data, ask: is this arriving from outside the component? If no, delete the check.\n3. If arriving from outside (via `interface.clj`), add the validation there — and remove it from the internals.\n4. The schema lives in `schema.clj` or `spec.clj` — not scattered across `core.clj` with `when-let` guards.\n\n## Anti-patterns to reject\n\n- Validating in both `interface.clj` and `core.clj` — pick one (interface)\n- `(when (some? x) (do-thing x))` inside pure functions — caller is responsible\n- Schema validation inside pipeline stages (`->`) — validate before the pipeline\n- HTTP handler calling `valid?` AND the component interface calling `valid?` again\n- Defensive `(get m :key \"default\")` sprinkled through internals — that's validation in disguise",
               :always-inject? true,
               :id :std/validation-boundaries,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "004",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (004): Validation at boundaries — schemas and validation only at interface/external entry points"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Web architecture mode — Fulcro for stateful product UI, server HTML for simple pages, no new browser frameworks without ADR",
               :agent-behavior
               "This rule decides which technology a web surface SHOULD be built in.\nMiniforge products with a browser surface default to **Fulcro for\nstateful product UI**, **server-rendered HTML for simple pages**, and\n**no new browser frameworks without an ADR**.",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Web architecture mode — Fulcro for stateful product UI, server HTML for simple pages, no new browser frameworks without ADR"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:review :plan :implement},
                :file-globs
                ["**/*.html"
                 "**/*.css"
                 "**/*.js"
                 "**/*.cljs"
                 "**/*.cljc"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Web architecture mode\n\nThis rule decides which technology a web surface SHOULD be built in.\nMiniforge products with a browser surface default to **Fulcro for\nstateful product UI**, **server-rendered HTML for simple pages**, and\n**no new browser frameworks without an ADR**.\n\nThe Fulcro details live in `frameworks/fulcro.mdc`. The JavaScript /\nCSS / HTML / browser-security details live in their own files. This\nrule is the *router* that says which set of standards applies.\n\n## Surface taxonomy\n\n| Surface | Standard |\n|---|---|\n| Product control plane | Fulcro |\n| Workflow / evidence / policy screens | Fulcro |\n| Complex admin screens | Fulcro |\n| Simple docs / status / landing pages | Server-rendered HTML/CSS |\n| One-off dashboard utilities | Server-rendered or small JS |\n| Legacy pages | Leave until materially touched |\n\n## WEB-001 — Prefer simple server rendering for simple pages\n\nSimple informational pages MUST use server-rendered HTML unless they\nrequire durable client-side state. Examples: status pages, docs pages,\nsimple reports, static configuration views.\n\nAdding Fulcro (or any other client framework) to a page that has no\ndurable client state is over-architecture; reject in review.\n\n## WEB-002 — Prefer Fulcro for stateful product pages\n\nA page SHOULD be built in Fulcro when it contains *any* of:\n\n- multi-entity editing\n- workflow state\n- async job state\n- policy or rule editing\n- graph-like navigation\n- approval / rejection flows\n- inspectable evidence state\n\nIf the page lacks all of the above today but is *expected* to gain them\nwithin the current product roadmap, start in Fulcro now to avoid a\nmid-feature rewrite.\n\n## WEB-003 — No new frontend framework without ADR\n\nRepositories MUST NOT introduce a new browser framework without an\nexplicit architecture decision record under `docs/adr/` (or the project's\nADR convention). The ADR MUST address: bus factor, agent legibility,\nhiring pipeline, server-contract impact, and migration cost.\n\nCurrently allowed modes:\n\n- server-rendered HTML + CSS (with optional small vanilla JS per\n  `languages/javascript.mdc`)\n- Fulcro (per `frameworks/fulcro.mdc`)\n- Fulcro RAD (per `frameworks/fulcro-rad.mdc`)\n- explicitly grandfathered legacy code (named in the ADR)\n\nReact, Vue, Svelte, Angular, Solid, htmx, Alpine, Stimulus, Lit, etc.\nare NOT pre-approved. Each requires its own ADR.\n\n## WEB-004 — Keep server transport separate from UI state\n\nhttp-kit handlers, Ring middleware, API routes, and any server-side\nHTTP plumbing MUST NOT contain browser workflow state-machine logic.\n\nServer transport MAY:\n- serve HTML and static assets\n- expose API endpoints\n- handle session / authentication\n- provide WebSocket / SSE transport\n\nServer transport MUST NOT:\n- encode client workflow state in handlers\n- generate large inline JavaScript blobs\n- duplicate frontend state transitions as hidden server control flow\n\nThe boundary between transport and UI state is enforced by `frameworks/api-surface-classes.mdc`\n(internal vs first-party vs customer surfaces).\n\n## Migration rule\n\nDo NOT rewrite all existing web dashboards immediately. Convert\nfeature-by-feature when *any* of these is true:\n\n- the page gains durable client-side state\n- the page edits multiple related entities\n- the page needs optimistic updates\n- the page needs retries / repair workflows\n- the page needs route-driven workflow state\n- the page has recurring bugs from ad-hoc JavaScript\n- the page participates in product governance workflows (approvals,\n  audit, policy enforcement)\n\nWhen converting, retain the existing JSON / REST endpoints (per\n`frameworks/api-surface-classes.mdc`) — Fulcro consumes them via Pathom\ninternally; cross-language clients keep their flat surface.\n\n## Frontend review checklist\n\nEvery frontend PR SHOULD answer:\n\n1. Is this page simple, transitional, or product-stateful?\n2. Should this be Fulcro? (per WEB-001 / WEB-002)\n3. Are component queries explicit and local? (FUL-002, FUL-017)\n4. Are entity idents stable? (FUL-004)\n5. Are mutations domain-intent named? (FUL-008)\n6. Are async loading / error states visible? (JS-006, FUL-013)\n7. Are destructive operations guarded? (JS-007, FUL-023, BSEC-004)\n8. Are permissions enforced server-side? (BSEC-001)\n9. Are design tokens used? (CSS-001, CSS-002)\n10. Are focus states visible? (CSS-007, CSS-008)\n11. Is any JS interop isolated? (FUL-026)\n12. Are durable facts stored once in normalised state? (FUL-020)\n13. Are tests covering critical mutation / load behaviour? (FUL-032)\n\n## Agent behaviour\n\nWhen asked to add or modify a web surface:\n1. Classify the surface against WEB-001 / WEB-002 *before* writing code.\n   Output the classification in the PR description.\n2. If the surface qualifies for Fulcro, switch to `frameworks/fulcro.mdc`\n   for the implementation rules.\n3. If introducing anything that isn't on the WEB-003 allow-list, stop\n   and produce an ADR draft for review before writing code.\n4. Run the frontend review checklist before requesting review.",
               :id :std/web-architecture-mode,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "330",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (330): Web architecture mode — Fulcro for stateful product UI, server HTML for simple pages, no new browser frameworks without ADR"}
        #:rule{:title
               "Author work specs with required priority, theme, normative-refs, and testable acceptance criteria",
               :agent-behavior
               "1. Check `work/themes.edn` first — pick the existing theme that fits,\n2. Set `:spec/priority :tier` honestly — do not inflate tier to jump\n3. Write acceptance criteria as testable assertions — one per line",
               :enforcement
               {:action :audit,
                :message
                "Standard: Author work specs with required priority, theme, normative-refs, and testable acceptance criteria"},
               :applies-to
               {:phases #{:release :review :verify :plan :implement},
                :file-globs ["work/**/*.spec.edn"]},
               :severity :low,
               :knowledge-content
               "# Work-Spec Authoring\n\n## Context\n\nWork specs (`work/*.spec.edn`) are the unit of autonomous implementation\nfor miniforge: a well-formed work spec is sufficient for a miniforge\nworkflow (or a human-driven agent) to plan, implement, verify, review,\nand release a coherent change. A poorly-formed work spec silently\nwastes tokens and agent cycles.\n\nThis rule applies whenever an agent (or a human) creates or edits a\nfile matching `work/*.spec.edn`.\n\n**Parent authority:** `foundations/specification-standards` (Dewey 020)\ngoverns the normative specs this rule's work specs implement against.\nThis rule is scoped specifically to the work-spec authoring format.\n\n## Required fields\n\n<required>\nEvery `work/*.spec.edn` file MUST contain the following top-level keys:\n\n- `:spec/title` — short imperative string\n- `:spec/description` — full prose description of what + why + how\n- `:spec/intent` — `{:type <keyword> :scope [<path-glob> ...]}`\n  - `:type` MUST be one of `:refactor :feature :bugfix :general :chore\n    :docs :test :performance`\n  - `:scope` MUST be a vector of file paths or glob patterns, NOT a\n    prose description\n- `:spec/constraints` — vector of short constraint strings\n- `:spec/acceptance-criteria` — vector of testable assertions\n- `:spec/priority` — see below\n- `:spec/workflow-type` — a keyword identifying the workflow chain\n  (e.g., `:canonical-sdlc`)\n</required>\n\n## Required `:spec/priority` block\n\n<required>\nEvery spec MUST carry:\n\n```clojure\n:spec/priority\n{:tier       :blocker | :high | :medium | :low\n :axes       #{:dogfood-enabler :token-conservation :observation\n               :correctness :scale :ux ...}   ; at least one\n :theme      <theme-id-keyword>                ; MUST exist in work/themes.edn\n :rationale  \"One sentence — why this tier\"\n :blocks     [<filename-string> ...]           ; specs this unblocks\n :blocked-by [<filename-string> ...]}          ; specs that must land first\n```\n\nTier semantics:\n\n| Tier | Use when |\n|---|---|\n| `:blocker` | Nothing above it moves until this lands. Costs real tokens or blocks release. Use sparingly. |\n| `:high` | Next-in-line when blockers clear; compounds blocker value. |\n| `:medium` | Default. Useful but not critical-path. |\n| `:low` | Discretionary. |\n\nAxis semantics:\n\n| Axis | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `:dogfood-enabler` | Makes miniforge more capable of running miniforge |\n| `:token-conservation` | cuts cost per successful run |\n| `:observation` | Improves what humans + automation can see |\n| `:correctness` | Closes a correctness gap (bug, silent failure) |\n| `:scale` | enables a larger deployment or use case |\n| `:ux` | Improves human-facing experience |\n\nPick all that apply — most specs are multi-axis.\n</required>\n\n## Required theme membership\n\n<required>\n`:spec/priority :theme` MUST be a key in `work/themes.edn`. A spec\nreferencing an unknown theme is rejected at review time.\n\nThemes carry the authoritative `:theme/informative-spec` pointer + the\n`:theme/normative-refs`. A spec inherits those anchors from its theme,\nbut MAY add spec-specific `:normative-refs` as well (see below).\n</required>\n\n## Normative anchor\n\n<required>\nEvery work spec MUST have a normative anchor. Satisfied when EITHER:\n\n- The theme has one or more `:theme/normative-refs`, OR\n- The spec declares its own `:normative-refs` (map with\n  `:primary` + optional `:secondary`, or a flat vector of strings —\n  matches the existing `rn-*` pattern)\n\nSpecs with no normative anchor are rejected. This enforces the N-series\nprinciple that implementation conforms to strategy, not the other way.\n</required>\n\n## Testable acceptance criteria\n\n<required>\nEach entry in `:spec/acceptance-criteria` MUST be a single assertion a\ntest or a human can check. Prefer criteria that become test names.\n\nGood examples:\n\n- `\"poly check reports 0 errors on main\"`\n- `\":workflow/dag-considered event emitted on every plan phase\"`\n- `\"A killed run resumes with no re-run of successful phases\"`\n\nBad examples (rejected at review):\n\n- `\"Implementation complete\"` (not testable)\n- `\"System works\"` (what does works mean?)\n- `\"Improves performance\"` (by how much?)\n</required>\n\n## Invalid examples\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\nNo priority block:\n\n```clojure\n{:spec/title \"Fix thing\"\n :spec/description \"...\"\n :spec/intent {:type :bugfix :scope [\"...\"]}\n :spec/acceptance-criteria [\"Works better\"]\n :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}\n```\n\nIssues: no `:spec/priority`, untestable acceptance criterion, no\nnormative anchor, no theme.\n</example>\n\n<example type=\"invalid\">\nProse scope:\n\n```clojure\n:spec/intent {:type :feature\n              :scope \"Refactor the agent module and related areas\"}\n```\n\n`:scope` MUST be a vector of file paths or globs — otherwise tooling\ncannot compute affected bricks, change-classifiers cannot filter, and\nthe plan phase cannot decompose the change.\n</example>\n\n## Good example\n\nSee [templates/work-spec.edn.template](../templates/work-spec.edn.template) — a complete, valid spec showing all required fields.\n\n> **Normative-refs inheritance:** A spec inherits `:theme/normative-refs` from its theme automatically. Only add a spec-level `:normative-refs` when the spec cites a section the theme doesn't already cover.\n\n## Agent behavior\n\nWhen you create or modify a `work/*.spec.edn`:\n\n1. Check `work/themes.edn` first — pick the existing theme that fits,\n   or propose a new theme entry (with `:theme/informative-spec`) in the\n   same PR\n2. Set `:spec/priority :tier` honestly — do not inflate tier to jump\n   the queue; the `bb work:queue` renderer reveals this to reviewers\n3. Write acceptance criteria as testable assertions — one per line\n4. Set `:scope` as file paths/globs, never prose\n5. After editing, run `bb work:queue` to regenerate `work/QUEUE.md`\n   and confirm your spec appears in the expected theme at the\n   expected tier\n\nWhen reviewing a work spec:\n\n- If any required field is missing, reject\n- If the theme doesn't exist, reject (or propose its addition)\n- If acceptance criteria are untestable, reject\n- If normative anchor is missing, reject",
               :id :std/work-spec-authoring,
               :detection {:type :custom},
               :category "021",
               :description
               "Engineering standard (021): Author work specs with required priority, theme, normative-refs, and testable acceptance criteria"}],
